      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1896-03-28_WidnesExaminer_art0040" n="art0040">
        <p>MADE-UP DISHES</p>
        <p>hich are a most important in the week's menu of household, are made piquant and tasty )y the addition of</p>
        <p>OVRIL,</p>
        <p>ich is the guaranteed product</p>
        <p>of ,IME OX</p>
        <p>BEEF</p>
        <p>xl which imparts the all flavour of Fresh Meat to even the most tasteles dish.</p>
        <p>PLEASANT PARAGRAPHS.</p>
        <p>A MIDSUMMER Dream of the Millennium—To be able to sit in a draught without catching cold. " RICHES have wings ; " but, unfortunately, they seldom use them except to travel in the wrong direction. _ _ .</p>
        <p>_ DIPLOMACY consists first in presenting your ultimatum, and then seeing how much of it you can get. THE man who never argues with "children, women, or fools" is not likely to have many argu- ments.</p>
        <p>HAPPY is the woman who cannot see how her daughters are going to get as good husbands as she did.</p>
        <p>A GREAT many people believe in being just before they are generous, and it takes them so long to be just that they never get time to be generous. CHARLEY GOODLY: "I owe everything to my mother." Bobby Lender: "Please don't overlook the ten you owe me."</p>
        <p>SHE : Tell me, when you were in the army, were you cool in the hour of danger ?" He " Cool ! I actually shivered !" ERRING.—StUdent (translating) : " And—er— then—er—er —er—went—er —and er." Profes- sor: "Don't laugh, gentlemen—to err is human."</p>
        <p>_ OLD ENGLISH.—First She: " How ridiculous men are ! Here he has written me a letter com- paring my eyes to stars, my teeth to pearls, and swearing that the sun retires abashed from the brightness of my presence." Second She : " I don't call that foolish. I think it pretty smart of him—giving you the kind of love talk that was in vogue when you were a girl." Mu. SAYLES : "Wiry, my dear—why are you crying?" Mrs. Sayles (with sobs) : "Oh, John, don't you remember my new crepon skirt I got at such a bargain last week for fifteen shillings?" Mr. Sayles : " Yes; what then? " Mrs. Say-les (weeping afresh) : "I p-p-passed Lacey's t-t-to- clay, and they were all m-m-marked down to fourteen shillings and ninepenee. 800-hoo !" THEORY AND PRACTICE.—Mrs. Dorcas: "Our league of emancipated women is going to have a lecture on the social equality of the sex. You must be sure to come." Mrs. Cobwigger : " Certainly, my dear. I shall bring Miss Smith with me, as .she believes in social equality." Mrs. Dorcas: "Not for the world. You know she isn't in our set." SITE: "Before we were married you said it would give you the greatest pleasure to gratify my every wish." Ire: "My dear, I said your lightest wish; and I'll do it, too." Two VICES.—" Smoking is a bad thing," said Wagg. "Look at Vesuvius—smokirrg. all the time, and a constant source of danger." "True,' said Jocose; "and allied to its smoking, Vesuvius is always indulgin,, in what the Irishman called wee bit of th' crater.'"</p>
        <p>YOUNGLOVE "May, dear, the bank cashier in- forms me that you have overdrawn your account?" Mrs. Younglove? "He does! The villain! George, see here! There, now—only five cheques used, and my book nearly full! The miserable, dishonest scoundrel !"</p>
        <p>"You have brought new sunshine into my life," he said, rapturously. "Do you mean that?' she asked, timidly. "Of course I mean it. Can you doubt me ? ' "Oh, of course, I know you wouldn't intentionally misrepresent. But you know a young man so often thinks a girl has brought sunshine into his life when in reality it's only moonshine." WITH EFFECT.—A temperance orator was hold- ing forth at the corner of a street in South London. 44 My friends," he cried, "let me warn you. As you value your lives, your wives and little ones, and your homes, do not drink such vile stuff as beer, or brandy, or whiskey. I say, no; but —" Here he paused for effect. The audience burst into loud laughter—nor was the reason far to seek. The speaker had raised his arm in exhorta- tion, and his hand was now pointing to a placard on an adjacent hoarding. This placard bore the words—" Drink only —'s Gin." It was a good advertisement for the gin, but it was not what the orator meant.</p>
        <p>FAIR EXCHANGE.—The Japanese relate a story to their children of a certain nobleman, who, having eaten up his fortune, took rooms next door to a famous restaurant. in order that he might enjoy the odours of the cooking viands in the which he could no longer indulge himself. The proprietor, hearing of this, after some time presented a bill, wherein he chart ed two yen fifty sen for the vapours of food inhaled by the poor nobleman during the month. Whereupon the ex-gourmet, calling for his old cash-box which now held but a few copper coins, shook it violently in the ears of the proprietor and replied—" As you charge me for the fumes of your food, I pay you with the sound of my m oney." WANTED THEM Bow - LEGGED. During last autumn a pitman was asked by a friend, who was very bow-legged, to purchase, when next " i' the toon," a pair of stockings for him. On the follow. ing pay Saturday the pitman entered the shop of a well-known hosier to make the purchase. The shopman was most obliging, but having shown the intending purchaser nearly every pair in stock, he at last thought it time to ask of the man a , more minute. description of what was required. Ire said he had shown nearly all they had, and he was sure their shop was second to none, and as they had hitherto given satisfaction to all classes, it was strange that they could not now suit a: cus- tomer. The pitman laconically replied—" What I want is a pair o' bow-legged yins." IN THE DAYS OF HIS YOUTH. —On the anni- versary of American Independence in 1873 Lord Rosebery dfned at Lord Houghton's, among the _guests being the American Minister and Mr. J. L. Motley, the historian. Lord. Rosebery was always youthful-looking, and, 20 years ago, seemed a mere boy. In the course of conversation, Lord Houghton made some remarks on the occasion which had brought them together. Motley, who at opposite Lord Rosebery, looking at him said— " Perhaps our young friend does not understand -what we are talking about ? " He got for reply this—" Oh, don't I! It's all about the bird of freedom and that sort of bunkum." What Mr. Motley thought of the precociousness and out- spokenness of the youth (he looked 16, but was was really 26) is not recorded. A BOY'S EFFORT TO EXPLAIN THINGS.— " Mamma, do you like to kiss Mrs. Jewhillaker ?" No, dear." "Do you think Mrs. Jewhillaker likes to kiss you?" "I don't think she does." "Then why do you and she always kiss when you meet?" "I don't know, dear." think Mrs. Jewhillaker would ra"ther"D you n' tdidyi% kiss her?" have no d ouel of idi k".V:ovuoludn von?" you rather Mrs. Jewhillak 441)1, vary much t" "Then that MusD be w.</p>
