      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1896-03-28_WidnesExaminer_art0041" n="art0041">
        <head>"CAPITAL" CHAI.</head>
        <p>"CAPITAL" CHAI.</p>
        <p>"Torn" Hughes will be missed in the co-opera- tive movement which was dear to his heart, and his removal leaves the ranks of the old guard who entered the movement as Christian Socialists sadly thin. Like Mr. Neale, he was an idealist to the marrow, and though he and the practical men of the movement sometimes came to loggerheads, his criticisms were received in good part, and what he said was listened to with respect. Mr. Hughes had intended to be present at the annual con- gress of the co-operators which is to be held at Woolwich at Whitsuntide. He was at the Hud- dersfield congress last year, genial as ever, full of life, and bubbling over with kindliness, but he was ageing fast, and to his friends the end can hardly have come as a surprise.</p>
        <p>So there is to be no Chartered Company for Ashanti. We are delighted to hear it; we are over- done with Chartered folk already. But that an at- tempt has been made to initiate this very project there can be no manner of doubt.</p>
        <p>The life of a special correspondent at this moment is not wanting in excitement. No sooner,for exam- ple. has Mr. Seppings Wright—the special artist of the " Illustrated London News "—arrived in Lon- don from Ashanti, than he has been dispatched by the proprietors of that journal to Wady Haifa, to join the Egyptian Expedition in the Soudan.</p>
        <p>Canon Knox-Little is again occupying the pulpit in St. Paul's Cathedral at the noon service this week. Last year the eloquent, preacher was too ill to fill his accustomed duties,and in his opening sen- tences on Monday he expressed his thankfulness that once again he was Ole to meet his many friends. The sight in the cathe(:.rat 'at. s very remarkab!alfor the great congregation which filled the Bacebe- neath the dome assembled in about fifteen minutes, men from the warehouses and shops hurrying in to hear the Canon in their dinner hour. At times one was reminded of the late Dr. Tait's face when the Canon turned his profile rapidly towards the con- gregation.</p>
        <p>A mysterious circular which was ultimately ex- plained away, addressed to military officers as to the possibility of a swift mobilisation,almost threw the country into a panic a few months ago; and here now we have another circular, but addressed this time to retired naval officers. Its object is to ascertain whether they are willing to resume active service if they receive a summons to that effect. From this all manner of inferences are drawn, the most ominous of which is that there are dangers threatening us ahead, the knowledge of which the country is not allowed to share. with the Cabinet. But I believe the real meaning of the circular is simple enough. it is only to enable the Admiralty to ascertain what number of new men it requires to provide in the way of officers. The retired list is lull of competent men, trained to their profession, eager to have an opportunity of rejoining it, and, moreover, in the prime of life,who should rather be looked upon as a reserve than as a body of half superannuated officers. I believe the replies which the Admiralty have received have been so numer- ous that they can see their way to officering the new ships they will be able to build down to the end of their present programme.</p>
        <p>It is not easy to ascertain what truth there is in the suspicion that the Cabinet is in a divided con- dition on the question of the advance into the Sou- dan. There is no room, however, for doubting that Mr. Chamberlain is the really aggressive spirit in it, and that he has found Mr. Goschen and Mr. Balfour to be more easily drawn after him than any other of his colleagues. As far as the correct interpreta- tion of the hints anJ disclosures of policy already made enable us to go, it would appear that before the project reached the ears of the public the Cabinet had committed themselves to a movement to Dongola, as an intermediate resting point for a subsequent speedy advance upon Khartoum. Then domestic and international criticism frightened them, and they modified their ideas.</p>
        <p>"General" Booth has now all but completed his arrangements for the "General post" that takes place among the heads of the movement every five years. Commissioner Howard succeeds Commis- sioner Booth-Tucker (now on his way to America) at the Foreign Office. The Australasian command is given to "Commandant" Herbert Booth, who :has acquired colonial experience in Canada. Commis- sioner Coombs comes from the antipodes to take command in the United Kingdom. Holland and Belgium are given in charge of Commissioner and " Marechale ' Booth Clibborn. The new "Chancel- lor of the Exchequer" at the International head- quarters is Commissioner Pollard.</p>
        <p>If there are to be many more Boer witnesses in the Jameson trial, the hardworked reporters will have to apply for the services of a special interpre- ter. On Tuesday they were left to make the best they could of the name of a place which came trip- pingly off the tongue of Bouwer, the Boer witness, but for which the ordinary signs of stenography offer no equivalent. In the depositions the name appears as Van Uithoouis Winkelspruit. The near- est the "Evening Standard" reporter got to it was Van Nit Hookiswinkel Sprint. the "Globe" gently modified this to Van Nit Hoorriswinkel, while the "Sun" simplified it to Van Oudtshoorn's, and the "Evening News" boldly made one fearsome word of it, thus—Vanoudtschawanswinkel. The rest of the reporters fenced gently with it, or, like prudent men, declined it altogether.</p>
        <p>The Prince of Wales will shortly receive an in- fluentially signed Canadian petition of a somewhat peculiar nature. It is nothing less than that, put of special consideration for the loyalty shown by the people of the Dominion, the Prince of Wales Leinster Regiment (made up of the old 100th and 109th) should revert to its original name of the 100th—the Royal Canadian Regiment. The Royal Canadians were raised in Canada at the time of the Crimean War, and Senator Boulton,of the Canadian Parliament relates what a queerly-dressed crowd they were on arriving in England for Shorncliffe Camp. The regiment was uniformed in relics gathered from old military stores in Canada, and "only lacked pigtails and powder to make it ap- pear as if one of the Duke's veteran battalion of the Penisula had come to life.</p>
        <p>Same difficulty has apparently arisen in regard to the formal constitution of the Royal Commission on the Licensing Question and the settlement of the terms of reference. Mr. William Johnston has, at the request of the First Lord of the Treasury, again postponed his question on the subject—this time until next Monday. As far as can be ascertained, the difficulty is mainly with regard to the members of tbe Commission who are to represent the liquor interests. Some of the brewers are showing a strong indisposition to have anything at all to do with the Commission, which they regard as an unnecessary body, to the constitution of which Government so largely indebted to the publican vote ought not to have consented. The full list of temperance members has already been published, and I do not hear of any alterations in connection With it. The following, it is said, will probably constitute the neutral element :—Viscount Peel (chairman), Mr. Wharton, M.P., Sir Algernon West, l',^rd Jersey, The O'Connor Don, and Mr. Graham, M.P. •</p>
        <p>I have authority for the statement that the Venezuelan dispute has assumed such a phase that Fir Julian Pauncefort will be able to leave Wash- ington about the end of the month for London, in brfier to have a full discussion of the British case pith Lord Salisbury on the latter's return from the outh of France.</p>
        <p>1 It is significant that the movement on the sub- ect of the death duties has not beep by any means warmly taken up among he Ministerialists. The fromoters of the memorial now express their will- ingness to be satisfied with a fair number of" repre- Fentative signatures," without attempting a count f beads. But if Lord Salisbury ever agrees to re- l.eive the deputation a vigorous outcry may be tmfely anticipated.</p>
        <p>TO PRESERVE YOUR HAIR use common sense: don, ktin it by sousing it with crdd water or starve it by neglect living things reqnire n,purishment, and pure grease is un- doubtedly the beat nourisher of the hair: for more than a c. nturv.</p>
        <p>ROWLAND'S MACASSAR OIL has been considered the best preserver of the hdr ever used :it prevents it falling off 'or turning grey, ramoves scurf and promotes a luxuriant growth: for children it is uneurpass.d : also in a golden colour lor fair hair Bottles 33 6d., 75., 10s 6d. (equal to 4 small). glow:and's Macassar 011.—Nourishes the hair. Rowland's Macassar oil.—Prevrnts baldness. Ask anywiv-re for Rowland's Macassar OIL ef 20, Hatton -Garden, London, and avoid cheap, worthless imitations.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1896-03-28_WidnesExaminer_art0042" n="art0042">
        <head>Al! rights teserved. THINGS A WOMAN WANTS TO KNOW.</head>
        <p>Al! rights teserved. THINGS A WOMAN WANTS TO KNOW.</p>
        <p>• 1 WONDER why it is that so little thought is given, as a rule, to dressing - jackets, and, indeed, dressing gowns. The former are the most comfortable garments it is possible to have, and I cannot imagine any lady doing; without one, but the generality of people con- sider a plain flannel dressing-jacket is sufficient, and that nothing more is required, nor can be desired. Just lately I have been seeing a goud deal of a friend of mine who is recovering from a long illness, and I have been very much pleased with the daintiness of her bed-jackets. One especially attracted me. It was of white cash- mere, trimmed with yellow lace. lam giving you a sketch of it in this column. You will see that it is made with a square yoke, back and front, over which is a collar cut in battle- ments, stitched round three times with coarse white silk, and then edged with yellow lace. The full bishop sleeves are gathered into shaped cuffs stitched like the collar, with a long lace ruffle beneath. Let me commend this jacket to your notice, for though you may consider the colour and fabric somewhat too fragile for your use, you</p>
        <p>Pretty Dressing Jacket. No. 1945. Paper Pattern, 61c1 To be had from PATTERN DEPARTMENT, 18, HENRIETTA.</p>
        <p>STREET, LONDON, W.C.</p>
        <p>can instead choose a pretty French merino of pale colouring, and fashion it after this design, and I am sure you will be thoroughly pleased with the result.</p>
        <p>My friend complained to me very much of bow tired she was of scents during her long imprisonment in a sickroom ; in fact she said that she had' entirely given up perfumes, and every now and then had her room freshened by some pungent odour. When I was there a very strong smell of camphor pervaded the apartment, and she told me that by putting a piece of lump camphor on a saucer and laying a red-hot poker on it, the strong fumes from the camphor generated through the room, fumes not at all overpowering, but only exceed- ingly. re fre During the last month I have been taking many long walks, as one is wont to do in the early days of spring as soon as the roads are dry. Being unused to exercise, I found my feet terribly sore and rather inclined to blister. My companion one day was an old soldier, who bad done a great deal of marching, and he rather smiled at my discomfiture. It was no laughing matter to me, and I told him 80. He then said that if I followed his advice I should not know *hat blisters were in future. The remedy is simplicity itself, so I readily followed it. Ench night, for several nights, I rubbed the soles of my feet with methylated spirit, and now I find I can take an eighteen or twenty irile -74.11; without any fear of: blister'S or sore feet. Have you :aocleed bow tflucli black silk and satin are being worn for the small spring capes that are all the rage? Formerly these materials were relegated to our elders, but we have changed all that, and every lady now may use them with impunity. It was of black satin of the richest quality that the original of the accompanying sketch of a cape was made. The•garment is short and full you will notice, and is handsomely trimmed in the form of a shoulder-cane with jet passementerie. A full ruffle of lace with long ends in front completes this dainty little wrap. I was very much charmed with this cape When I first saw It, a.,•,1 i ct thoroughly coveted it, but at first the Lost seemed too v,reat. However, I found that I had a good</p>
        <p>A Pretty Cape. No. 1940. Paper Pattern, eld. To be had from PATTERN DEPARTMENT, 18, liraquirrr.t STREET, LONDON, W.C.</p>
        <p>deal of jet by me, which, by careful manipula- tion, would answer the purpose, so I bought the satin, and laid the jet on carefully myself. I lined the cape with a length of pale-green brocade, which had been lying by me for some time, and now I am wearing it, conscious of the fact that it is one. of the smartest garments to be seen anywhere. I never throw away any pieces of trimming, especially jet, for it is wonderful how they will • piece together, and come out as good as new again. I know that the piecing is a good deal ot trouble, but if you can make up your mind to devote the time to it, the result is perfectly certain to be good. This piecing is work which will admit of no scamping, for all edges must . be neatly sewn, otherwise they will curl and look untidy as soon as the garment is worn. . I am very pleased to notice that simplicity in hair-dressing is still maintained. The smartest coiffures are those which have the hair drawn into a neat little knot at the back of the head. The hair is worn loose in front as before, but. fringes, are things of the past. Some.people think it is necessary to wash the hair perpetually in order to dress it according to the'pi esent fashion, but this is by no means desirable, for • constant washing weakens the hair considerably, and makes it inclined to :fall. I have recently found a charming shampoo which, if used every two or three weeks, keeps the hair in perfect condition, and at the same time slightly strengthens it. Take the yolk of one fresh egg (two eggs if the hair is very long), beat it up well, and add to it one pint of warm rain-water, to which has been added a teaspoonful of household ammonia. Beat all well together, and with this wash your hair. When it is thoroughly cleansed, rinse it with a plentiful supply of warm soft water, and when dry the hair will • be beautifully soft, and ready to curl or arrange 'in any way you like.</p>
