      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1896-03-28_WidnesExaminer_art0051" n="art0051">
        <head>WOMAN'S RESCUE.</head>
        <p>A WOMAN'S RESCUE.</p>
        <p>There is a woman in 'Hadley, near Wigan, to 'whom more than two years age, a reporter of the" Wigan Observer" was sent on a subject which has created a great local sensation. Her name is Mrs. Henry Lloyd Roberts, and her present address is 231, Liverpool-road Hindley, near Wigan. This lady, in a bright, chatty way, told the story of her trouble and of her triumph, and,for a reason which will presently become apparent, the facts are worthy of republication. It seems that a little before the rep3rter's visit, the woes of indigestion and nervousness attacked Mrs. Roberts with terrible force. " I could not eat anything," she said, " and I was so nervous that when I only expected a letter I was all of a flutter. I could not sleep. In fact, I was afraid of going to sleep for fear</p>
        <p>I SHOULD NOT WAKE AGAIN. A knock at the door drove me wild.. If I walked a yard my heart would palpitate, and I had got to think I had heart disease. it was agony I could ILA move about. My face and lips were pale, and people used to stare at me like a walking ghost. I was bad for a long while, and I went to an infirmary. When I went I thought I should have died on the way. They did me no good— indeed, I was worse when I stopped that when I</p>
        <p>began. Then reading the " Wigan Observer," Mrs. Roberts made the discovery which led to her triumph. She read an account of the wonderful cures effected by Dr. Wil- liams' Pink Pills for Pale People, and wrote for a supply. The first three boxes did her good. " I have had eight boxes altogether," she said. " They realty have done me a wonderful amount of good. I could only eat corn-flour, and had to live on slops, but now I can eat anything I please, thanks to these. I would advise any- one troubled with their nerves to try them. I used to have regular attacks (lasting forty-eight hours) of neu- ralgic headache, but Dr. Williams' Pills entirely cured them and they never returned."</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1896-03-28_WidnesExaminer_art0052" n="art0052">
        <head>TWO YEARS AFTER.</head>
        <p>TWO YEARS AFTER.</p>
        <p>The above interview took place two years ago, and in order to ascertain whether the cure was permanent, a letter was written last month to Mrs. Roberts on the subject. She replied, "I am happy to say that I have had good health ever since taking Dr. Williams' Pink Pills for Pale People, and if I feel the least symptam of my old complaint, I take a Pill or two, and am all right again. I always keep some in the house, in case of need." _</p>
        <p>The latter is a proper precaution, as, while Dr. Wil. iams' Pink Pills are unlike any other medicine, and their effects are permanent, no other medicine has as much usefulness, as a strengthening and tonic medicine, fcr men and women. Take care that the package bears the full name, Dr. Williams' Pink Tills for Pale People, and in case of doubt send direct to Dr. Williams' Medicine Company, 46, Holborn Viaduct, London, E.C., as the Pills can .be had post-free at '2s. 9d., or six boxes for 13s. Vd. They have cured numerous cases of neuralgia, i I , ndigestion, rheumatism, influenza, ansemia, palpita- tions, and the disorders which arise from impoverished blood, such as muscular weakness, loss of appetite,short- nese of 'breath, pains in the back, nervous headache, early decay, all forms of female weakness, hysteria, paralysis, locomotor ataxy, rheumatism, aud sciatica.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1896-03-28_WidnesExaminer_art0053" n="art0053">
        <head>JAPANESE SILK, AND WOOLLEN INDUSTRIES, &amp;c., &amp;c.</head>
        <p>JAPANESE SILK, AND WOOL- LEN INDUSTRIES, &amp;c., &amp;c.</p>
        <p>(BY ME. H. WHITHAM.)</p>
        <p>every child in thP empire is a genius in the nial“:- face ure of colors!' _ . .</p>
        <p>Mr. Carpenter, writing of this business, says : "There are villages in Japan that make nothins but jute rugs. I visited one in Osaka, and I was intro- duced to the biggest of the manufacturers, a man who employed 3,000 hands. The work was done almost altogether by hand. He had 200 houses in his establishment. Some of the children who were rug-making were under six years of age, and there were a number of girls about ten. They receive about 21. a day. They have no Sundays but have two holidays each month." The Japs are now placing fine imitations of Turk- ish and Egyptian carpets in the market. These carpets are all made by children, and in the low gloomy rooms of the Japanese houses troops of little boys and girls are working at this dusty trade with the zeal and intelligence of grown people. Of course jute rugs and hemp carpets do not compare in beauty or usefulness with woollen rugs and car- pets, but they must be reckoned with, and it is a cold fact that, vihether they are as good or not,they are displacing European and American manufacture. They have been copying modern machinery, and they purpose manufacturing woollen goods, and with their adaptive genius and great taste they are likely to make an impression when they begin to put this class of goods in the market. They are rapidly displacing woollen floor coverings. Japanese floor matting now floods the market, and for the making of which they are without rivals. The total of Japan's exports for 1894 was close on I £4,000,000. When looking at these figures the low I values arising from cheap labour should be kept in mind. Of course the list of exports from Japan is long and varied, and I could not pretend to exhaust it, yet before touching upon the mineral products, it may be as well to notice one or two of the minor phases of the new invasion. In matches she is likely to soon have the whole market. The United States received in 1892 about 150 gross, in 1893, 1,300 gross, and in 1894, 5,285 gross. That was doing a match business by leaps and bounds. Workmen on watches are paid about 41d. a day. In America the same labour receives from Ils. to 14s. per day. The correspondent of the " London Times" who described the Kioto exhibition, says: "Boots and shoes I can vouch for from personal experience, whether made by Chinese or Japanese. I have worn them in London, and had them valued by London boot makers at just double their cost." I cannot go this far yet, although a Jap cobbler has his shop next dlor, I have not been able to go in for what I was afraid might be " cheap and nasty." Eve-glasses, cigarettes, and beer, the latter,the Japs assert, is now claiming the Eastern market to the confusion of the Germans. As the Japs become more civilized, that is, Europeanised, they become better able to cultivate the " beer and bacca taste. The exports in brass and copper wire are not, as yet, very large, only £227,764. They excel in the manufacture of all forms of carved and ornamental furniture. Their aptitude at inlaying exceeds that of any other people. Their carving gives evidence of surprising skill. In ivory and hone and wood carving they have no superiors. The agency here in San Francisco is now selling matches, woodware, doors, sashes, blinds, watches, clocks, boots, shoes, clothing, and all sorts of " notions." Mr. Burns,tbe agentwaited upon the head of a new colonisation, and offered to contract to deliver at this port sashes, blinds, and mouldings to be used in the construction of houses at 30 per cent. less than they could be bought here, and equal in every way to those made in this coun- try. One writer states that "Japan is now publishing some of the most beautiful books in the world, and there is an establishment which will compare in size with those of our big publishers. The prices for printing and engraving are wonderfully cheap." The principal paper buyers:of Japan are American and English. Instead of cases marked, " made in Germany 4' it will now be" made in Japan.' In concluding this brief, and, I feel, vary inade- quate collection of Japanese Industrial items it is right that something should be said of the metal trades. Ship building may be left out, although I find that Sir E. Arnold says,—" Japan will be one of the world's great naval powers. She can build her own ships. She is building five now." I also see that the China Mutual Steamship Company of London has all its vessels repaired in China and Japan. This is evidence that British labour is be- ing displaced by the cheaper Oriental.</p>
        <p>'One of the results of the recent Chinese-Japanese war has been to throw open to the latter nation the markets of China. This, and the persistent endea- vours of Russia to fasten on the Corea and by that means gain the Mongolian market, makes it still more evident that the workers of the west need to keep their eyes wide open. 1 have no desire to pose as a "calamity prophet," but to be forewarned is to be forearmed. So Tar back as 1873 the Japanese astonished Europeans by their display of silk fabrics at the Vienna Exhibition. Their exhibits of simple smooth stuffs, and surprisingly beautiful twilled fabrics full of softness and elegance, heavy brocades and other figured materials, were entirely unanticipated. Mere imitativeness could not have produced such results. We now know that other nations are striving in vain to compete with Japan in the manufacture of these articles, for which the Jape seem to have a natural gift in the making. In 1894 there were in Kioto 120,000 silk looms and they now compete with Trance in all kinds of silk; several large factories have lately been erected. One which was ,erected a year or so ago cost £lOO.OOO, and it is largely operated by women and girls who receive from Sd. to 10d. per day. The best of the male operatives make as much as 2s. a day, and they are working with modern machinery. "Ths Swiss Consul reported to his country in 1894 , that" The Japanese industry is making rapid pro- j greeBB in the silk and satin line, both in house-made ,goods and satins manufactured on power looms," and intimated that his countrymen trust expect a eerious rivalry in the production of many classes of silk goods which i they have hitherto monopolized. They have a full supply of raw material at their door, and they fully appreciate all the advantages. They do all in their power to stimulate the produc- tion of raw silk and to improve its quality. Mr. Rein in his book "The Industries of Japan," says, "The introduction of silk culture into Japan is recorded as taking place in the second half of the third century. The exports of Japanese raw silk began in 1859. A noticeable improvement in the exported grege took place in 1872, when the Government built at Tominka, in Musashi, a great reeling establishment." The high price of this raw silk from Tomioka has caused a great deal of emu- lation, and a large number of 'Mendes have sprung up in silk-raising centres and silk markets gener- ally at the suggestion and expense of the Govern- ment. Sir Edwin Arnold after a careful survey of e labor and resources of Japan which says In silk-growing and silk- tmhaeuisnitguaantidonth, she will 'developond iu an infinitely varied and in- finitely numerous list Of industries in which cheap labor must be the chief element, she will rule the Iworld. Hemp has been grown in apan for several thousand years, and has occupied much the same place in the industrial history of that country that flax did in ancient Egypt. The trousers and blouses of every-day wear met with in every part of Japan are made of this enduring material. My lady readers will know how beautiful and tasteful that filmy article known as "drawn work* is. When I have watched the Spaniards and Mexicans absorbed in the tedious work I have wondered if competi- tion could enter into it. Americans familiar with the ingenuity of the Japs caused experiments to be made in that country to ascertain whether the so-called Spanish "drawn work" could be profit- ably imitated. The result surpassed all expecta- tions; not only did they successfully imitate, but,as is usual with them, improved on their models, in- troducing many new patterns. Japanese 'drawn work is now taking the place of the more expen- sive Spanish and Mexican work. At one time a high grade of linen was imported for this "drawn work, that didn't suit the Japs,so they set to work to remedy the trouble, and with such success that they promise to rival the products of the best linen manufacturers of other countries. Hemp is culti- vated all over Japan, and the climate and soil are favorable to its highest development. The possi- bilities and manipulation of hemp by the Japs led them to think of jute, and they began importing large quantities from lndia for the purpose of con- verting it into rugs and carpets. They are now mak- ing the most beautiful rugs in the world and the cheapest. An American firm in Kiobe is now ship- ping vast quantities to America and Europe. This firm had artists to go to all the great museums of Europe and copy the colors and patterns of the finest rugs. They bound these patterns in a book which they sent out to Japan and put into the hands of the workmen, and now these famous rugs are being copied in jute. The jute was brought from India, and the new rugs are equal to the originals!' They sell for a song in comparison to the Turkish rugs,and there is a possibility that the Japanese will take up with the making of woollen rugs. If they do they will crowd the Eastern rugs out of the market, for they are born artists, and</p>
        <p>THE EXAMINER, SATURD 'A, MARCH 28 1896._</p>
        <p>7 7 7, .2 ti I.</p>
        <p>The weArn-ss and ailments so many women suffer are known only to their own sex. Men have no conception of them. We often comment superficially on the thin necks Or emaciated forms of women, looking at them only from the standpoint of beauty, when, if we knew what many of them have suffered, we would withhold much that escaped our lips. However, in thousands of cases it is not only possible, but quite easy, for women to gain strength and flesh. All they need is to be shown the way, which is revealed in the following letter :</p>
        <p>"I feel I must write and tell you how much good the use of Scott's Emulsion has done my wife. She had a very severe confinement, followed by a gathered breast. An operation was performed, which left her in a very weak state; her appetite was entirely gone, and I was beginning to get exceedingly anxious at see.- ing her wasting gradually away. A friend Id mine advised me to try Scott's Emulsion, which I did, and with many doubts as to its efficacy. I can assure you, however, my wife began to mend soon after beginning to take it. She is continuing its use,and every day seems to improve her. I think it is most wonderful to see with what rapidity she gains weight and flesh. I cannot too highly and conscientiously re- commend its use to all who, like my wife, have suffered and who long to regain their lost strength. I shall only be too glad to hear if this letter should be the means of causing anybody to give it a trial, as they will find I have in no way exaggerated the sterling qualities of Scott's Emulsion.—Faithfully yours (signed), C. F. HORTON, 48, Flaxm an- road, Coldharbour-lane, Brix- ton, January 9th, 1896." It is very easy to satisfy yourself of the many uses of Scott's `3,sialulsion. The medical world, including the most learned physicians, has endorsed the formula for making it nearly a quarter of a century, and you can ask your own doctor about it if you should be in doubt. Scott's Emulsion contains the best Norway cod-liver oil, so manipulated that it is easy on the digestive organs and perfectly palatable, and combines this im- portant food with the Hypophosphites of Lime and Soda, thus making an ideal nourishment for feeding the blood and restoring the vital forces. Among the first beneficial effects of taking Scott's Emulsion are increased appetite and a feeling of strength. Scott's Emulsion nourishes the system when no other similar preparation does any good, this being due to its peculiar martipula- tian and high standard of excellence. It is useful in all phases of emaciation, loss of flesh, and weakness. None genuine unless the wrapper contains trademark of a man cakryiug a big fish on his back. (14)</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1896-03-28_WidnesExaminer_art0054" n="art0054">
        <head>CARD</head>
        <p>A CARD</p>
        <p>KNOWN as Rev. Joseph Holmes' Mexican Prescription is the only original guaranteed Remedy for tall those who suffer from nervous weakness, exhausted vitality. kidney or bladder diseases, gravel, prostate, spinal weak• ness, brain fag and kindred complaints. It has stood the test for twenty years. Send a self-addressed stamped envelope to Jos. HOLMES' Remedy Co. Bloomsbury Man !lions, Bloomsbury Square, London, and get full particulars of this great specific free of charge. Mention whether for male or female, Beware of fraudnient imitators.</p>
        <p>H.OOD'S PILLS cure constipation and liver ills. Sold by cnemists. Insist upon HOOD'S. is. iL'ci. (4)</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1896-03-28_WidnesExaminer_art0055" n="art0055">
        <head>BOWLING.</head>
        <p>BOWLING.</p>
        <p>LiNCASHIRE END CHESHIRE COUNTY ASSOCIATION.— THE LANCASHIRE CHAMPIONSHIP.—The first meeting of the Council was held on Tuesday evening, Mr. Alderman Meakin in the chair. Nearly all the affiliated districts were represented. Mr. J. Isherwood (Leigh) was unani- mously elected vice president of the association. A special committee of five was appointed to select the team to play Yorkshire in Lancashire on the 11th July. It was also resclved to refer all disputed matters during the season for settlement to the same committee, and that they agree on a list of fixtures for governing county everts. Forty-three affiliated districts entered the Lan- cashire Championship Competition on the understanding the playing teams would be limited to the dozen selected and two reserves, whose names must be tendered prior to the time named for the delivery of the "first jack." All contested events to be played off between the 28th March and 25th April inclusive. The following was the result of the draw :—Radcliffe v. Cheetham, to be played at Prestwich; Walton v. Chorlton Green, at North War- rington; Timperley v. Brooklands, at Old Trafford; Ker- , sal v. Prestwich, at Cheetham Hilt; Douglas v. Sefton Park, at Newton-le-Willows; Northenden v. Higher Broughton, at Chorlton ; Whalley v. Caledonians, at StTetford; Broughton (Cobden) v. Cheetham Hill. at Kersal Moor; Didsbury v. Croft Bank, at Timperley ; Newton-le-Willows v. Harpurhey, at Tyldesley ; South Warrington v. Heaton Mersey, at Brooklands. Byes: Ashton, Tyldesley, Chorlton,Wigan,Warrington (North), Kersal Moor, Hindsford, Salford (Regent), Fallowfield, Urmston Nelson, Urmston Victoria, Old Trafford, Hazel Grove, Withington, Stretford, Levenshulme, Leigh, Old Trafford (Limited), Lower Broughton, Pendleton (Wool ,- pack and Horseshoe).</p>
        <p>Mr. Gladgtone, in acknowledging an intimation from the Duke of Argyll that the sum of £31,000 had beea subscribed for the relief of the Armenians, comments upon the "deplorable and ignominious failure of Europe" to discharge the duties which it owes to the Christian subjects of the Sultan. A Birmingham jeweller named Brocklebank was re- manded on Monday on a curious charge of frald. It is alleged that by means of advertisements throughout the country he received hundreds of watches to repair, and that very few were sent back.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1896-03-28_WidnesExaminer_art0056" n="art0056">
        <head>IMPERIAL PARLIAMENT.</head>
        <p>IMPERIAL PARLIAMENT.</p>
        <p>MONDAY.</p>
        <p>In the House of Comm—Was on Monday in answer to Mr. W. Johnson, Mr. BALFOUR said that although the Royal Commission on the Liquor Traffic had practicd.lly been decided upon, it was impossible for him to state the names. There was no intention to exclude Ireland from the scope of inquiry. The Budget, he informed Mr. Lambert, would be introduced on Thursday, April 16. Mr. CHAMBERLAIN stated, in reply to a question put by Mr. Harrison, that the Government had" no inten- tion whatever to grant any charter to any company on the West Coast of Africa." Mr. LAMBERT asked when the Bills dealing with the relief of agricultural land from rating and the amend- ment of the law relating to agricultural holdings were Ito be introduced. Mr. Balfour said the bill fra the re- lief of agricultural land could not be introduced. before the Budget, and he did not see any immediate prospect of introducing the Agricultural Holdings Bill. On the motion for the seconding reading of the Diseases of Animals Bill, Mr. WHITE moved the rejection of the measure. Mr. PRICE seconded the amendment. Amongst the Conservatives who opposed the Bill was Mr. G. Whiteley (Stockport), who declared that the victory of the Unionist party at the last election was not gained on such measures as the one under dis- cussion, and that the majority then gathered together was being used, not in passing measures for the benefit and advantage of all, but in promoting measures which were designed for the benefit of on class at the expanse of the whole community. Some cross-voting took place but the second reading was carried by 224 votes to 95.</p>
        <p>In the House of Lords on Monday a questbn was put to the Government with regard to the prosecution of persons known to be guilty of the offence of paying wages in public-houses. Lord BELPER replied that pro- secutions were instituted in all cages of the kind that came within the knowledge of the police. Inquiries had been made in many industrial centres, and it was found that such cases were extremely rare. Lord HARRIS briefly introduced a Bill to remove or modify some of the restrictions now placed on the use of " horseless carri- ages."</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1896-03-28_WidnesExaminer_art0057" n="art0057">
        <head>TUESDAY.</head>
        <p>TUESDAY.</p>
        <p>The first subjects for discussion in the House of Com- mons on Tuesday were six bilis dealing with the water supply of London, promoted by the London County Council. Mr. J. Stuart, in explaining the object of the first bill on the paper, sugyested that as the principle embodied in it applied to each of the others there should be ne discussion upon the subsequent bills. The main principle of the bill, he added, was that of purchase by the representative municipal authority, first by agree- ment, and failing that by compulsory arbitration. Sir F. Dixon-Hartland moved the rejection of the measure, and the amendment was carried by 287 votes to 125. The other five bills were negatived without a division. Mr. CtruzoN, replying to Mr. W. Allen, said no des- patches respecting the advance up the Nile had been re- ceived from Lord Cromer. Mr. J. Morley asked whether any papers with regard to the advance were to be laid before the House, and Mr. Curzon replied that no papers were likely to be laid on the table" at this stage." Mr. BALFOUR, in reply to a question from Mr. Dalziel. refused to add anything to his previous statements with , regard to the allowance to the Duke of Cambridge. Mr. S. BUXTON subsequently moved for the appoint- ment of a Select Committee to consider the question of the administration of the fair wages resolution of Febru- ary, 1391. Mr. G,shen, in accepting the resolution, ex- pressed the hope that those concerned would not seek to invent impossible definitions or to impair the efficiency of the great services. Mr. Broadhurst was speaking in support of the resolution hen the House was collided out.</p>
        <p>In the House of Lords a message was received from the Queen conveying Her Majesty's acquiescence in the ropuest that certain funds which are now distributed for Established Church purposes in Wales might not be al- lowed to he absorbed in the general education fund. Lord Monteagle said, in answer to a question, that the Government were not unfriendly in principle to the nasimiliation of the Irish municipal franchise to the English system, but there could be nothing done in re- gard to that this session. WEDNESDAY.</p>
        <p>In the House of Commons on Wednesday Mr. QUILTER moved the second reading of his Pure Beer Bill, which,he said, was honestly brought forward in the interests of agriculture. Colonel KENYON SLANEY supported the bill. Mr. USRORNE, in proposing that it should be rejected, stated that as a brewer he believed that it would be in- juriou, to the interest of agriculture, Mr. KLAIBER seconded the amendment. Sir W. Lawsox thought the bill was a sham, and that we had quite enough sham statutes already. Sir W HARCOURT said the liquor trade was becoming a gigantic monopoly, and what the noun. try really wanted was some security against that mono- poly. Sir M. HICKS-BFACH RnZgekted to the promoters of the bill that he should endeavour to provide for some kind of expert inquiry into the necessity for any further measures to prove the use of deleterious substances in the manufacture of beer, and also ascertain whether, in the opinion of those experts, it was advisable or desirable to define the materials of which beer was composed, without undue interference with the freedom of the brewers to use any wholesome material they wished to. Mr. QUILTER had no hesitation in areeDtine in good faith the offer made by the Chancellor of the Exchequer, and withdrew his bill.</p>
        <p>Mr. J. W. MACLURE moved the second reading of the Foreign Goods (Provention of Fraud) Bill. Mr. KEARLICY was speaking at half past five, and the debate stood ad- journed. The Select Committre on distress from want of em- ployment was afterwards appointed.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1896-03-28_WidnesExaminer_art0058" n="art0058">
        <head>SHIP CANAL.</head>
        <p>SHIP CANAL.</p>
        <p>LAST WEEK'S TRAFFIC. Last week was an exceptionally busy one on the Ship Canal, the traffic being the heaviest recorded for many months. Among.rt the arrivals v ere 22 vessels with general Car(), 24 with pottery materials, 15 with stone and slate, 7 with pia iron, 4 with green fruit, 3 with cotton, 3 with logwood, and one each with iron ore, tiles, timber, and woodpnlp. Twenty-ono vessels sailed with general cargo, and several cargoes of coal, pitch, and salt were exported.</p>
        <p>Two mrm have heen anprehended in London on sus- picion of being concerned in the extensive robbery from a branch post-office at Gl Isgow. They were found in poss ession of a large number of stolen postal orders, and one of them had stolen a date-stamp from a London office, by me ans of which he had put several orders into circulation. One of the prkoners is strongly suspected of lo,lng concerned in the robbery and murder at Mus- well Hill. He W3S captured by a post-office clerk, at a moment when he had in his possession a loaded revolver.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1896-03-28_WidnesExaminer_art0059" n="art0059">
        <head>WAP.RINGTON.</head>
        <p>WAP.RINGTON.</p>
        <p>CHANGES AT THE INFIRMARY.</p>
        <p>Mr. E.i..—Bowdea,who for some years has capably filled the position of senior house surgeon at the Warrington Infirmary, has lately resigned that pest to take up a private practice, and now resides in Bold-street. Mr. Henry Langdale, for many years the junior house surgeon, has been appointed to succeed Mr. Bowden, and the vacancy created by Mr. Langdale's promotion has been filled by Mr. E. W. Battle.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1896-03-28_WidnesExaminer_art0060" n="art0060">
        <head>CHESS.</head>
        <p>CHESS.</p>
        <p>The Warrington Chess Club were beaten by the Jewish Club of Manchester (away) on Monday night in the" B" League. The scores were as follows . .</p>
        <p>Won. Jewish Club. Drawn. Warrington. Won. 0 L Glass — Father Firth B Menkin 1 F Dunne 1 J D Temple G H Mason 0 C Horowitz 1 F G Taylor 1 J Landes — A Wood N Kauffman J Abensur</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1896-03-28_WidnesExaminer_art0061" n="art0061">
        <head>QUEEN'S THEATRE OF. VARIETIES.</head>
        <p>QUEEN'S THEATRE OF. VARIETIES.</p>
        <p>On Monday night an admirable programme was submitted at the above popular place of amuse- ment and was most heartily appreciated. Messrs. Geraghty and Gilligan,lrish knockabout comedians, contributing some capital items. Miss Marie Riviers, who is an accomplished ballad vozalist,was also a favourite, several encores falling to her lot. The performance of Martina,contortionist and hand jumper; of lee freres Napier, grotesque gymnastic and eccentric jugglers; and those of Henri Sardou, illusionist, commanded a good share of the appreci- tion. Captain Alexander Fox as a venttiloquist and mimic was exceedingly entertaining, and evoked much applause. Mr. A. G. Spry occasioned uninter- rupted amusement by his comic songs and dances, whilst Miss Eugenie Creswell provided a further enjoyment by her ballad singing. PRESENTATION TO COUNCILLOR DR. CANNELL. The members connected with the Loyal Friend- ship Lodge of the National Independent Order of Oddfellows assembled in large numbers on Monday at the Green Dragon Inn. Bridge-street, the occa- sion being the presentation of a beautifully framed emblem to Councillor Dr. Cannel!, who, though he has only been medical adviser for the short space of two years,has won the esteem of every member of the lodge. P.G. W. Daniels took the chair, and V.G. James Green the vice-chair. They were supported</p>
        <p>. . by the following brethren :—Bros. Councillor Dr. Cannel], P.G.M. Thomas Knight, D.S.J. Crosby, P.P.G.M. Henry Longshaw, P.P.G.M. James Bram- hall, P.G.E. Smith Wood, G.M.J.W. Johnson, P.G. Thomas Dickenson, jun., P.G. Richard Rigby,Secre- tary John Burgess.—The Chairman called upon Bro. Henry Longshaw to make the presentation, which he did after a few appropriate words.—Bro. Cannell spoke in suitable terms in acknowledgment of the gift,and Bros. Daniels, Longshaw, Knight, Burgess, Bramhall, and Wood spoke in eulogistic terms of Dr. Cannelrs services to the lodge. Songs were afterwards given by Bros. Appleby and ti oughton, and a solo by Bro. Rigby, a member of Mr. Down's band. The meeting was brought to a close about 10.30, after a most enjoyable evening had been spent.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1896-03-28_WidnesExaminer_art0062" n="art0062">
        <head>ODDFELLOWS7 PRESENTATION.</head>
        <p>ODDFELLOWS7 PRESENTATION.</p>
        <p>At the fortnightly meeting of this lodge, held on Saturday night at the registered offices, Academy- street, a presentation of a beautifully framed and illuminated past officer's certificate and jewel was made to P.G. John Gartland. There was a good attendance, and the proceedings were presided over by Bro. Herbert Hicks, N.G. of the lodge, and Bro. George Bamblett, V.G. Amongst the visiting brethren present was the Prov. G.M. (Bro. J. P. Whittle), the C.S. of the District (Bro. John Emson) and Bro. C. Barnwell, N.G. of the Admiral Nelson Lodge. The N.G. requested the Grand Master of the district to make the presentations on behalf of the Prince of Wales Lodge. The Grand Master (Bro. Whittle) on rising to present the certificate and jewel said it had fallen to his lot in little more than two months to make no fewer than Ave such presentations. It had given him great pleasure to attend this lodge to perform another such cere- mony, and to assist in the recognition of services rendered by individual members to their lodges. He then asked Bro. Gartland to accept the certific- ate, and forthwith pinned the gold jewel upon the breast of his coat. The jewel bore on one side the emblem of the order,and on the other the following inscription :—" Presented, by the Prince of Wales Lodge to Bro. John Gartland, for valuable and active services, March 21st, 1896."—8r0. Gartland briefly responded, thanking the Grand Master for his cemplimentary words and the lodge for the presents. He had only done what he thought was his duty towards the lodge, and should continue to do so. (Hear, hear.)—The Secretary of the lodge (Bro. T. Wadsworth), responding to the "Prince of Wales Lodge," gave a few figures relating to the membership of the lodge, and its financial position. He thought they were in a very encouraging condi- tion, considering all the drawbacks. During the year they bad. made 27 new members, making the total on the roll into 292. (Applause.) Songs and recitations were rendered at intervals,and the ledge was closed in the orthodox manner.</p>
        <p>HEARTS OF OAK BENEFIT SOCIETY.-FORMATION OF A LOCAL BRANCH.</p>
        <p>On Monday evening a meeting in connection with the Hearts of Oak Benefit Society, London, was held in the Druids' Hal:, Sankey-•st., Warrington. The primary object of the gathering was to solicit the support of the Warrington members of the society in the election of country delegates for the next year, and it had also been decided to consider the advisability of forming a local electoral branch. Mr. Charles Whitfield, of Widnes, presided, and was supported by Messrs. J. W. Weeks, secretary of the Liverpool branch, and Mr. John Bennett, Widnes. There was a good attendance.—The Chairman, after expressing pleasure at seeing so numerous an at- tendance. said that the society had reached a crisis. The London members wished to deny the country members a proportionate representation on the delegate board. This they in The country were pre- pared to strenuously oppose. When they considered that the society now numbered 207,000 members, and out of that 115,000 were in the country, they would acknowledge that they were entitled to some representation, not only on the delegation board,but in the committee of management. (Hear, hear.) They had also 11,000 on the Juvenile Society.— Mr. Weeks gave an explanation of the differ- ences between the London and country members. They in the country had done all they could to avoid the fight, but unfortunately they had failed. He appealed to the Warrington members for sup- port in the coming struggle for fair representation. Mr. Bennett moved "That the meeting, having heard Mr. Weeks' remarks, pledges itself to support by its interests and iotes, the country executive in its endeavours to retain its representation on the Board."—Mr. Lawes seconded, and the motion was unanimously carried. —The Chairman having intro- duced the question of the formation of a local branch, Mr. Lunt asked if Warrington could in- clude in its membership members from Earlestown, where he believed there was a large number.—The Chairman said that was so, and having answered other minor questions, put the motion to the meet- ing, and it was unanimously carried.—The following committee was at once selected :—Messrs. J. C Far- rington, H. Lunt, C. Crook, T. Rigby, J. Nfuskett, J. T. Whittall, W. Sparkes, W. Hall, J. Worrall, R. Whitfield, A. Maghall, and H. Winstanley.—Votes of thanks to the Chairman and speakers concluded the meeting.</p>
        <p>BUPTURES.-REY NOLDso Patent Im perceptible , Imillrubber St.m Pad TRUSS. Suitable in all cases. perfect Fit Ensured. Write or call for particulars.— REYNOLDS, 10, Slater-treet (a few doors Bold-street), Liverpool. Open on Saturday until 7 p.m.— aivt</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1896-03-28_WidnesExaminer_art0063" n="art0063">
        <head>ALLEGED BIGAMY AT WARRINGTON.</head>
        <p>ALLEGED BIGAMY AT WARRINGTON.</p>
        <p>"A PITIFUL CASE."</p>
        <p>On Tuesday morning at the Warrington Borough Court, before Councillor Roberts and Dr. Smith,a young and respectable-looking woman named Harriet Ann Geddes was charged with having stolen on the 1211 March, two blankets and other property to the value of £5, the goods ot Charles Lewis Geddes. Mr. A. Browne prosecuted and said that from his client's point of view it was a very sad case, inasmuch as, until lately, he thought a great deal of the prisoner. Some three years ago he was a widower and met prisoner whom he courted and subsequently went through the form (If marriage with her. He wished him (Mr. Browne) to say that he lived happily and comfortably with her until a week ago when her husband having turned up, she left him. He did not know that she was previously a married woman. Since his marriage with her he (prosecutor) had had one child by her. He was exceedingly sorry for what had occurred but she had pawned so many things he was bound to prosecute. The police had certain facts which they considered showed a clear case of bigamy and they wished him to apply for a remand of this case, as they intended to go on with the bigamy case. The Chief Constable said that was so. They were in possession of the two certificates showing that the prisoner had gone through the form of marriage with two persons; and, more than that, there were now in court actually the two men who had gone through the form of marriage with her. There could, therefore, be no doubt as to the truth of the charge. Mr. Browne intimated that if the police went on with the charge of bigamy his client might withdraw the charge of theft. All the pawntickets had been recovered. The case was extremely sad on both sides. The Chief Constable.—Yes it is, your worships, an exceedingly sad and pitiful one. Detective-Sergeant Monaghan deposed apprehending the prisoner on the previous afternoon at Ellesmere Port. _</p>
        <p>_ _ The magistrates decided to grant the remand, the prisoner raising no objection. When asked by the Deputy Magistrates' Clerk if she had any app:ication to make as to bail, the prisoner did not answer.</p>
        <p>Mr. Browne observed that he had not the slightest ob- jection to bail being granted, sseing that the woman was in such a unfortunate position. The Chief Constable said he was willing to allow bail with reasonable bonds. The woman had an infant child and it was mainly on that ground that he acceded. De- tective-Sergeant Monaghan had some difficulty in appre- hending her and it was only by the exercise of his usual skill and moz e than usual ingenuity that he arrested her.</p>
        <p>No one presented himself to stand as surety, but the Bench allowed bail, the Chief Constable agreeing to take a surety of £lO if the prisoner could secure the same.</p>
        <p>WARRINGTON CRICKET, BOWLING AND ASSOCIATION FOOTBALL CLUB.</p>
        <p>The annual meeting of this club was held in the Patten Arms Hotel, on Monday evening. There was a good attendance of members, including Alderman Sut- ton, Councillors Pierpoint and Tinnion, Messrs. S. M. Crosfield, G. Pilling, J. L. Whittle, .T. Dewhurst, L. A. Voisey, W. Richardson, J. Dunn. R. J. Byerley, E. Howard, W. Westwell,W. Gill, J.Mainwaring,S.Kenyon, Kiernan, R. Silcock, C. Higginhottom, C. Leigh. W. H. Robinson (hon. treasurer), and C. H. Unsworth (hon. secretary). Owing to a prior engagement, Mr.T.Morris (president) was unable to occupy his usual place as chairman, and Mr. T. Sutton was unanimously voted to preside.—Mr.W. H. Robinson (hon. treasurer) presented his annual balance sheet, which showed that the income for the past year included—subscriptions,£3o2 183. 6d. ; football receipts, £l5 ; cricket gate money, £67 145.8 d.; the total receipts being £4ll 13s. sd. The total expen- diture was £391 6s. Bd. The balance against the club has now been reduced to £23 15s. 4d., as against £44 , 25.1 d. —On the motion of Mr.S.M. Crosfield,lseconded by Mr. J. L. Whittle, the report was unanimously passed.— Mr. G. Higginbottom proposed Mr. W. H. Robinson as president for the ensuing year. He spoke in high terms of the abilities of their treasurer, who had laboured in- cessantly for the benefit of the club,and much of its suc- cess was due to his exertions.—Mr. Byerley seconded, and Mr. Dewhurst supported, the latter mentioning that during his 34 years experience he (the treasurer) had al- ways laboured for the benefit of the club and been one of the main factors in bringing about its present high state of excellence. (Applause.)—The resolution was put to the meeting and unanimously carried.—Mr. Robinson, in reply, thanked the members for the high honour which had been conferred upon him. He accepted the hon cur with thanks, and asked for the co-operation of the members in maintaining the excel- lence which the club had attained.—The election of cap- tain was the next item, and on the motion of Mr. C. H. Unsworth, seconded by Mr. C. H. Spinks, the name of Mr. S. M. Crosfield was unanimously adopted.—Mr. S. M. Crosfield explained that he would not be able to offi- ciate for the whole of the season, for after the Ist June be would be leaving England and not returning until the middle of August. A deputy captain was appointed,Mr. J. J. Crosfield being unanimously selected.—The meet- ing then proceeded to the election of the various cap- tains and committees, and the following were approved: Captain, cricket, second team Mr. C. R. Adamson ; captain, bowling club. Mr. T. Morris ; secretary. Mr. G. Pilling ; tennis secretary, Mr. C. Anderson ; auditor,Mr. D. Tinnion. Committee :—Cricket, Messrs. E. Eng- land, C. Adamson S. M. Crosfield, L. A. Voisey. Bowl- ing: Messrs. R. Adamson, C. Leigh, W. Richardson, and T. Cooper. Football: Messrs. J. Dunn, J. Hannah, R. Silcock. Tennis: Mr. Foster Jackson. Votes of thanks were unanimously passed for past services to the late president, the hon. treasurer and the hon. secretary,and the Chairman acknowledging a compliment proposed by Mr. W. H. J. Jenkins and seconded by Mr. Byerley, the meeting terminated.—The new professionals for the en- suing season are A. Thewlis late of Lascelles Hall C.C:, and G. Scrimshaw, of Manchester C.C.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1896-03-28_WidnesExaminer_art0064" n="art0064">
        <head>OUR LOCAL CHURCHES-WARRINGTON</head>
        <p>OUR LOCAL CHURCHES-WARRINGTON</p>
        <p>ST. PAUL'S CHURCH.</p>
        <p>A Lenten oratorio service was held at this church on Sunday. when there was a numerous congregation. A. G. Gaul's "Passion music" was rendered by the choris- ters, who were assisted by Mr. G. Green, tenor, and Mr. J. B. Hudson, baritone,of the Liverpool Cathedral choir. The offertory was in aid of the choir fund.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1896-03-28_WidnesExaminer_art0065" n="art0065">
        <head>CAIRO-STREET CHAPEL.</head>
        <p>CAIRO-STREET CHAPEL.</p>
        <p>On Sunday the pulpit of this chapel was occupied by the Rev. W. H. Drummond, who in the morning preached the first of the two sermons en " The Apocry- pha— its place in religious literature." He briefly re- ferred to the various opinions held with reference to the books comprised in the Apocrypha, their authorship and value and sacred writings. He divided them into two classes—historical and religious, and dealt that morn- ing with the historical portion. In the evening the rev. gentleman spoke upon" Sir Thomas More's Utopia—s dream of Christian Socialism" There were good con- gregations and the pastor's discourses were attentively listened to.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1896-03-28_WidnesExaminer_art0066" n="art0066">
        <head>WOLVES IN RUSSIA.</head>
        <p>WOLVES IN RUSSIA.</p>
        <p>VILLAGES IN A STATE OF SIEGE.</p>
        <p>Information has been received in St. Petersburg from Kieff that an unusual number of wolves have recently made their appearance in that government. It is now many years sines there was an " oblagat" or general hunt after these voracious creatures,and, in the interim, they have increased prodigiously. In the northern parts of the government it is asserted that the wolves are so numerous that whole villages are in a state of siegt, in consequence, the inhabitants sometimes not venturing to go out of doors.</p>
