      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1896-03-28_WidnesExaminer_art0107" n="art0107">
        <p>" • •-• . •</p>
        <p>DOTTGALES PATENT SELFRNISING FLOUR m'EsBITSTE PASTRY. THE EXAMINER SATURPAY, MARCH 28, 1896.</p>
        <p>eptitgt</p>
        <p>it is perhaps due to.this fact that he had little to say in explanation of what the Government has done or in anticipation of what it intends to do. His llusions to the Soudan empedition were singlarly vague. He,like the rest of his party,seems to find it impossible to invent a plausible pretext for this lastest outcome of the forward policy. If any inferences may be drawn from the utterances of this member of the Government the expedition is neither intended to reconquer the Soudan nor to strengthen the Egyptian frontiers, but simply to uphold European prestige in the minds of the dervishes, this prestige having been sadly dimin- ished by the defeat of the Italians.</p>
        <p>UNLESS the statements made by Animal Training. Mr. S. J. BENSUSAN in the "English Illustrated Magazine," be promptly and conclusively contradicted, humane people will not in the future find much pleasure in witnessing the performances of trained animals. There is a fairly widespread belief that animals are trained more by kindness than by severity but this, it is now affirmed by the writer referred to, is an amiable delusion. Not merely severity, but cruelty of a diabolical description is regularly practised in the "breaking in," so to speak, of dumb creatures. The foreigners are, it seems, the most inhumane trainers, but then, says Mr. BENSUSAN, "the average foreigner has no soft ' place in his heart for the brute creation." But ' even in England there are some ghastly " rehearsals " to be witnessed in under- ground cellars and other quiet places, for the most part carried out by the imported foreign trainers. The weekly trainings which are necessary to keep the poor animals up to the necessary standard take:pla2e"with an accom- paniment of suffering that would shocks slaugh- terman." Of all trained animals the dog is most cruelly tortured. This is because dogs are chr ip and can be easily obtained. In the case of such creatures as monkeys and elephants the cruelty of the trainer is restrained by the knowledge that his victims soon die under ill-treatment and that it may cost a round sum to replace them. Mr. BENSITSAN tells of an instance in which a foreign trainer was soundly thrashed: by some workmen engaged at a place of entertainment in London, and when the manager demanded an explanation of this conduct the workmen handed him a dog collar. "The manager took up the big broad collar; it was full of sharp jagged nails. He went up to the dogs, and they shrank from him howling. Their necks were scarred and bleeding." A visit to a dog-trainer's home is described in the following words :—" The practice-room was hung with spiked collars, whips, and other things, of all sizes and shapes. He had lost two of his dogs, and had s.nt for two more from the Dogs' Home, and was trying to teach one when I went in. He had it in a spiked collar, and the poor beast was too frightened and too stupid to do what he wanted. He was in a rare rage, he lashed and dragged and jerked, the dog got more and 1' more terrified, until at last his temper got the complete mastery over him, and he strangled the poor beast." Several in- stances of distressing cruelty are quoted by the writer, but even then he maintains that he has not described the worst. He has purposely avoided the more sensational details, and omitted several that would give positive offence. "I have been told," he adds, "by some paople living within hearing of the cellars where some performing animals are constantly kept that Sundays are usually chosen for rehearsals, and that the entire day is made hideous by the sound of howling and wailing." If all these statements be true there is evidently a wide field for the intervention of the Society for the Prevention of Cruelty to Animals, and it is quite certain that the public will support that Society or any agency which undertakes to suppress the vicious and cruel trainer.</p>
        <p>The only result of the opposition of the London and North-Western Railway Company to the War- rington Extension Bill is the piling up for them- selves and for the Corporation of larger bills for law expenses. It did not take the Committee of the House of Commons long to decide on which side their verdict should be cast, and the Corporation has, as a matter of fact, got all it asked for. In- deed, the case presented for the extension was practieally, unanswerable. By judicious diplomacy all the local authorities which might possibly have opposed the extension had been conciliated— always, of course, excepting the railway company. For this result the members of the Corporation who have been most directly concerned with the pro- motion of the Bill and the Town Clerk may fairly be congratulated.</p>
        <p>The" new photography" is developing with fear- some rapidity. Just as people had begin to recover rom tie sensation caused by the announcement that with the aid of certain photographic apparatu and certain mysterious " X" rays the bones of the body could be photographed through clothes and flesh, Mr. Edison comes forward and declares that he has discovered a method by which the naked eye can look through both bones and flesh. It is said that after testing no fewer than eighteen hundred different materials Mr. Edison has found a substance which so increases the power of human vision that a person may see through a piece of wood eight. inches in thickness. At this rate the " new photo- grapher" will soon be a terror to all the decent inhabitants of the locality in which he resides.</p>
        <p>Warrington loses a very useful and a much-re- spected townsman through the death of Mr. S. J. Beaman. When he was a member lof the rown Council, Mr. Beaman undoubtedly did a good deal of valuable work for the town. He was particu- larly interested in the management of Longford Depot and he took a leading part in the re-organisa- tion of that branch of the sanitary work of the town. It is now generally admitted that the Long- ford department is in a vastly more efficient con- dition than it used to be, and. it will be just as generally admitted that the town is largely in- debted for this excellent result to Mr. Beaman's shrewd common-sense, technical knowledge and determined adherence to his own opinions.</p>
        <p>RUNCORN COUNTY COURT.</p>
        <p>A sitting of this Court Was held on Tuesday before His Honour Judge Wynne Ffoulkes. The business comprised 54 ordinary summonses, 5 default summonses, 2 adjourned actions, and 11 judgment summonses.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1896-03-28_WidnesExaminer_art0108" n="art0108">
        <p>TO CYCLISTS. TWO GOOD LINES IN CYCLES :-</p>
        <p>REFORNI " COVENTRY High-Grade Mount, weight 271bs, with best detachable Tyres, £lO 17s. 6d. " SPECIAL" LADIES, Best "detachable Tyres, weight 301bs. £lO-10s.</p>
        <p>J. 1.7. GARLAND, BRIDGE-STREET, WARRINGTON. Large Stock of Premiers, Excelsiors, and New Rapids. Several second hand Machines, cheap.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1896-03-28_WidnesExaminer_art0109" n="art0109">
        <head>POLICE CHANGE.</head>
        <p>POLICE CHANGE.</p>
        <p>P.C. Wright, fer many years a constable in Runcorn has been removed to Saughall, and his place at Runcorn has been taken by P.C. Nicholls from that place.</p>
        <p>BILLIARDS.-RIINCOEIN Y. FRODSHAM.</p>
        <p>A match between the members of the Runcorn Volun- teers Social Club and the Frodsham Conservative Club was played off at Runcorn on Monday night. The Run. corn team proved to be superior to their opponents, winning six out of the seven games played. The scores were :—Runcorn, 1028 points ; Frodsham,B2s ; majority for Runcorn 203. ACCIDENT TO A RUNCORN WORKMAN.</p>
        <p>Thomas Johnson, employed at the Old Quay Works of the United Alkali Company at Runcorn, and residing at the Stonehills, met with a serious accident whilst follow- ing his employment on Tuesday. He was engaged in rearing a wall of bricketts which are now made at these works from " blue billy," when a number of them fell upon him, and he received serious injuries to his back. He was conveyed home, and Dr. McDougall has since attended him.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1896-03-28_WidnesExaminer_art0110" n="art0110">
        <head>RUNCORN SCHOOL BOARD.</head>
        <p>RUNCORN SCHOOL BOARD.</p>
        <p>A special meeting of this Board was held at the Town Hall,Runcorn,on fuesday evening. Mr. James Pritchard occupied the chair, and there were also present—The Rev. Dr. Gastaldi, Messrs. S. Taylor, J.P., C.C., F. J. Norman,J.P., E. Imison, J.P.,and G. Moss (clerk). Miss Collier, of Flint,was appointed as assistant to Miss Robb at Mill Brow Infant School, at a salary of £4O per an- num. Cheques for the teacher? salaries were after- wards signed.—This concluded the business. MARINERS' CHURCH, RUNCORN.</p>
        <p>Mr. Gibson presided at the weekly entertainment in this church on Monday evening. The programme included songs by Miss A. Turner, Miss Temple, Miss M. Hill, Messrs. Stanbie, G. Finney, Bazley and B. Hill. A duet was also given by Messrs. Stanbie and Knowles. Selections on the piano were played by Mr. Full and Mr. Price. A mouth organ and piano duet was contributed by Mr. Full and Mr. G. Finney, and a musical duet (con- certina and organ) was performed by Mr. P. Cartwright and Miss A. Hill. Mr. J. Gibson gave a cornet solo,and Miss E. Turner a recitatioa. The accomp %nista were Mr. Gardener and Mr. Full. The proceeds were in aid of the choir.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1896-03-28_WidnesExaminer_art0111" n="art0111">
        <head>MEETING OE BELLI:UNWIRE AT RUNCORN.</head>
        <p>MEETING OE BELLI:UNWIRE AT RUNCORN.</p>
        <p>The annual gathering of the Runcorn branch of the Chester Guild of Bellringers was held on Saturday. In the afternoon six score touthes of grandsire triples were rung on the Parish Church bells by the members from Runcorn and Frodsharn. After this a substantial tea was partaken of at the residence of Mr. Stubbs, and at the subsequent gathering,under the presidency of the Rev. H. B. Firth, the following appointments were made :—The Vicar. the Rev. Canon Wood, chairman of the branch ; Mr. P. Trevitt, secretary and treasurer ; Messrs. Poster (Frodsham), and H. Clare (Runcorn), re- presentative of the parent guild. Votes of thanks were accorded to Mr. Stubbe, to the Vicar for the use of the bell, and to the Rev. H. B. Firth for presiding.</p>
        <p>BILLIARDS AT THE LIBERAL CLUE.</p>
        <p>At the Liberal Club, Roncorn, on Wednesday evening, the members had as their visitors a team from the Northwich Liberal Club to engage in a match at billiards. Prior to the engagement a tea was partaken of at Mr. James Lea's Temperance Hotel, in High-street. Great interest was manifested in the match,and as will be seen from the undermentioned scores, the home team won by 131 points. Score</p>
        <p>RUNCORN'. NORTIIWICEC. R Owen — 62 S Harzreaves</p>
        <p>_ W Brown l5O 3 W Hughes I-1 C Barns J Green l5O D Bebblngton lO7 W Wright l5O C Harri.on 3 Elliot 125 E" ri filth s R. Green 139 3 Shaw l5O W J Carter l5O W Rogerson lll T Riley l5O E Eastwood T Lea J Crowley T Yates l5O W Danlon 132</p>
        <p>Total 1162</p>
        <p>Total 1531</p>
        <p>DISPUTE AS TO DISEASED PIGS AT RUNCORN.</p>
        <p>At Runcorn County Court, on Tuesday, his Honour Judge Wynne Ffoulkes was engaged in hearing an action taken by William Clutton, 40, Norton-street, for the re- covery of £ll7s. 6d. on the purchase of four pigs from John Eloise, Runcorn.—Mr. Burton,solititor, represented the defendant.—Annie Goban, Norton-street, a relative of the plaintiff's stated that in February last she went to Mr. Hulse's pig cote with the object of buying pigs. There were eight in the cote, and she bought four of them for which she paid £4 128. The defendant de- livered them at the plaintiff's premises on Februars 13th. She supplied them wtih meat but they would rot eat any, and she told Mr. Hulse about it on the following day. As the pigs became worse she again went to him, and he changed one of the pigs. On Saturday she told him that if he did not tell the police of the condition of the pigs she would do so. He then reported the pigs. Sergeant Lee afterwards came to look at them, and on the order of inspectors of the Board of Agriculture three of the four animals were destroyed. The other one died. The plaintiff received £2 175.61. from the Government as compensation.—ln cross-examination, witness admitted that she selected the pigs she bought for the plaintiff.— Frances Goban statee that the defendant told her that the pigs would get all right again.—Sergeant Lee stated that on the 15th February, the defendant came to the Police Station and said that his pigs were a little unwell. Witness went to the defendant's yard,and after examin- ing the four pigs, told him that they were suffering from swine Lever, and he would have to report the matter to the Board of Agricultare. The defendant told him that he had sold four others. He only bought eight altogether He stated that he bought five from Mr. Riley, of Taryin, and three from Mr. Reece, of Weston Point. From en- quiries made witness found that the defendant bought four from the latter place, and on telling the defendant so, he admitted that he had slaughtered one and buried it. The carcase was exhumed a- d on a past-mortem examination being made, it was found that the animal had died from swine fever. The pigs purchased by Mrs. Goban for the plaintiff were also suffering from the same disease. The defendant was fined 5. and costs by the magistrates for failirg to report swine fever. Witness did not think the defendant really understood pigs. It was the first dealing he had had with them. His premises were now an infected place.—Mr. Burton for the defence, submitted that there was no case for the plaintiff as the defendant did not give a warranty when the pigs were parchased.—His Honour thought there was an implied warranty as tt,.- ecz,re offered as marketable pigs.—Mr. Burton contended that there should have been an expressed warranty for the plain- tiff to uphold his claim.—His Honour said in every case of that kind, whatever was sold was supposod to be of warrantable quality.—Mr. Burton continuing, said that was the first transaction ot the defendant with pigs. Be purchased nine pigs and received no warranty with them. His client had no knowledge of the pigs' sickness and sold them as he received them. He submitted that it lay upon the plaintiff to prove that a warranty was given.—His Honour said his view was that there was an implied warranty when the goods sold were marketable. The only question was, if his view was correct, whether it applied to a private venture or to a person regularly dealing in it. He was a little doebtful whether it would apply to the defendant who was not a pig dealer. The law distinguished between an innocent dealer, and a person who was a regular dealer. He, therefore, thought the safer course would be to non-suit the plaintiff, and he could take further proceedings if he could mak out a better case. He should not allow any costs.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1896-03-28_WidnesExaminer_art0112" n="art0112">
        <head>TRE URBAN COUNCIL ELECTIONS AT RUNCORN.</head>
        <p>TRE URBAN COUNCIL ELECTIONS AT RUNCORN.</p>
        <p>At four o'clock ma Friday afternoon, the time for can- didates to withdraw from the Urban District Council of Runcorn expired, and it was then found that contests would take place in three wards. The Rev. Dr. Gastaldi has been allowed a "walk over" in Central Ward, the other nominated candidate (Mr. J. Pritchard, watch- maker, High-st.), having retired. The retiring council- lors for Heath and Newtown Wards, Messrs. James Pritchard and John Jones, have also been re-elected without opposition. In the remaining three wards, two candidates will go to the poll to-morrow (Saturday) in each ward. They are as follows :</p>
        <p>BRIDGEWATER WARD.—Mr. William Handley, and Dr McKer zie.</p>
        <p>MERSEY WARD.—Mr. C. Dutton, and Mr. C. Horse- field.</p>
        <p>HALTON WD.-Mr. F. Wiswall, and Mr. E. Gatley.</p>
        <p>During the week the candidates have been busy can- vassing, and have set forth their views on local affairs in their addresses. Sev•ral will he found in our adver- tising' columns. The polling will commence at twelve noon to-day (Saturday), and will continue until eight o'clock. The ballot boxes will then be conveyed tr.) the Town Hall where the votes will be counted, and the result declared about nine o'clock. Mr. F. A. Lake, the returning officer, has arranged for the polling to take place at the following schools: Bridgew iter Ward, at the Parish Church schools • Mersey Ward, Holy Trinity schools ; Halton Ward, 'Mill Brow Board klnfant) school.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1896-03-28_WidnesExaminer_art0113" n="art0113">
        <head>HALTON PARISH COUNCIL.</head>
        <p>HALTON PARISH COUNCIL.</p>
        <p>The last meeting of the first elected council of Haltonf was held in the girls' schoolroom, on Tuesday evening. The Rev. G. D. Wray occupied the chair, and there were also present—Messrs. J. Astbury, Looker, Hughes, J. Stokes, McCullock,Jeacock, Birch,Barrew and J.Woodier (clerk). Mr. Charles E• Linaker, of Frodsham, writing with re- spect to the Clerk's letter referring to the dangerous footpath at Clifton, said he would take an early oppor- tunity of inspecting the place referred to. He was under the impression that the footpath was diverted by the Railway Company some years ago, and that there was some liability on their part to maintain a fence, but this he would enquire into.—The Chairman said that was more satisfactory. Messrs. Robert Davies and Company, solicitors, War- rington, wrote with reference to the pinfold at Halton, reminding the Council of their promise to write them respecting it after their last meeting. They would like to know whose the fence was, about which Mr. Evans complained,and if th^ Council did pull down the pinfold and leave a gap or opening.—The Chairman übmitted the report of the committee's interview with Mr. Evans with reference to this matter. He told them that he wanted a rough stone wall of some permanent descrir- tion, built on top of the old wall surrounding his (the Chairman's) garden. If it was about eight yards in length Mr. Evans would be satisfied with it. He thought it would be better if the Council did that as they did not want to come into collision with the Duchy of Lancaster if they could prevent it. —ln answer to a question as to who was the owner of the pinfold, the Chairman said it was a nice question o law to whom the pinfold belonged. After some further discussion, Mr. Hughes proposed that Mr. Ashley be asked for a tender to build the wall required by Mr. Evans.—Mr. Stokes seconded, and it was unanimously agreed to. A cordial vote of thanks to the Chairman brought the meeting to a close.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1896-03-28_WidnesExaminer_art0114" n="art0114">
        <head>PARISH MEETING.</head>
        <p>THE PARISH MEETING.</p>
        <p>The Parish meeting was afterwards held, pre- sided over by the Rev. G. D. Wray. The Clerk submitted the accounts of the parochial charities. The receipts amounted to £25 73. 4(3., and of this amount £22 Iss. id. was distributed to deserving poor people. After meeting other expenses connect-el with it, there was a balance in hand of 14s. 9d.—The Chairman said there was one parish account which ought to have been read at that meeting, and that was with regard to the Stockham Charity,which belonged to Stockham and Halton. They were not now in possession of it, but it would be read at the next meeting of the Council. The accounts of the overseers were next read for the half year ending September 29 The half year commenced with a balance in hand of £2 19s. 7d., and a rate at is. 4d. in the £ produced £3BO 14s. 11d. After meeting all exnenses there was a balance carried forward of £lO Os. I.W.—Mr. Faulkner asked what had been the expenditure of the Parish Council.—The Clerk said the general expenses of the Council for last year were £lB 33. 7d„ and for this year £4 153. 91. In addition the improvement at the Hang- ing Rock amounted to £l4 17s. 6d., and the footpath to Runcorn £3 lie. 3d. The improvements at the Ceme- tery had cost £23 93.. and the general expenses of the same £39 93. 7d, Thus exclusive of the Cemetery general expenses, the Council had spent £64 17s. ld. This did not amount to a 21. rate, and the Council was empowered to spend 3d.—Mr. Barrow said they must not overlook the fact that the part3h council had done away with the salary of a clerk for the Burial Board.— The -Chairman said that was so. He did not think the council could be accused of being extravagant.—Mr. , Stokes wished to know how much had been the call upon the overseers on the cemetery account ?—The Clerk said the receipts were £26 103. 3a., so that the general ex- penses had exceeded the insome, by about £l3. The account varied considerably. Some ..ears the cemetery paid its way.—This concluded the business.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1896-03-28_WidnesExaminer_art0115" n="art0115">
        <head>EXAMINER.</head>
        <p>THE EXAMINER.</p>
        <p>SATURDAY, MARCH 28th, 1898.</p>
        <p>THE speeches which were made at Tory Demon- the Unionist demonstratiots in strations. Warrington on Saturday were more remarkable for what was omitted from them than for anything they really contained. Tory speakers are, in fact, very seriously handicapped in address- ing town audiences at the present time. When, for instance, Mr. CONINGSBY DISRAELI proposed his resolution in favour of fostering the "cultiva- tion of wheat-growing areas" he said nothing about the methods which, it is now well under- stood, the Government intend to adopt in order to "foster" or "relieve" agriculture. He did not tell his audience of "Conservative workingmen" that the Tory idea of fostering agriculture was to secure the rent of the landlord by abolishing one-half er two-thirds of the rates on agricultural land and taxing town residents to make up the deficiency. Nor did Mr. DISRAELI tell his audience ithat another Tory way of relieving agriculture is by making meat dearer in all the large centres of population. He never even hinted at any such measure as the " Diseases of Animals Bill" which the MINISTER OF AGRICULTURE has brought into the House of Commons. This Bill is neither more nor less than an open attempt at protection for the benefit of a small class of agriculturists and at the expense of the meat-consuming public Under the plea of preventing the spread of disease ,it completely prohibits the importAtion of live cattle into this country and thus cuts off at one stroke a supply of cheap food upon which the working classes aro largely dependent. It is, under the circumstances, not surprising to find that even such a pronounced Tory as Mr. WMTELEY, member for Stockport, has been im- pelled to protest against this Bill. In Mr. WHITELEY'S opinion the Bill contains "nothing good or satisfactory, for its effects will be diame- trically opposed to the int Crests of the great mass of the population," but it is needless to say that Mr. DISRAELI'S speech at Warrington contained nothing so outspoken or candid as this on the agricultural policy of the Government. Mr. DISRAELI was speaking to a tow n audience and he was no doubt well aware that it was not by the promise of such measures as the one referred to that the Conservetives won the boroughs at the last election. Mr.RosEuT PlERromrr,the mem- ber for Warrington, would also . have been amply justified in dealing at greater length with his favourite subject of foreign polities and the "in- tegrity of the Empire." Mr. PIERPOINT is evi- dently of opinion that the GERMAN EMPEROR was not animated by friendly motives to this country when he sent his famous telegram to President, KRUGER, yet it would have been interesting to hear what he had to say about the kind of diplo- macy which has within the last two or three weeks brought this country into a position in which it is entirely dependent upon German good-will to save it from an extremely grave foreign crisis. That the country has been brought into this position is perfectly clear to anyone who has considered the possible results of the extraordinary decision to invade the Soudan. That movement in Egypt is viewed with the most intense dislike by the French Government. and in this matter the French have the complete support of the Russians. In order to neutralise the enormous influence of these two countries, Lord SALISBURY has, it is under- stood, received the approval and support of the members of the Triple Alliance. But then, Ger- many is the dictator of the Triple Alliance, and, therefore, it is upon Germany more than upon any other country that we now rely to see us through a perilous land unnecessary enterprise. This is surely a development of Tory foreign policy which was worthy of Mr. PIERPOINT'S highest flights of eloquence. We do not expect the member for Warrington to condescend to deal with domestie topics except in the most cursory fashion, but it is disappointing to find him touch so lightly upon even foreign affairs. T.lere is no compensation to be had for these dis- appointments from a perusal of Lord ASHBOURNE'S speech. Lord ASIIBOURNE is a member of the Cabinet but he is not one of the "inner ring," and</p>
        <p>THE LATE MR. S. J. BEAMAN.</p>
        <p>- At the Bewsey-street Wesleyan Church on Sun- day morning, the Rev. T. Rippon, of Lytham, formerly of the Warrington circuit was the preacher, and at the close of the service, he alluded to the death of Mr. S. J. Beaman, with whom he said he had lived on very intimate terms when he was stationed in Warrington. Mr. Beaman was a man who was respected by everyone at the Bewsey-road Wesleyan Church,and his death was a great loss. After the pronouncing of the benedic- tion, the organist, Mr. B. Wadsworth, played the Dead March in Saul, the congregation standing.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1896-03-28_WidnesExaminer_art0116" n="art0116">
        <p>RUNCORN AND 'W iDNES.</p>
        <p>RUNCORN WOMEN'S TEMPERS NCE ASSOCIATION.</p>
        <p>Another of the series of entertainments, given under the auspices of the above association, took place on Sat- urday evening, in the Brunner Guild Hall. The chair was occupied by Mrs. Harrop, and there was a good attendance. Songs were given by Misses Carter,Bazley, Hill and Dyer; Messrs. Bazley,Millington and Harrison ; recitations by Mrs. Harrop,Miss Watson and Mr. Littler. Miss Baker was the accompanist. An enjoyable time was spent. _</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1896-03-28_WidnesExaminer_art0117" n="art0117">
        <head>RIINCORN POLICE. COURT.</head>
        <p>RIINCORN POLICE. COURT.</p>
        <p>At this court on Wednesday morning, Mary Ann Atherton, Pritchard's-row, Weston, was brought up un- der a warrant charged with being drunk and disorderly in Heath-road, Weston, on the 14th inst. The prisoner stated that she was too ill to appear in answer to the summons on Monday. but P.C. Morris who proved the case, stated that he saw her drunk on that day.—A fine of is. and 14s. costs was imposed. THE ALLEGI,D THEFT FROM A RUNCORN TRADESMAN. At Runcorn Police Court on Friday morning, Mark Smith, a tramp from Liverpool, was again brought before Mr. E. Imison, on a charge of stealing a pair of sea boots and an overcoat, the property of Arnold Unsworth, Percival-lane, Runcorn, on the 11th March.— Matilda Cooper, keeper of a lodging-house at Frodsham Bridge, stated that the prisoner came to her house wear- ing the coat and boots produced. She asked him where he had got them from, and he repliei that a friend had given them to hirn.—Prisoner was committed for trial at the Quarter Seasons to be held in April. CONFIRMATION SERVICE AT RUNCORN.</p>
        <p>The Lord Bishop of Chester conducted a confirmation service at Runcorn Parish Church on Friday evening. I here was a large congregation. The number of can- didates numbered about the same as last year. From the parish of Al! Saints which includes Weston and St. Michael's churches, there were 110 candidates, from Holy Trinity 20, and Weston Point 12. The Bishop was assisted by the Rev. H. B. Firth, in charge of the pas- toral staff, Revs. W. H. Stables. H. C. Moyle.W. Scholes, Dr. Preston (Holy Trinity) and the Rey. D. S. Bowen (Weston Point). For NAMR BROOCHES, soli silverd name broOches 100, (any name) Government stamped. Presents or Articles for yourself, you may visit DEBERT'S, 10, CHURCH-STREBT, LIVBRPOOL, where yea chn get hundreds of articles such as Watches, Clocks, Jewellery, Electro-Plate, Cutlery. Purses, Pocket Knives, etc., at wholesale prices. See advertisement (Illustrated Chron- graph Watch, 6s. 11d., etc.), on page 7 f this Paper. -Advt BRUNSWICK WRaLRYAN BAND OB ROPE, RUNCORN.</p>
        <p>A public meeting and entertainment in connection with this Society was held in Brunswick schoolroom on Monday evening,and was well attended. Mr. Peter Leath- wood occupied the chair,and gave an interesting address. Thn programme was of a miscellaneous description. A couple of glees were well rendered by the choir,and songs were contributed by Miss Nellie Temperley, Miss Grace Deakin, Miss N. McDonald, and Miss M. E. Leathwood. A number of recitations were given, the reciters being Miss L. Millington, Masters T. Hindley, F.J. Kirkham,J. C. Brimelow, W. Brown, W.Burrows, J. Parry,W.Brime- low, and 0. Atherton. An interesting item was a dialo- gue entitled "Six little nurses" by a number of girls. The Rev. T. Brackenbury delivered a most encouraging address. Mr. J. D. Bisbrown was the accompanist. SAKMEN'S INSTITUTE, RI7NCORN.</p>
        <p>A very successful entertainment was provided in this institute on Tuesday evening and was largely attended. The proceedinge were presided over by Mr. Byfleld. An excellent programme of vocal and instrumental music was submitted. Solos were well sung by Miss Jones, Messrs. G. Finney, J. Price, J. Moores, T. Woods, and H. Darlington. The two latter vocalists also delighted the audience with a comic duet. The instrumental se- lections included pianoforte duets by Messrs. A. Price and T. Foster, and a pianoforte solo by Miss C. Shaw, The accompanists during the evening were Messrs. T. Foster and A. Price.</p>
        <p>ON" PETIM MACEIINZIE."</p>
        <p>An exceedingly interesting and able lecture was de- livered in St. Paul's Wesleyan Church on Wednesday evening 135 the Rev. Dinsdale T. Young, of Salford,Man- cheater. The subject of the lecture was "An apprecia- tion of Peter Mackenzie." The chair was occupied by Mr. Frith.and he was supported on the platform by the Revs. T. Brackenbury, W. George. C. De Putron Gild- don,and R. Benton (frodsham), Mr. Rawlinson, and Mr. F. J. Norman. The proceeds were in aid of the Halton- road Wesleyan Church trust funds,and there was a fair attendance. The lecturer having referred to the high character of the late Peter Mackenzie,proceeded to speak of his intellectual abilities and of his power as a preacher. •He described him as having been the most popular preacher in the Wesleyan Church. The lecture was lis- tened to with intense interest, the subject of the lecture having had many admirers at Rencorn. The lecturer's services were acknowledged on the motion of the Rev. W. George. BRUNSWICK WESLEYAN SUNDAY &amp;WOOL, RUNCORN.</p>
        <p>The 98th annual meeting of the above mentioned school was held on Monday evening. The chair was occupied by the Superintendent of the circuit, Rev. T. Bracken- bury, and there was a large attendance. The secretary's report stated that there were on the books 37 male and 31 female teachers; 232 male scholars, and 252 female scholars. The average attendance had been as follows: —Teachers male 25, female 15; scholars male 161, female 166. The treasurer's report was also read and was con- sidered very satisfactory. The election of officers was then proceeded with, with the following result :.--3uper- intendents, Messrs. J. Simpson, T. Simister, and E. Taylor; general secretary, Mr. F. J. Norman, J.P. ; treasurer, Mr. A. R. Norman ; school secretaries, Messrs. Unsworth, Birkett and Povah ; visitors, Messrs. G. H. Kirkham, Brown, Jones, and Nancollis. It was decided to bring before the attention of the scholars the advan- tages of the school sick club, which was in a sound,flnan eial condition. It was also decided to hold the anniver- sary in July,at which the Rev. H. B. May will preach.</p>
        <p>CONCIRBOATIONAL TEA MEETING AT -RUNCORN.</p>
        <p>In the Bethesda Lecture Hall on Wednesday evening, the annual tea and meeting in connection with Bethesda Congregational Church were held. A good number at down to tea, the trays being provided by the following: —Mesdames Forrester, Diggle, Walton, Ackerley, W. Lacey, T. Taylor, Hedley, T. Lunt, Heatheringten, J. Lunt, Morris, Brindley, Mundy, Handley, Cameron, Rhodes, Corf, Richardson, and Misses Richardson, Rodgers, Robb, Mr. and Misses Cringe'', Messrs. W. Handley, J. J. Speakman, T. Riley, Stanway, J. Jones, and W. H. Chadwick. The after proceedings were pro. sided over by the Rev. J. S. Simpson, who addressed a few words of welcome. A short address was delivered by the Rev. A. H. Sayers, of Frodsham. He urged all present to be sincere Christians, earnest workers in the church and loyal to their minister. An excellent programme was submitted. The first item was an auto-harp solo by Mr. C. C. Le Couteur, and Mr. John Parry followed with a song. Other vocalists were Miss Heatherington, Miss M. Heatherington' Miss M. Lacy, Miss Harrop, Mr. A. Shingler. Mr. W. Brad- bury, Mr. E. Worrell, and Mr. Gowanlock. All received hearty receptions, and several encores were demanded. The Bethesda Prize Glee Party gave a fine interpretation of the glee " Sleep, gentle lady," and in resporne to the loud applause of the audience they complied with an en- core. Mr. W. Lunt contributed a violin solo, and Miss A. Rogers recited. The accompanist was Mr. C. C. Le Couteur.—Votes of thanks concluded the proceedings which were of a most enjoyable character.</p>
        <p>PRESENTiT- lON TO MR. THOMAS COOKE, OF RUNCORN. An interesting feature of the proceedings in connec- tion with the quarterly meeting of the Runcorn branch of the Sons of Temperance, held in Greenway-road Sun- day School on Saturday evening was the presentation of a beautifully-illuminated address to Mr. Thomas Cooke, on the completion of his twenty years' service as secre- tary to the Society. Mr. Thomas J. Holt read the ad- dress which stated that Mr. Cooke had been secretary of the Society for twenty years, an earnest worker and loyal member of the Order.and concluded by expreesing good wishes for his future welfare.—ln the course of a short speech, Mr. D. Bisbrown said they were met toge- ther that night to do honour to Mr. Cooke,wheo occupied an unique position, having acted as their secretary for twenty years without a break. They could not speak too highly of his abilities. He had taken the deepest interest in the Society, and he (Mr.Bisbrown) thought that such services should receive suitable recognition.—Mr.Edward Key, in making some appreciative remarks, said Mr. Cooke had been an excellent secretary. He was courte- ous and never said an unkind word, atad had often been to the members a friend in need.—The Chairman (Mr. Heesom) in presenting Mr. Cooke with the address, hoped he would live long to continue as their secretary. —ln replying, Mr. Cooke thanked the members for the address. He stated that he owed his position in life to his ceonnection with the temper- ance cause. Proceeding to review the past history of the secretary he stated that when he joined it twenty years ago, there were 80 members, now there were 280. He said it was singular that the Chairman that evening was in the chair when he joined the order 20 years ago. A short musical programme was submitted; and greatly enjoyed. S's were interpreted by Mr. George Holt, Mr. Walter Dutton, Miss E. Bazlev, and Mr. W. Milling- ton. Mr. Charles Atherton and Mr. Thomas Cooke con- tributed humorous songs, and together they gave a humorous duet. A duet was also given by Mr. Moores and eon. The duties of accompanists were satisfac- torily fulfilled by Mr. William Cooke. During the even- ing refreshments and fruit were handed round by the committee. At the close of a pleasant evening's enter- tainment. a vote of thanks was passed to those who had assisted, on the motion of Mr. Bisbrown, seconded ty Mr. Thomas Whyte.</p>
        <p>4rwirrei MACHINES, Hand £2 18s. 6d, ireadle Ret HousEnotn LINENS, hemmed and marked free of -charge.— J. and W. Dirrrox, Sankey-street, Warrington.—Add</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1896-03-28_WidnesExaminer_art0118" n="art0118">
        <head>THINGS HEARD AND SEEN AT RUNCORN.</head>
        <p>THINGS HEARD AND SEEN AT RUNCORN.</p>
        <p>BY OUR RUNCORN CORRESPONDENT.</p>
        <p>On Saturday, the election of councillors for Bridgewater, Central, and Halton Wards takes place, and two candidates will go to the poll for each ward. Two of the candidates are retiring councillors, namely Dr. McKenzie and Mr. F. Wis- wall. The first named is being opposed by Mr. William Handley, well-known throughout the town, and one who formerly did good work on the Board of Commissioners, and in Halton Ward. Mr. E. Oatley, secretary of the Watermen's Association, is opposing the return of Mr. Wiswall, engineer of the Bridgewater Undertaking. A vacancy occurs in Mersey Ward owing to the retirement of Mr. R. H. Posnett, through pressure of business, and Mr. Horsefleld, coal dealer and carrier, and Mr. Dutton, of Wilson's Hotel, are both striving hard to secure the seat. Very little interest seems to be mani- fested in the election, and the present deadlock in trade at Runcorn may partly account for this. The candidates themselves are confining their efforts chiefly to canvassing their wards.</p>
        <p>There seemed to be a hopeful ring about the meeting of directors and shareholders of the United Alkali Company en Friday last. The dividend paid by the company cannot be said to be large, but it is something to be wondered at, that there is any dividend at all,when the dead-weight of their num- erous disused works is taken into consideration. Several remarks, interesting to those residing in Runcorn and district, were made at the meeting. The Chairman of the company (Mr. Brock) poked fun at the suggession of the Salt Union to commence chemical works on their own account. He remarked that the Chairman of the Salt Union had spoken with great lightness and confidence, &amp;lout their geing into the chemical trade, and said " We could start with the newest and most improved plant,and we should be free from the burden of any old-fash- ioned and disused chemical works." Mr. Brock stated that he might start without " the burden of old-f ashioned and disused chemical works," but he would certainly end with the " burden " of many old and disused salt works.—Whether this will be any deterrent to the Salt Union, who, it is anticip- ated, will choose Weston Point for the site of their chemical works if they embark upon the project, remains to be seen.</p>
        <p>Some remarks were also made at the same meet- ing with regard to the Cashmere soap manufactured by the Company at Runcorn. The packing of this commodity was criticised in a humorous vein by a shareholder. He said it was placed upon the market in a shocking bad manner. He thought those who had charge of it did not know any more about putting soap before the public than a duck did about an umbrella. (Loud laughter.) He had been to some shops and asked for the soap, but he thought he might as well have asked the grocers for their eye-teeth. (Renewed laughter.)—Now Runcorn, this will never, never do</p>
        <p>The conference between representatives of the Ship Canal Company and members of the Runcorn Council on ThurQday afternoon last week was of a friendly character, and there seems to be a disposi- tion on the part of the Company to meet the wishes of the Council in a fair and straight forward manner. The complaints with respect to the improvements which the Company have effected at Runcorn can- not be said to be of any great enormity, but these little matters if unattended to may bring the newly provided facilities into disrepute, and make them of little benefit to the neighbourhood. Many resi- dents in Runcorn will hope that the sanguine ex- pectations of Mr. Bythell with respect to the future development of shipping in Runcorn and district, may speedily be realised. Although the shipping trade in Runcorn has not deteriorated in the same manner as the chemical trade, there is still room for improvement, and with fair dues and tolls, Puncorn may yet be expected to reap some benefit from the Ship Canal.</p>
        <p>lhe Widnes School Board—or at least some per- sons connected with it—seem to view with an un- friendly eye the local branch of the National Union of Teachers, if one must jodge from their recent ac- tion with regard to the hire of a schoolroom.Of rate the Union has received a considerable number of Widnes recruits,and the members require a meeting place. In Runcorn the School Board place a room at the disposal of the members free of charge,but in Widnes they are asked to pay, and pretty smartly too, for this privilege. The members consider the price asked for the hire of a room exorbitant, and have appealed to the Chairman of the Board. M the work of the Association is for the most part educational, it is to be hoped that the little friction which has arisen may be amicably settled.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1896-03-28_WidnesExaminer_art0119" n="art0119">
        <head>---,,-. SHIP CANAL.</head>
        <p>---,,-. SHIP CANAL.</p>
        <p>MANCHESTER PORT SANITARY AUTHORITY.</p>
        <p>A conference was held on Wednesday of the denuta tigns from the Runcorn Rural and Urban District Coun- cils who recently attended the inquiry at Manchester- with reference to the proposed order for the establish- n3ent of a Port Sanitary Authority for Manchester and district. Atter some discussion it was decided to recom- mend the two respective Councils to petition against the order if made in its present form. The Runcorn bodies desire to be allowed to remain as they were be- fore the making of the Shin Canal, and if they are compelled to contribute to the establishment and maintenance of the proposed authority they ask that the other districts interested should be called upon to pay a share of the expense.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1896-03-28_WidnesExaminer_art0120" n="art0120">
