        <p>WANTED General SERVANT ; no washing, good home; age 20 to 26.—Apply 590, ASHTON OLD-ROAD, Open- shaw, Manchester.</p>
        <p>11 ARK Dorking EGGS, from fine stock, Muzeen's strai.,„ 2s. 6d. doz., carefully packed.—Miss LANCIDALIT, Leconfield, Beverley. Yorks.</p>
        <p>EGGS for sitting from Splendid exhibition birds, Lang- shans,Laycocks direct Silver Wyandotte. Heaths noted strain. Black Minorcas, Mobridge and Garnets strain direct 4s. sitting, unfertile replaced.—THomls FELL, wadi.) wl, game. and poultry breeder, Longton, near Preston,</p>
        <p>HOME WOIIIC, Plain Writers. Book Agency, by Corres- pondents, Coming Addresses, ss. to 255. Weekly. Par- ticulars 2d., and Stamped a Idressed envelope own writing.— GROVES, 31, Pa'ace Terrace, Fulham, London.</p>
        <p>TO LET. HOUSE and SHOP, together with bakehouse. Stable &amp;c. Situated opposite Ram's Head, Haydock.— Apply THOMAS HARRISON.</p>
        <p>WANTED at once respectable GIRL, about 16, to assist in housework. Must have reference and be accustomed to children. Protestant preferred ; two in family ; wages 3s. week.-27. BRYANSTON-ROAD. St. Michael's, Liverpool.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1896-03-28_WidnesExaminer_art0122" n="art0122">
        <head>SPECIAL MEETING OF THE RUNCORN</head>
        <p>SPECIAL MEETING OF THE RUNCORN</p>
        <p>URBAN COUNCIL.</p>
        <p>Mr. E. Imison, J.P., presided at a special meeting of the Runcorn Conncil ma Tuesday evening, held in thet Town Hall, Runcorn. There were also present : Rev. Dr. Gastaldi, gessrs. S. Taylor, J.P., C.C., H. J. Grice, F. Wiswall, J. Jones, W. Collier, R. H. Posnett, J. W. Lightburn. J. Pritchard, D. Bishrown. G. Hewitt, T. Dean. G. H. Kirkham, with Mr. F. A. Lake (law clerk), Mr. E. Marshall (secretary and accountant), and Mr. J. Wilding (surveyor).</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1896-03-28_WidnesExaminer_art0123" n="art0123">
        <head>OVERSEERSHIP.</head>
        <p>THE OVERSEERSHIP.</p>
        <p>The General Purposes Committee recommended that Mr. E. Imison and Mr. S. Taylor be the overseers of the poor of the parish of Runcorn for the ensuing year. Mr. Lightburn moved the confirmation of the minutes, and it was unanimously agreed to.—Mr. Imison thanked the members for their confidence in re-appointing him to a position , the duties of which he had great pleasure in performing. They had been before four auditors that day and they complimented the assistant overseer on the way in which he had performed his work. They also added that he had done well in getting in the mouey consider- in, the depressed times through which they were pass- ing. The wish was expressed that every assistant over- seer would keep books as Mr. Sutton did. Previously the overseers had had an opportunity of reporting to the vestry meeting but this year it had to he done to the Council. He thought it was due to an efficient officer to bear testimony to the splendid way in which his work was done. (Hear, hear.)—Mr. Taylor also expressed OS thanks for being appointed by the Council to the position. In the past they had endeavoured to do their duty fearlessly, without injuring anyone. He thought the chairman would bear him out in saying that there was no one in Rune ore' who had need to complain in that resnect. They went carefully through every assess- ment. He wished to confirm the remarks with reference to the assistant overseer. They bad reason to feel proud of having such an Official. He saved the overseers a great de,l of labour which otherwise they might have had if they had a I,•ss efficient officer.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1896-03-28_WidnesExaminer_art0124" n="art0124">
        <head>TECHNICAL INSTITUTE.</head>
        <p>THE TECHNICAL INSTITUTE.</p>
        <p>Mr. Wiswall moved the passing of the account of Mr. Bnwden, architect, for the balance due to him of £ll5 39., including £37 3s. for extra work in connection with the stone staircase at the Technical Institute. He c-insidered that Mr. Bnwden's charges had been very reasonable, and that he had done his work exceedingly well. They had had no extras on the building which was an unique fact.—Mr. Dean seconded.—Mr. Big— brown remarked that he understood Sir John Brunner was paying the expenses in connection with the stone staircase.—Mr. Wiswall explained that the account was for preparing plans and not for the execution of the work.—The Secretary stated that Sir• John Brunner paid £B7 for the work done.—The motion was carried.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1896-03-28_WidnesExaminer_art0125" n="art0125">
        <head>PORT SANITARY DEPUTATION.</head>
        <p>THE PORT SANITARY DEPUTATION.</p>
        <p>Mr. Wiswall moved the signing of a cheque for ES les. lid. the expanses of the deputation to Manchester re Port Sanitary Authority, and for goods and services rendered in connection with the Ship Canal Bill.—Mr. Pritchard seconded, and it was agreed to. The meeting then terminated.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1896-03-28_WidnesExaminer_art0126" n="art0126">
        <head>IMPERIAL PARLIAMENT. (Continued from Page 3.) THURSDAY.</head>
        <p>IMPERIAL PARLIAMENT. (Continued from Page 3.) THURSDAY.</p>
        <p>Mr. G. BALFOUR informed Mr. Macaleese, in the House of Commons on Thursday, that Mr. J. Ross, Q.C.,, had been appointed to the judicial bench in Ireland. Mr. BALFOUR informed Sir A. Rollit that the Govern- ment would appoint a committee to deal with the ques- tion of the current wage clause in the Government con- tracts.</p>
        <p>Mr. ALLEN asked whether any notification was made by Lord Cromer favourable to the advance to Dongola prior to the order for the present expedition. Mr. Cun- ZON replied that the expedition was decided upon by the Government in consultation with the military authorities in Egypt, who had expressed the opinion that a forward movement against the dervishes should be made. Asked as to the attitude of the Porte,Mr. CURZON said the Sultan had not protested against the expeditlen ; he had made inquiries as to its nature and objects, and explanations had been given.</p>
        <p>On the House voing inta Committee of Supply on the Naval Works Bill, the CHANCELLOR of the EXCHEQUER, in the course of the discussion, said he drew a broad distinction between the old sinking fund and the new. The new sinking fund was absolutely intact. The sum specifically allotted to the repayment of debt as the re- cult of this year would be £7;250,000 more than had ever been specifically allotted for such a purposa before. There never had been—at least in our time—so large a surplus as the probable surplus of the present year. It is arranged that Sir John Gorst. who was prevented from introducing the Education Bill last night, will in- troduce the measure on Tuesday aftern-on. The ARCHBISHOP of York introduced into the House of of Lords on Thursday a bill imposing further restric- tions on the sale of intoxicating liquors on Sundays. In London the houses are to be closed (should the Bill pass) from midnight on Saturday till one o'clock on Sunday, and from two o'clock on Sunday until five o'clock On Monday morning. In towns and districts outside Lon- don licensed houses are to he open for an hour on Sun- days. The privileges of residents and real travellers are safeguarded, and the Archbishop declared that he had no desire to cirprive the working man of his glass of beer on the only day in the week on which he could dine with his wife and children. After a rather lengthy debate the Archbishop of York did not press for a division, and the bill was practically dropped. Gen</p>
        <p>The &amp;tents General in London of the Australian. African, and Canadian Governments have requestea the home Government to recognise in Great Britain marri- nes with deciased wives' sisters which had been legally performed in the colonies.</p>
        <p>GOOD TF.MPLARY.</p>
        <p>The Earlestown Ebenezer Lodge held its weekly session in the Co-operative Hall on Tuesday ,ven- ing, Bro. A. Morris, C.T., presided. There was a good attendance of members and visitors. Ont. candidate was proposed and admitted to member- ship. All other business was left in the hands of Business Committee. Programme of the evening was "Topical Talks," "Beer--what is it ?" The fol- lowing taking part Bros. Woods, Taylor, Hatton, Dean, Hopkins, Macklin Parker, Cutler, Drew, and j Sisters Batton, Owens, Woods, ard Pall. Refresh- ments were handed round and enjoyed by all.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1896-03-28_WidnesExaminer_art0127" n="art0127">
        <head>GRAPHIC ACCOUNT OF A VISIT TO ONE</head>
        <p>GRAPHIC ACCOUNT OF A VISIT TO ONE</p>
        <p>OF MANCHESTER'S PALACES OF INDUSTRY.</p>
        <p>INTERESTING DETAILS.</p>
        <p>THB Great City of Manchester is undoubtedly the</p>
        <p>largest centre of commerce in Vie world. It is. next to Lon- don, the most important city in the British empire; its commercial enterprise is famous throughout civilisation, and has even penetrated to the darkest recesses of barbaric humaßity. Manchester is the centre of a population within 12 miles of her Town Hall as great as I.ondon, the whole of which is engaged in the cause of labour. Day by day the powerful and mighty engines of steam power are, under the dual control of the mech nic and operative. busily engaged In contributing to the successful trading of this marvellously active and industrious community. In my visit to this won- derful city I chanced to discover amongst the many gigantic and colossal hives of industry a well-known famous furnish; lug establishment,</p>
        <p>"THE PANTHEON."</p>
        <p>Suffice it to say that" The Pantheon" is indisputably the very largest Furnishing Establishment in the North of Eng- land exclusively engaged in Furnishing It is not a Drapery Establishment, but is a solid, compact, massive warehouse containing over a million square feet of Showrooms packed from the basement to the root by the Finest Stock of Furni- ture open for inspection outside the city of London. The des- tinies of this great establishmPnt, I find, are presided over by Mr. W. H. Worsley, the sole and absolute proprietor, to whose untiring energy and unflagging enterprise "The Pantheon "owes its high position. To visit "The Pantheon" I took the tram from the corner of Mosaley-street. Piccadilly, going Alexandra Park way. In 3 very tew minutes, after passing St. Peter's Church and the mammoth Central Sta- tion, I was informed by the guard of my arrival at" The Pantheon." My visit being in the evening, I was amazed at the brilliant display of electricity, the whole of the vast building having just been splendidly lighted by the Manches- ter Electric Company. under the able supervision of Mr. Crews, electrical consulting engineer, of Cross-street, I was enabled to see everythingas favourably as in daylight. " The Pantheon " has immense show windows fronting Gay- thorn over 100 feet long, forming a gigantic display. Noting the invitation displayed ou the windows, " Inspection Invited," without diffidence I immediately embraced the op- portunity by walking inside ; announced my desire to see 'The Pantheon," though not wish,ng to purchase. I was met in the most cordial manner by one of the numerous salesmen, who very courteousy offered to conduct me. I was at once struck with</p>
        <p>OVERWHELMING SURI?RISE,</p>
        <p>the extraordinary immen ,Ity of the stock being sufficient guarae.. of variety. On the ground floor I lound a well arranged stock of Bedroom Furniture of the better cla,s, suites innumerable at from £3O to £l5O. Magnificent speci- mens in richly-figured pollard oak, American walnut and burr, satinwood, exquisitely inlaid rosewood,Chippendale and Sheraton. These stood out prominently. Struck with the grandeur of one large ollard oak Bedroom Suite, I ventured to ask the price, expecting it to be quite £lO3 to £5OO, and was surprised to find the price only £120; another Suite, by Goodall, which must have cost nearly £203, pece only £6b. The cheaper bedroom suites I was intormed•were displayed in another showroom reserved for the purpose. A summary of the stock on view is briefly 200 bedroom suites complete, over 1,000 bedsteads. 150 drawing-room gni, es. 150 cabinets, 175 dining-room suites, and over 150 sideboards, besides the thou- sands of bargains in all the sundries which are essential to household furnishing. My curiosity was next aroused in a visit to the Carpet Showroom, which has been newly refitted. Here I revelled in exquisite artistic colourings</p>
        <p>DISSOLVING THE SOUL</p>
        <p>in a sense of loveliness. Carpets in all their gorgeous colours, Axminster, Persian, Brussels, Wilton, and Tapestry, at incomparably low prices. Axminster 2s 9d per yard. Brussels is lid, Tapestry from 9id. Over 500 Second-hand Carpets form a special plum for those requiring a bargain ; large Second-hand Axminster and Bru sells Carpets from 15s 6d to £3 10d, according to size and quality. Adjoining the Carpet Showroom is the Linoleum and Oilcloth Depart- ment, fitted with rollers for the proper display of the pat- terns. Linoleums from id to 2s id, Oilcloths from 6d to is 11d. Inlaid Linoleum! at 3s 11d. I was now conducted upstairs to the Dining-room Furniture Showroom. I was immediately impressed by the enormous display of Massive Solid Dining-room Furniture, the bulk of which is magnifi- cently carved in bas relief, reminding one of the artistic merit.displayed in THE ATHENIAN TEMPLES. Magnificent productions by splendid old makers, such as Gillows, Garnett, Lamb, Henry Ogden and Snits, &amp;c., at positively less prices than the common new London furniture now so largely retailed by drapers, &amp;c. For instance, I noted a magnificent ?ft 6in Sideboard, by Doveston, Bird, and Hull, whieh could not have cost less than £lOO, price £l6 guineas. What possible chance has new Londou furniture in competition with bargains such as here displayed? Next a splendid solid oak Sideboard, with brilliant crystal minors, for £l2 lOs ; original cost £75. You can positively obtain Sideboards, second hand,condition equal to new, from 30s to ' £lO, worth three or four times the amount; and, better still, you can depend upon the stability. A common new side- board is practically worthless ; eracks may develop suffi- ciently wide as to almost dis:lcse the contents to your visitors. I should strongly recommend the purchase of a good high-class sideboard, second hand, in preference to new. It doessit matter what price you wish to give,you can obtain a splendid bargain at Worsley's in Sideboards from 30s up to £lOO, all equally cheap and durable. The same rule applies to Diningroom Suites, Overmantels, and Dining Tables. The bargains in this department are simply wonderful. A fine, handsome Diningroom Suite, in morocco, with couch, two easy chairs, and six single chairs, second hand, but In good order cost £55 for £9 10.; this is a sample of what may be obtained. Of course there is ample varietAin price, suites from £2 10s to £65. A complete Suite in solid oak may be had for comparatively a trifling sum. My attention was called to a Solid Oak Dining-room Suite, consisting of couch, two easy chairs, and six single chairs, in morocco; a magni- ficent Sideboard, Overmantel, and Dining Table, the lot for £25, well worth £75, and cost £lOO. This seems almost in- credible. but a visit will amply demonstrate the verification. I now hear a brilliant display of rapid evolutionary chords on a pianoforte, and am thereby attracted to the New Pianoforte Saloon recently erected adjoining the Dining-room Show- room. I find that the proprietor is not only a remarkable energetic business man, but that he is endowed with no mean ability as a pianist, the performance of a Chopin Polonaise amply demonstrating this upon a magnificent Bmrd Piano- forte. The Pianoforte Saloon contains all the best known instruments by the highest class makers at a price from 20 to 30 per cent, less than music sellers' list prices. For instance, Kirkman's span brand new Upright Grand Cottage Pianos, with all the latest improvements, are offered at 284 guineas, a price hitherto unknown, A very fine Trichord new Piano- forte, specially made for Worsley's, is retailed at 131 guineas, .also something new as to price. There are a hundred instru- ments on view, but the gems of the bargains are the Second- hand Pianofortes. Here you can get pianos from 50s. to:£so, simply incredible value. Go and fee for yourself; it will cost you nothing.but may profit you much: American Organs equally cheap. The Music Saloon contains a number of CHARMING PICTURBS By well-known artists, at astonishing low prices. There are on this floor varieties of sundries, articles of virtu,omamental and saluable property of all kinds, but space forbids all I could write of what I saw. The next showroom is the Draw- ing-room, and it is almost impossible to do justice to the very beautiful and exquisite display of really High-class New and Second-hand Drawing-room Furniture. You can pur- chase a suite for £2 10s. and you can buy one as high as £l5O. There are 163 suites on view, for variety and beauty un- rivalled. There is only space to illustrate a sample bargain, viz.: A. magnificent Second-hand Drawing-room Suite. cabinet, overmantel, two tables, card table, and coal cabinet, the whole in Chippendale mahogany of Louis XIV. style, richly carved ; the suite has been newly re-upholstered in rich brocaded silk of delicate artistic colourings ; everything is equal to new. Price for the lot 211 guineas; worth quite £7O. Very pretty suites may be purchased at £7 to £lO. The next department is the Bedstead Showroom, where you are at once dazzled by the MOUNTAINS OF GOLD.</p>
        <p>The brilliant collection of solid Brass Bedsteads, having the appearance of a mountain of gold. Hundreds of exquisitely finished brass bedsteads at incredible low prices are on show from 323 6d up to £25. Two thousand bedsteads from 5s 6cl may be inspected, besides many wondeiful bargains in Second-hand Bedsteads For instance, a magnificent mas- sive solid brass bedstead, which lost £25 when new, is shown for £4 10s. I must now pass on to describe the enormous Bedroom Suite Showroom, which is the largest in Manchester, and contains 150 bedroom suites from 35s to £65. The suites are shown in corridors, with plenty of width aud space al- lowed for inspection. The great feature at "Tne Pantheon" Is that there is plenty of room, and a be illiant light enabling you to see what you are purchasing perfectly. In this depart- ment you have splendid opportunities for securing a bargain. I noticed one, a fine handsome majestic 6 feet Bedroom Suite, In richly figured wood, all solid; wardrobe 6 feet, dressing table 5 feet, washstand 4 feet 6 inches, three chairs, towel rail, and pedestal, in condition quite equal to new. This suite is fitted with every convenience, and cost from £7O to £BO. The prise is only 25/ guineas, the fact being that Worsley's must buy a tremendous quantity of goods awfully cheap, and simply ask a small profit on it, no matter how little the cot. I have seen bargains worth three and four times the money asked for them. The best plan to adopt to prove this is to take the trouble to pay a visit to " Th. Pantheon" and see for yourself, As I said before you are not asked to buy; you can walk in and walk out with perfect freedom, experiencing only</p>
        <p>riOURTESY AND POLITENESS. J To give a full account of the vast stores of " The Pantheon " would occupy the whole of this paper. I may, however, just mention one fact, and that is you cannot ask Woreley's for any article of furniture without being quickly shown what 'von require. You are never met with the response, "Very sorry. bnt we are just out of what you require, but we can get it for you." Worslevs keep a tremendous reserve s',ock. You can obtain anything you require in the furnishing line with difficulty. the Kitchen and Sitting-room furnishing departments are cram full of bargains ; for instance, second hand mahogany hair-seated Sofas or Couches from 15s, mahogany carved back Dressers from £3 ICs, mahogany Dining Tables from 15s. Kitchen Dressers from 13s, and so on. The show rooms for Hall Furniture, Library, and Office Furniture are equally cheap and good. A magnificient second-hand Hat and Umbrella St- nd and pair of Hall Chairs, which cost EN, may be purchased for £6 103, a smaller set ;or £2 15s IN CONCLUSION,</p>
        <p>I can only say that the greatest marvel of all Is that Mr. Worsiev has instituted a new , Gradual Payment System, which he inaugurated. Worsley s offer you all the advan- vantages of their low prices. Any of the targains I have described may be purchased at the prices advertised, and they will allow you to pay oft by Gradual Payments, making no difterence whatever in the invoke price. I cannot conclude my letter without recommendingeveryone to visit Woreley's before purchasing ; all his competitors try to imitate his style of business, but my experience teaches me that Worsley reigns supreme—Worsley is there, the rest are nowhere. Worsley's are open every day from 9 to 7, and " The Pantheon," being almost next door to the Central Station. Manchester, is very easy to find. The Alexandra Park, Junction. and Bradshaw-street trams all going City- road may pass thel door.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1896-03-28_WidnesExaminer_art0128" n="art0128">
        <head>MAKE LIFE ENJOYABLE.</head>
        <p>MAKE LIFE ENJOYABLE.</p>
        <p>Loss of health means loss of all which makes life en- joyable ; for, in addition to the bodily pain and suffering which it eausev, it entails a vast amount of mental pain and anxiety, especially in the homes of the working classes. It brings in its train a host of discomforts which retard the recovery of the sufferer, and hinder, instead of assisting, Nature to perform her functions. When a working man suffers from any ailment which prevents his following his daily avocation, he is natur- ally filled with anxiety regarding the maintenance of those depending on him for their daily sustenance. His very love for his family retards and hinders his speeds, recovery, and his spirits become depresf ed at the antici- pation of the glne:ny prospects before them in the evei., of a long continuance of his illness. It is well known that depression of spirits often proves a serious bar to a ' speedy recovery. These results arise in addition to the various incon- veniences and discomforts which the sufferer and his family experience as direct results of his illness, such as additional expenses for luxuries for the sick, with in- creased difficulty to obtain those luxuries, or even the necessities of life.</p>
        <p>Most of The various forms of disease which trouble the labouring classes of the country, and which are often attribut9ble, more or less, to some unhealthly condition of their daily work, may be classed under a lew leading heads.</p>
        <p>To avoid thcl discomforts arising from various forms of disease,every working man should seek the aid of</p>
        <p>THE VEGETABLE TONIC,</p>
        <p>Which has gained a world-wide renown as the most wonderful medicinal discovery of the age ; and as un- failing remedy it is prescribed by many leading physi- cians in various ailments.</p>
        <p>For Bronchitis, Asthma, Coughs, and Colds, Shortness of Breath, Neuralgia, General Weakness, Low Spirits, Nervousness, Indigestion, Influenza. GWILYM EVANS' QUININE BITTERS</p>
        <p>s Sold In Bottles at 2s. 91. and 4s. 6d. each. See the name Gwilym Evans on Label, Stamp and Bottle. Sole Proprietors : Quinine Bitters' Manufacturing Co., Ltd., Lianelly, South Wales.</p>
        <p>GSVILY3I EVAN'S QUININE BITTERS.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1896-03-28_WidnesExaminer_art0129" n="art0129">
        <head>BEFORE THE MAGISTRATES.</head>
        <p>BEFORE THE MAGISTRATES.</p>
        <p>CHILD NEGLECT.—DRASTIC PIINISHMF.NT FOR A FATTIER. On Monday morning, at the Warrington Borough Police Court, a wiredrawer named John Richardson, living at 26. Ellesmere-st., was charged with neglecting his eve children, aged fourteen, twelve, ten, seven, and three years respectively, on the 12;h inst. and divers other dates within the past six months.—lnspector McAteer, who prosecuted on behalf of the N.S.P.C.C., stated that in consequence of a complaint made to him by the wife of the defendant he visited the house on the night of the date named and found defendant drunk. His five children were in the house, and their condition was most emaciated. They were aso insufficiently clothed and very dirty. He (witness) had often cau- tioned the defendant during the past tw • years as to his conduct owards his wife and chi:dren. The defendant was nothing but a drunken wastrel. Many times wit- ness had seen him drunk, and had been called out of bed early in the rgorning by the wife, who had sought his (the Inspector's) protection from the violence of her hus- band during his drunken bouts. Inspector McAteer asked the Bench to deal severely with the defendant. If they sent him to prison for a time it would be a blessing to his wife, who was a hard-working woman, and who alone did something towards maintaining the five chil- dren. She earned 7s. per week, and out of that amount had to pay Is. for rent, thus having only 33. to support herself and children.—Charles Hadlon, defendant's father-in-law, also spoke to defendant's laziness and in- temperance.—lt was also mentioned that he had been up at the Court 10 times previously.—The magistrates characterized the case as a very bad one, and after severely censuring the defendant committed him to prison for three months w ith hard labour.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1896-03-28_WidnesExaminer_art0130" n="art0130">
        <head>MINOR cASES.</head>
        <p>MINOR cASES.</p>
        <p>WARRINGTON BOROUGH POLICE COURT.</p>
        <p>THURSDLY%—Before Co!onel Itbyway (chlirman), nd Mr</p>
        <p>S. Wright.</p>
        <p>THEFT By A BROTHER-IN-LAW.—William Charles Earle was charged with stealing on the 17th March, six boots value El 18s. 6d., the property of David Burgess, his master.—The Chief Constable stated that the prosecutor and prisoner were brorthers-in-law and the former had beed employed by the latter at his tcot repeiring shop in Academy-street. and on Monday he WAS left for a part of the day in charge of the shop. At 3 o'clock Mr. Burgess sal. the boots safe and on the following day, the 17th he missed them. In the mean- time the prisoner had absconded to Macclesfield. Prisoner had pledged boots at two pawnshops, and sold a pair at a shoplin Mersey-street. Enquiries inplacclesfield alarmed pri- soner who doubled back on his tracks and came to Warrington again. He was then arrested. When charged he said he had never done such a thing before in his life and wished to effect compeomise with prosecutor. The latter, properly, re- fused asit would be condoning a felony.—Prisoner was sent to gaol for 14 days' hard labour. DRUNK AND DISORDERLY.—John Brown, a militiaman, was fined 2s. 6d. and Is. costa for being drunk and disorderly in Brick-street at a quarter to 12 that morning. FRlDAY.—Before Alderman Harrison chairman), and Dr.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1896-03-28_WidnesExaminer_art0131" n="art0131">
        <head>Gornall.</head>
        <p>Gornall.</p>
        <p>CLOTHES-LINE THEFTS BY A GIRL OF 14,—Maud Smith, 8 Jolley's-yard, 14 years of age, was charged with stealing from a clothes-line in Hanover-st., at seven o'clock on Wednesday, two tablecloths value ss.,the property of Thomas Starkey; she was further charged with stealing from Arpley-st., on Thurs- day, a shoulder shawl value be., the property of Margaret Isherwood. —She pleaded guilty.—The Chief Constable stated that the girl was the daughter of a widow, and had been at a oose end for some time. She had been going about hawking watercress, sometimes begging, and had haunted the back yards of respectable people. On Wednesday evening Mrs. Starkey placed the tabelcloths upon the clothes-line in her yard. At 7.20 she missed them, and information was given to the pollee by the manager of the pawnbroker's shop at which the gooas were pawned. While they were searching for her she went to the back yard of Margaret Isherwood and steles. shawl. She then went to the same shop and offsrt d it ist Wedge, but the manager, Mr. J. C. Dalton, wisely det ined her until the police arrived. Though it was her first appear- ance he (the Chief Constable) suggested that it would be a mercy to send her away from her present surroundings, i e. to a reformatory school. 29 pawntickets had been found in possession of her aunt or mother.—Defendant was sent to a reformatory school for four years and a conviction recorded against her in the second case.—Mr. Talbot then expressed his indelitednes3 to Mr. J. C. Dalton for the assistance he had given the police on this and many other oceasions.—Aider- man Harrison, on behalf of the magistrates, also thanked Mr. Datton. STRIKING A YOUTH.—Robert Watson, 4, Williamson's Square, was charged with assaulting and beating Arthur Skelhorn, 50, Wellington-street,on the 16th March.—Defend- ant pleaded guilty.—'rhe complainant, Arthur Skelhorn, said that on Monday morning he came to his work late and defendant, who had also come late, scolded him. He replied and defendant struck him with each fist. His lip was burst and he had to go to Dr. Cannell. Witness's father w -nt to defendant about it and the latter offered to fight him.— Defendant said he struck complainant because he made use of a foul expression to him. He was sorry he struck him,—A fine of 103. and costs (18s. in all) was imposed. Mofinalr.—Befere the Mayor (Councillor Fairclough), in the chair; Aldermen Harrison and Francomb, and Mr. Monks.</p>
        <p>DRUNKENNESS.—For this offence William Curley, 5, jolley's-yard, was fined 5s and costs or seven days' impeson- merit ; Margaret Baguley, of Richardson'o-row, Liverpool. was committed to prison for a month with hard labour; and Jane Johnson, 9, Silver-square. was discharged on a promise '• to turn over a new leaf." She had been celebrating it. Patrick's day. A NEGLECTFUL CAB-DRIVER FlNED.—William McCormack 31, (Mord-street, was fined ss. and coots for leaving his cab in Winwick-street without anyone in charge on Thursday morning.—lnspector Taylor deposed to finding the cab at the Central Stat ion with no one in charge of it, and after lengthy inquiries and searching the defendant was found at his home in an intoxicdted condition.</p>
        <p>FURIOUS DRIVLNG.—Thomas Marsh Stringer, farmer, of Crowley, wee summoned fcr furiously driving a horse and trap down Bridge-street on Wednesday night.—? C. Whit- taker stated that the defendant drove at the rate of fifteen miles an hour down the narrow part of Bridge-street on Wednesday night when the thoroughfare was rather crowded. One or two persons narrowly escaped being run down, and when called on to stop by the police he took no notice, but re- sumed his course towards Bridge-foot He was followed and overtaken at the level crossing In Wilderspool-c.auseway. Defendant denied that he was driving so fast as stated, and said that he had not seen the constable beckon him to stop.— Corroborative evidence was given by Sergeant Chanter and Iropector Cox.—The magistrates imposed a fine of £l. and pats (135.), the alternative being a month's imprisonment. CHIMNEY ON FlRE.—Joseph Moores 15, Mersey-street. was tined is. and costs for allowing a chimney of his house to be on fire.</p>
        <p>COIL-DEALERS FlNED.—Earnest Haskins, coal-dealer, of Longford-street, pleaded guilty to having failed to have att ched to three hags of coal on the 9th March labels in,ll- - the correct weight. Inspector Place proved the charge. the defendant advancing the excure that the labels had come off the bags accidentally The Bench did not con- sider the offence a serious one, and discharged the defendant on his paying 2s. 6d. to the poor-box.—Thomas James Bmmess, coal-dealer, of Hail-street, was summoned for a similar (trance of 5 bags, and also for exposing for sale ten sacks of opal whiah were deficient in weignt. Mr. Place said the defendant was not in charge of the lurry on which the ten bags were found. It was in charge of two young men, but of course the summons had to be taken out against the defendant as the owner. There were 21 bags on the lurry, five of which were unlabled and ten deficient in weight De- fendant was fined 28.6 d. including costs for the first offence, and 53. and costs for the second.—James Bate, another coal- dealer, of Clegg-street, was fined 2s. 6d. including c ists for not having labels at:ached to five sacks; and Si. and costs for exposing the same sacks of coal while deficient in weight. In his case also the defendant was not in charge of the lurry, which was ariven by two young men.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1896-03-28_WidnesExaminer_art0132" n="art0132">
        <p>rIURS• —SEALSKIN JACKETS and FURS of every des- 1. cription Cleaned or Rc —Dyed and A ltered. Fur Skins Dresse i and Made-up into Rugs, Muffs, Collars, &amp;c.—B. COOK E, Manufacturing Furrier 11 Upper Newington, and 21, Rnshaw-street,Liverpool, Established over 21 years,</p>
