      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1896-03-28_WidnesExaminer_art0133" n="art0133">
        <head>EXAMINER, SATURDAY, MARCH 28 1896.</head>
        <p>THE EXAMINER, SATURDAY, MARCH 28 1896.</p>
        <p>umpAr.--Bcforo Cotricillov' ( 'a)</p>
        <p>Star:iev ~;q1</p>
        <p>A. PUBLIC HOUSE EXT*:NSIOS OF RET'USELL —Mr. Thomas Ireland, landlord of the " Cheshire Cheese," atch- ford, applied for one hour's extension of time—from 11 to 12 —on Thursday, the occasion of a supper being held there.— Councillor Roberts said he thought it was a case that did not deserve much clnsideration. There were case:, where, with even his strong views on such matters, he would grant an extension. bnt this was not one of them.—Mr. Ireland said thal, he dA not want the extension for drinking purposes ; the gathering was striely private.--Dr. Smith asked if the supper could not commence earlie, and the applicant replied in the negative.—Councillcr Roberts sal(' he did not think the reasons put forth in favour of the extension wera strong enough to justify them in granting it.—Dr. Smith did not express any further opinion and the application was then refused</p>
        <p>THE THEFT OF A PuP,sE.—Frederick Styles, RO3B and Crown-street, was brought up on t emand charged with steal- ing from the Stranger's Arms, on Monday, a purse contain- ing 18s. MO., and a metal cross, the property of Joseph ' Per. ival.—The Chief Constable said that ou Monday morning about 1 .30 Mrs. Percival, wife of the landlord of tha Stranger's Arms " was sitting at her breakfast when else had occasion to lake her purse from her pocket. She placed it down ou a woocten seat and shortly afterw,rds went up- stairs, leavi.,g the purse. Ten minutes later she came down, and saw prisoner leaving the house. Immediately after, she missed her purse and informed a n_an named Deleoze who made inquiries and follow ea prisoner, catching him in Mersey-streeL. tie told him Itc would have to go back to the " Stranger's Arms ".aud prisoner inAde no objection. On the Way he visited a closet and aiterwards accompanied Delooze to the house, where he was searched Rau noticing found upon hint. The police afterwards searched the clos,t and found the purse and its contents. He was then charged and said " I took it from the form b :t I had not looked at its contents." The prisoner pleaded guilty, and adklect that he was '' hard up "at the time, and seeing the articles on a bench in a ; public-house, and yielding to a sudden impulse had taken them.—The Dench, taking into consideration that the pri- soner had already bee I in gaol for a week, and that this was his first offence, decide 1 to simply record a convi. tion against him providing he left the town at once.—Prisoner, who ex- pressed his thanks, promised to obey, and was accordingly discharged. , DISORDERLY TIPPLERS.—WiIIiaro Callaghan was fined ss. ' and costs and Jane Waterworth was c 'mmitied for 14 days, both being guilty of drunkenness and disorderly behaviour on the previous day.</p>
        <p>THURSDAY.—Oefore Mr. S. Cliandley and Alderman Sti, ton.</p>
        <p>THEYT FROM THE MARKET. —Ellen Gregory, a, Runcorn woman, was charged with stealing from the Market-place on Wednesday afternoon, a quantity of calicoes and print goods the property of Henry Ellis,and valued at 10s. ; also charged with stealing six yards or fiannellette Ce property of Rachael Higharn.—The Chief Cons; able stated that 'this was the Bth case crom the market and the circumstances were as usual. Ellis saw the woman loitering 5140ut and from some informa- tion he received he went to he- and looked under her shawl and found some of the goods. which he took from her. De- teetive-Sergeant M ltaghan afterwards found he- in the " Barley Mow" where the remaining r,oods were found in charge of a barmaid. Vachael Highatn's flanneilette was also found and identified. Defendant admitted the offence. She had apparently made several trios, leaving tr. e proceeds at the "Ba ley Mow." It was a case of systematic theft.—A fine of 20s. and costs was imposed or in default 21 days' hard labour.</p>
        <p>CFIOOL CAsEs.—The Following persons were dealt with for not Fending their children regularly to Bcho9l :—Martin Carey, Latchford, 2s. 6.1. ; rthomas Chrimes. Warburton-st., 2s. 6d. ; John Coyne, Silver-square. 2s. 6d. Charles liggin- son, Ellesmere st., 2s. 6d. ; Joseph ?cattle'', Catherine ss. ; Widiam Thompson, Fierpoint-s—, boy io he birched by father.</p>
        <p>Dh:sEnTiox.—John Robinson ;1,...,01 ted being a de3eele from Her Majesty's Royal lingineers in London sines 14th March.-:}le was remanded to Strangeways.</p>
        <p>THE CHARGE OF BURGLARY Al' A. WARRINGTON PUBLIC- HOUSE.—Thomas Larkin was charged on rei,taud with burg- lariously entering the oreinises of the British Tar Inn,rente 1 by Ann Moffatt.on the 18th March, with intent tl steal. The Chief Con.table related the circumstances of the case as al- ready reported. Arthur Moffatt, son of the landlady of the British Ta-, gave evidence, ate substance of which has al- ready been given.—Pi isoner was committed to the Liverpool AsAzes for trial.</p>
        <p>RUNCORN PE l'Y Sw.c,,zrONß.</p>
        <p>MONDAY% —Before kiajor liazlPlin-st (chairman), Messrs. P.</p>
        <p>J. Norman and S. 'C'avlon</p>
        <p>LICENSING.-•Jam eft Robinson api died for and was granted permission to sell at the ahree Crowns, King-street, lately occupie by Waite" Whit-. A His-BAND CHARGED WITH CRUELTY.—ChirICS Johnson, 6, P:cow-street, was su!nrnoned at the instance of his wife, Elizabetk Johnson, 6. Spiing-streel , fra having caused her to live separately from him through his per :istent croelty. and with having failed to maintain his wife and children. —Mr. F. A. Lake, solicit or, appeared for the defendant.—The complainant siated that she lived with the defendant until the end 0r last November. They had 0. iturnbea of qurrrels, and the defendant would not let tier hive any food. He also took away the bedclothes so that she an t• ar of her childre•i could not sleep at his house. He tobl her the sooner she cleared out the better. He was a stonemason by trade. When she asked him for come food, he toll her to go avid work for it. Since she had lived away from him he had only sent her Is. She went to his house and asked nirn w hat he was going to do, and he struck her with a breakfast can. The e were three children; the had one 7, r li:rge, and th defendant had tw, at his house She did not. think the defend int was working now. he had been ill about twelve months.--In cross-examin ition by 'air. Lake, she admitted that she had been married before, and that the child Florence was not the defendant's. For the last two years she had been seber. It was not true that, he disputes had aaisen in conseenience of her being more o: less in d.ink. She ad- mitted that the defendan had given her .66e. of his wages a week. There was also a son of the defendant's working, and also a lodger in the house. She had received £2 ss. a week. but not regularly. When the defendant was ill, she received ss. a week from the distress fund. She never saved anything for a rainy day. She denied that she sent out a begging letter for the henefi., of herself. The bill for thirteen pairs of boots during ten months was cor. ect, bet she denied that she sent most of them to the pawnshon.—For the defence, Mr. Lake said it was a qae3t ion for the magis:rates whe: her they would have their time taken up in heating the evidence he had to submit, which would prove many things which bad been denied by the compliinant in cross-examination. Under the new Act in those proceedings it was necessary for lite. complainant to prove c, velty and also that the defendant had means to upport her. She bad done neither. So far as the defendant. w,s concerned, his chances ef earn- ing a livelihood were gone in consequence of a breakdown in his health, and he ivis nesa- living on charity.—The Clerk did not think that the act atated that It was necessary to prove that the defendant had means.— Mr. Lake said 'the- poiut had been recently decided in an appeal case. -After a short cons,•ltation, the Cha:rman said they had decided to adjourn the case tor s month so that the defendant would have an opportunity of canting to tome tertus with reference to the maintenatica of the children,— Mr. Lake said he could not suggest, for a moment that the defendant was able to keep t' ern. The Chairman said he aas advised that the defendant must, keep the children.— M" • Lake hoped that the Clerk would take notes of the fact that no evidence of mr ans had been given, CHARGE 01 NEGLECT OF Flaarrar,—lrenry Lawson and his wife Mary, of Grindrod-Square, were surnmoled for having on the 15th March being in charge of four childaen did un- lawfully and wilfully neglect well children in a manner likely to cause them unnecessary stereiing or injury to their h lath. —Sergeant Brewster slated that at a quarte- televen in company with P.C's Booth and Wright, he vis:ted Law- son's house. the door was open,and on a bed the aitchen, four childaen were lying. There were do beric'othea to cover them, and ne one was in he noose with them. The childaen were in a most neglected condition, and were covered wii Is vermin. The furni: ure of the house consisted of a round table.—As neither ot the defendants out in an appea.rance, a warrant was isswed.</p>
        <p>NVITEtouT A. DOG LICEN3E.—Thomas Stokes, b oalman Cholmondeley-nlace, was scrnmoned for having on the fith February kept a dog without a license.—P.C. Williams proved the case,and a fine of Is. and costs was impwied. DRUNK AND DiSORD} RLY.—James Morgan. labourer. West- road, Weston Point ; Martin 0•Nr;i1, Riley's-row, Weston ; Thomas Hulse, Runcorn ; and Cha-ks White, LoEtoc. Gra- lam, were all fined for being drunk and disorderly. PROFANE LANGUAGE.—A,,n Corcoran and Ellen Moran, of Mersey-square, two old offenders, were charged with using obscene and orofane language in Ifersey-st. They pleaded guilty, and were committed to prison for 14 days.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1896-03-28_WidnesExaminer_art0134" n="art0134">
        <head>FIIODSHAM PETTY SESSIONS.</head>
        <p>FIIODSHAM PETTY SESSIONS.</p>
        <p>WEDNESDAY.—Before Messrs. C. Reinolds, and A. Thomas. FURIOUS DRIVING—John Pemberton, hawker, Sutton, was summoned for iuriously driving it horse and car, on the high- way at Ounham-o'th-itill on the 14th inst.—Sergeant Hunt proved the case.—A fine or 10s. and Os. 6d. costs was imposed A PUBLICAN AND HIS WIFE.—John price, publican, 6, Cyprus-street, Liverpool, was summoned at the instance of his wife Hannah Pr ice, of Min Mount, Helsby, for eeserting her and neglecting to provide reasonable maintenance for herself and daughter, Ada Price. Applications were made for separation and maintenanc, orders.—Mr. W. H. Churton, solicitor, Cheater, appeared for the complainant. —Defendant denied the desertion. but acknowledged not having provided fo.- his wife.—Mr. Churton said the defendent was a respeci, able man, and up to April. 1E95. he ric:ed as stationmaster at Heleby. He then resigned, and took a house in the village. On the day of removal he suddenly let. for Liverpool, saving he had an appointment there. He had never been in Helsby since, and had not provided for his wife and child. It had been ascertained that he was a publican at Liverpool, and was residing with another person. He (hdr. Churton) did not wish to make matters worse than they were, it was useless aggravating matters. Defendant had made an offer of 10s. per week, and his client was willing to accept this.—Hannah Priez! stated that she was married to the defendant 34 rears ago. She bore out Mr. Chu/emu's state- ment as to her husband leaving her. She would be satisfied with the defendants offer of iOs. a week provided a separa- tion order was made.—the magistrates granted a separation order, as well as a maintenance order for 10s. per week, and ordered the defendant to pay the costs £4 his. A DRUNKEN DIAIVER.,—Thomas Sballcross, Church-street, Frodsham, was summoned for being drunk in charge of a horse and cart at Helsby on the 3rd Inst. The defendant issued a cross-summons against Constable Breeze for assault at the same time and place.—P.O. Breeze spoke to seeing Shallcross on the Chs ster man road about 6 p.m. on the date named, driving ' a pony and shandry. :.reeing that he was esleep, witners I stopped the horse, and the sudden jerk awakened Shallcross I who wanted to know why 1.1.3 was being interfered with. Wit- ness totes him to sit still and he would take him home. Shallcross then endeavoured to get out of the trap,and witness had to get a man to lead the horse whilst he got into the trap to compel Shalleross to be quiet for feat of an accident. Wit- ness succeeded qi getting Shallcross to the Police Station where lie was seen by Sergeant Clays, He had a bottle of whiskey in his pocket. —Sergeant Clays stated that Shailcross was in a beastly state of drunkenness.and unfit to be in charge of a horse and trap.—Evidence was also given by Walter Stretch, Thomas McEwen, and Albert Dickson showing that the defendant Llvillcross was under the influence of drink, and that P.C. Breeze acted humanely towards him.—The defendant complained that his throat was very sore in conse- quence of the constable's rough treatment of him He wee treated worse than a dog, and as if he were a murderer. Ile had only had threepenny worth of drink, and denied that he was drunk.—Several witnesses gave evidence for the defence None, however, saw the assault They contended that hall- cross was not drunk, but admitted that he was under the influence of drink.—A fine of El and £1 14s. 9d. costs was imposed.</p>
        <p>HOUSEHOLD LIN Ess,hernraed and marked free of charge. J. and W. Dutto , Warrington.—Advt.</p>
        <p>Cocoa is more thAti a mere timulating and ie'reshing drink, It is also a Alit riti us fool, and one of the most pro:: ions gifts of natute—sustaining and invigorating the system probably more than any other beverage, The Lal‘ct refers to Cadbury's Cocoa as "the standard of highest purity at present attainable in regard to Cocoa. No alkalies used. —Advt. 1</p>
        <p>• ••etlty teaol in it. T:le Runeorn men • ; irmir to gain ie winning A0:0,1,A a i.; their energy was rewarded when 4. Vati.i:ner got over, but the ball had been knocked on. The issue was, however, not long in doubt, as Nickson ran smartly over directly after and pldced his side three points ahead,a lead which they easily held to the close, the game ending with the score :—Runcorn,one goal,two tries (eleven points) ; Bradford, one goal, one try (eight points). " Hawkeye "in his general impressions,says " Though not exactly brilliant. the game was by no means uninteresting, and there was plenty to keep the attention of the spectators throughout the game. At one time it looked as if Bradford would win easily, but they sadly failed to stay, and in the last quarter of an hour their efforts were very weak and lifeless. To the' superior scoring no:7er of their backs Runcorn undoubtedly owe their victory. They had not half as many opportunities afforded them as the Bradford men, but wilen they did occur nothing was let slip, and one could not wish to see anything better than . the tries scored by Butterworth and Warder. En the first portion the Bradford forwards had much the best of it, and gave their halves any amount of chances,which, as already named, were allowed to go begging. When the Runcorn pack got possession the men behind them made no mistake, with the result that Bradford lost ! when they might have won. At full back Smith was a long wax superior to Heughton, the last named giving Bradford both their tries. Amongst the threequarters, ' Cooper played a grand game for Bradford, as did But- ! terwoith and Warder for Runcorn, but the others were ' only mouerate. At half Runcorn held a big advantage, and for a first appearance Nickson played a fine game, especially when it is remembered that he was playing ! behind beaten forwards for a good portion of the game. ! Halt-back is still a very weak spot in the Bradford team, • and the sooner the halves can be persuaded to remem- ber that there are meu behind them the better for the team. In the forward ranks 0. Buehes, Firmer, and Laneley for Runcorn, and Bolden, Fe:irntey and Wilson or Bradford were the most conspicuous.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1896-03-28_WidnesExaminer_art0135" n="art0135">
        <p>) 13 . iii,</p>
        <p>CRICKETERS should scquire the "Pocket Diary and Companion,'' issued by the well-known Sports ida-m- -faaturers. GEoRGE G. Buss Es- &amp; Co„ Pckham, London. All the information Cricketers require is fond therein, as well as the usual Diary, yet it will go in the waistcoat pocket. Leather bound, gilt edges, 6d. ; postage, id. If you are no satisfied, G. G. B. &amp; Co. will return the price.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1896-03-28_WidnesExaminer_art0136" n="art0136">
        <head>WARRINGTON v. BATLEY.</head>
        <p>WARRINGTON v. BATLEY.</p>
        <p>These teams met on Saturday at Warrington to play their return match. The weather was in striking con- trast to that which favoured (?) the first meeting, for on that occasion, the rain came down in torrents and the wind blew a hurricane. That match was a complete farce, and Batley were lucky to win by a dropped goal which was really scored by the wind. On Saturday the weather was tine, and it was almost an ideal day for football, so that a crowd of about 6,000 ?ersans were present to witness what proved to be a close game with regard to the scoiing, but a very one-sided game as far as the play went. The teams were as follows :—Warrington : Heesom, full back ; Carey, Barber, Cross and Lawless, three- quarters ; Bate ana Higginbottom, half backs • Burrows, Taylor, Swift, Saimders, Hill, Andrews, Dakin, Fair- hurst, forwards. Batley : Naylor, full back ; Shaw, Goodall, Fitzgerald and Watson, three-quarters; Gooda;1, and Oakland, half backs ; Shackleton, Stubley, Kitchen, Scott, Littlewood, Willey, Menus and Balderson, forwards.</p>
        <p>Shackleton kicked off towards the pavilion goal punc- tual to time, and after Bigginbottom's return, a round of passing between that player, Bate and Carey, ended in the latter being downed on the line by Naylor. This was immediately followed by another bcut taken part in by Bate, Barber and Lawless, and Batley were exceed- ingly fortunate in escaping. Batley now showed up and Fitzgerald and the two Goodells put in some good work which brought the ball to the centre line; but a forward dribble by Taylor, Andrews, Saunders: and Fairhurst again transferred play to the enemy's line, when Naylor was lucky in getting hold and punting to the quarter flag. A change now came over the scene, for the three- quarter Goodall intercepting a pass which was meant for Lawless, made a dashing run down the field, and he looked a certain scorer when fieesom and Taylor man- aged to over-haul him within a few yards from the line. Batley still maintained the attack, and on one occasion, they forced Carey over the line with the ball in his pos- session. Finding he could not kick with safety,he made a 53n5at1011a1 run from one touch line to the other behind the goal posts. and got clear, so that his final kick went in touch at the quarter flag. Needless to say, Carey came in for a hearty recognition for this feat. Bate got the ball away from the line out and a fine dribble by Bate, Lawless, and Dakin, transferred the ball to the place where it had been the greater por- tion of the time up to now. Fitzgerald proved a stumbling-block to the above three players, and made a fire kick in touch. A scrimmage followed the line out, (r.r this lorber received a long pass from Bate, and cle.reed eve. y one hut Nay lor. No one was handy to re- ceive e. pass, and this a certain try was lost. Oakland was penaliscd foi offside tactics immediately after, and Lawless on being deputed with the place kick sent toe ball just outside the goal posts. Goodall was on4ble t. 0 reply, however, for he war tackled imme- diately on getting the ball. Bate got the ball from the scrimmage which ensued and passing to Cross,the latter took it in fine style and rushed over the line,thus open- ing the sering account. Taylor was unequal to raising this try to a goal. After the kick ont,Saunders charged '.ics.autl ii. .rmipany with Swat followed up with such effect, that f ie latter was robbed almost in the act of dropping on the ball. Filzgerald showed some of his international form by a grand run and fine kick in touch, and this put Batley on a permanent attack until half Lime. A fine pass was given to Watson. and the latter finding the way blocked to the line rounded his opponents and dropped a fne goal, thus putting Batley one point ahead. Batley still pressed hotly after re-start, and Beeson' attempted to kick in tonch when hard pressed, but the kick was charged down and Hig- ginbottoin mustied up and saved when it seemed almost impossible. A scrimmage took place on the line, and Goodall getting possession passed to 0 ikland who would have had an unopposed run in, but Barber upset his c-lctilatioes and intercepted the pass and kicked in tel.: bly sate quarters. The remainder of this half was fought in a portion of the field not quite so an- comfortable to Warrington.Bate on one occasion looking a likely scorer. and Saunders and Swift also came very near to scoring, but half time arrived with the score of a dre•lned goal to a try in favour of Batley. aili rt.-Ali. and Warrington during this half com- pletely overplayed Batlev,only lack of finish preventing a large score. This lack of finish almost lost Warrington the game, however, for it was only ten minutes from time that the try which put them ahead as scored. Soon after the kick off Bate sent to Carey and the latter had lear,, tines in not getting a dropped goal, the ball just going under the crossbar. A hot attack was now made and Batley had all their work cut out to keep Warring- ton out. Bate was almost over, but was tackled just too soon and the same player again struck the goalposta with a kick, from which Goodall got the ball away to the quarter flag. Barber was again floored after a brilliant run, and Cross foil similarly hard lines soon afterwards. Batley were evidently determined by fair means or foul to maintain their lead of one point and 'adopted very questionable tactics such as wasting time by kicking the ball into the next field and transgressing rules. They were repeatedly penalised for not putting the ball in the scrimmages straight, off side tactics and the like, but Warrington were not able to utilise them to any good account. At lenth,the str.ine6 feelings of the crowd were relieved, when from a scrimmage under the goal posts, Higgin- bottom got hold and passed to Bate. The latter handed to Cross, who made a brilliant run right to the line, when finding Naylor in his way, he parted to Barber, and the home captain completed a brilliant piece of work by scorieg at the corner. Hill struck the cross bar in trying to kicl, a goal. Warrington now buckled to with a determined effort to increase their lead, but they met as a. Lia.ly defence. Lawless and Barber had each hard lines, and on one occasion, Taylor found his way to the line, but found too many opponents round him to ground the ball. Near the end, Batley made one more desper- ate effort to pull the game out of the fire and managed to reach the home quarter flag. but Barber, Cross, and Heesom wore too many for them, and Warringtan again asserted their superiority, and when the whistle had blown, they were victors by two tries (6 points), to a dro.ieed goal (4 points).</p>
        <p>RUNCORN v. BRADFORD.</p>
        <p>l'oue thousand is a very liberal estimate of the number present, to witness this match at Runcorn on Saturday. The home team had been re-arranged after the surprising defeat by H 11, Robinson going three-quarter and a second-teamer in Nickson taking his place at halt. Brad- ford had a good team up, the only alterations from the published list being the substitution of Rutherford for Pollard in the three-quarters, and Armistead for Mar- shall amongst the forwards. The teams turned out with commendable promptitude under the charge of Mr. Dil- leon, of Warrington, who, it may be said, handled the teams throughout with firmness, and gave his decisions promptly, to the satisfaction of all present. In the open- ing stages of the game neither side could claim any material advantage, the most noticeable feature being some really good kicking by Smith, the Bradford full- back, and some very haphazard rushing on the part of the home forwards. In pushing power the two packs seemed very evenly matched, but the Bradford eight were clearly superior in tactics, and gained possession fue,i C. ecel, magee five times out of six. Unfortun- ately for their side these opportunities ;were not by any means utilised to the best ad- vantage, mainly owing to the selfish play of the visiting half-backs, particularly Smith. Each end of the ground was quickly visited in turn, fleughton on one occasion relieving finely. Following a eine- t passine run by the Runcorn three-quarters. Coupe kickeil well into the home 25. Instead of return- ing the ball Houghton chose to indulge in a bit of gallery play, with the result that the opposing forwards robbed him of the ball, dribbled over the line,and Wilson scored a try, which Cooper neatly converted. Bradford had the better of the argument until close on half-time,when a really smart passing movement by the Runcorn backs gave them a tooting in the visitors' 25. Smith saved finely, but the" dark greens" were not to be denied, and from a scrimmage just under the Bradford posts Butter orth got possession. Dodging cleverly several opponents, the sturdy young Runcorn three-quarter dashed over the line in fine style. Myres made but an indifferent attempt to convert the point, and half-time arriveel with the score :—Bradford, one goal ; Runcorn, one try. The hopes of the Runcorn enthusiasts, which had fallen to about zero when Bradford led by a clear goal, had somewhat revived when. Butterworth scored his clever try, but immediately after commencing the second half they were destined to fall again. Cooper in- dulged in one of his neat drops, and the bail went to tou. '1 lt player mulled badly. and Smith (the half-back) following up smartly, scored a try, which was not converted. This was not at all to the liking of W.Faulkner and his men, who at once forced the game, Nickson being very prominent in making openings for the men behind him. Still, Bradford wanted a lot of getting over,a eel g od work by their forwards kept Run- corn on the defence fora few minutes,several unproduc- tive drops at goal being indulged in by Crompton and Cooper. After a period of dull play at midfield, in sshich both teams showed rather more zeal than skill, the ituncorn terwards commenced to play a much more combined game. Getting possession of the ball,Nickson /started a passing run,which culminated in Warder gain- :elf; e e ,t 1 try near the corner. The cheers which this poi t elicited were redoubled when Farmer kicked a gran.' goal from the place, thus putting the teams on a level tenting. From now to the end there</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1896-03-28_WidnesExaminer_art0137" n="art0137">
        <head>WIDNE'i v. HUNSLEC.</head>
        <p>WIDNE'i v. HUNSLEC.</p>
        <p>A considerable surprise awaited the Hunslet specta- tors, who turned Out in good numbers for the return game between the Pa rkside tiiteen and Widnes, for they had the mortification of seeing the biggest score of the season piled up against thrir favourites. In a footbal sense, they had their reward, however, for the exhibition was a remarkably i iteresting one from first to last. The Hunslet back division was much disorganised by the ab- seece cf Hannah, Fletcher, and Robinson, who had not recovered completely from the effects of the Huddersfield engagement on the Wednesday, when Hunslet won a narrow victory. A forward (Eno) went full-back, and before toe game was five minutes' old gave his oppon- ents a simple try, Brennan being the scorer. The home team flattered their supporters a bit after this, for W. Goldthorpe got away on his own 25 line,and raced finely up the field, kicking deftly: over his opponents' heads, when Brennan and Nevitt faced him. Running on stronglF, he also managed to recover possession, and gave his brother Albert a pass on the line, which the last-named scored him, and then kicked a goal. Hunslet did not long remain in possession of the lead, for their opponents simply ran them off their feet, and actually put on three further tries before the interval, A. Peters, Drummond, and Raiford being the try-getters. The try scored by Drummond was the result of a beautifully-executed bit of finesse, and was warmly cheered by the home partisans. Early in the second half Lapping and Wright gave Walter Goldthorpe the ball close to the line, and a try and goal reduced the Widnes lead to four points. This had the effPct of wak- ing up the Lancashire men to the realisation of the fact that victory might yet be wrested from their grasp, and they went at it for all they were worth, not being by any means content to merely defend. Emery scored after a rattling rush, and Johnson kicked a goal. The second half continued to be very evenly fought, and save tt at Hunslet were dreadfully let down by their full back there was some fine football shown by both sides. If clinking run by Mitchell yielded a try to Hunslet after a long attack, and before the close Rispan put on another for Widnes, making the final reco:d :—Widues two goals and four tries (22 points) ; Hunslet, two goals and one try (13 points.)—Elunstet were fairly and squarely beaten from start to finish, and, with the exception of the centre three-quarters, their players were over- matched at every point of he game. It Was said that Hannah, Robinson, and Fletcher were given a rest be- cause the match was looked upon as a pretty easy thing for the home side.lf this was the case the executive got a rude and richly-deserved lesson.On their,side the Widnes forwards were a long way superior to their opponents, and this gave Drummond and Barber a good opportunity of showing their skill. Drummond's resource w•is wonder- ful, and he never received the ball without gaining a big advantage for his side. All the other Widnes backs did themselves credit, Brennan being in specially fine form at full back. The brothers Goldthorpe played throughout a fine game atcentre, and did all they could to stave of defeat.No fault could be found with the offensive move- ments initiated and carried out by the Hunslet backs, but their defence, usually strong, was terribly weak throughout.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1896-03-28_WidnesExaminer_art0138" n="art0138">
        <head>NORTHERN UNION.</head>
        <p>NORTHERN UNION.</p>
        <p>A meeting of the Lancashire section of the Northern Union was held in Manchester on Tuesday evening, Mr. J. E. Warren (Warrington) presiding. A deputation from the Runcorn Club applied that the Rochdale Hor- nets should be ordered to play the postponed match be- tween the two clubs at Runcorn, in view of the fact that a similar arrangement had been made with Oldham and Manningham. The Committee decided that as the matter had already been dealt with by the Northern Union they had no jurisdiction. Several applications by clubs at present playing under English Rugby Union rules for places in next season's competition were con. sidered, and it was decided to defer the matter until a meeting to be held early in May, when the clubs will be selected. The transfer of Elijah Prescott, from Wigan to Tyldesley,was allowed.</p>
        <p>WARRINGTON " A " V Sr. MARY'S.—On Saturday an- other meeting of these old rivals was held, again on the ground of the Saints. Both teams went to the field bent on victory, and a very stiff match was the result. Both weather and field were in the best condition. For the "A" team there played :—Full back, Spruce; three quarters. Forster, Scott, Isherwood, O'Brien ; half backs, Clarke, Foden; forwards, Bailey, Berry, Sudlow, Mea- cock, Savage, Wilcock. Scholtze,Freeth. For St, Mary's: —Roughley, full back; Bennett, Lawless, G. Roughley, Warburton, three-quarters ; Mason and Houghton half backs; Edgar,Johnson, Higginbotham, Davies.T. Rough- ley, Caldwell, Siddall, and Burrows, forwards. Clarke kicked off for Warrington and the ball was not returned. A scrimmage ensued in the home quarters and Fodeu increased the lead by a kick towards goal. Warburton caught the ball and returned it to Warrington territory. The Saints followed up and Clarke relieved, Roughley's return being charged down by Isherwood. This check was °pity momentary for Edgar put his side on the ag- gressive again by a flying kick. A Number of scrimm- ages ensued on the iNairington line, and in the midst of one Houghton was seen to fall upon the ball, just over the line. The point was allowed and Bennett kicked a neat goal. Play then settled at half way, Mason being prominent with smart passes to the threequarters. Berry next got away and made for the line, transferring to Wilcock, who lost the ball in a pro- mising positi U. Berry and Edgar both made marks, but to little advantage. Later. Mason intercepted a pass of Clarke's, and kicking past Spruce, a fine race en- sued between him and O'Brien, the latter of whom was the faster, and just succeeded in kicking dead at the corner. Clarke next passed to Isherwood who made a emart run, but could not pass Roughley. Scott next , seized the ball and was only just grassed on the line. , Foden also broke away but was similiarly treated. Both sides now played with vigour and neither side had any advantage until a bout of passing brought the visitor, near the line. Here O'Brien received toe ball and jumped over the line at the corner, grounding the ball. He was, however, rolled into touch at the same time, though there is no doubt that he grounded over the goal line first. The linesman put up his flag and the score was disallowed. Half-time arrived with St. Mary's 1 goal (5 points), Warrington ail. Mason restarted and Warrington pressed. Clarke passed to Foden who trans- ferred to O'Brien, and he scored smartly at the corner. Foden's kick fell short. Desultory play followed for some time. The Warrington forwards had the ball in almost every instance now and a long attack was made on the home line. Repeated rushes were made but as often checked. Roughley relieved and the opportunity was lost: The game ended with a victory for the Saints of 1 goal (5 points), to Warrington 1 try (3 points).</p>
        <p>litniPonw v. WIDNES "A " TEAMS.—These teams met at Widnes on Saturday afternoon, in a " friendly " match, it being the third time this season that the clubs have met. The weather was beautifully fine, and a large crowd was present, showing that considerable interest was evinced in the encounter, although the match was not under the auspices of the league. The opening stages were very fast and for fully twenty minutes neither team could claim any advantage. Eventually Jones scored for Widnes, after a pretty passing move- ment between the backs, but the kick at goal failed. Runcorn played up after this and W. Pendlebury got over under the posts, but for some reason the referee disallowed the try. The game after this underwent a great change, and finished up in a very regrettable man- ner with the score—Widnes, one try; Runcorn nil.</p>
      </div>
