      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1896-03-28_WidnesExaminer_art0140" n="art0140">
        <head>CONSERVAVIVE MEETING AT WARRINGTON.</head>
        <p>CONSERVAVIVE MEETING AT WARRINGTON.</p>
        <p>1. great Conservative meeting was held at the Parr Hall on Saturday,in connection with the Lancashire and Cheshire Conservative Wokingmens' Association. In the afternoon a conference of delegates was held, presided over by Mr. Seton Karr, M.P. In the evening a public meeting was held, presided over by Mr. J. C. Parr, J.P., D.L., and there were also present :—The Mayor and Mayoress, Aldermen Harrison, Bolton, Francomb and Wright, Councillors Smethurst, Slaw, Woods, Bennett, Skelton, Cannel!, Davies, Lee and Heaton, 1.13r5. W. Owen, R. B. Matthews, L. W. Reynolds, H. Greenall, W. J. For- shaw, John Fairclough, A. Lister-Kaye, R. W. Howson, W. H. J. Jenkins, E. Rose, William B. Roberts, Tinsley, L. Greening, Byerley, T. R. Westbrook, H. Toft, W. Skelton, A. Jolley, S. C. Nicholson, F. W. Deacon, Cap- tain Menzies, Mr. Colin Mclver, Mr. H. B. Melville, Major Pickmere, Messrs. W. S. Haddock, H. Hatton, J. Hesketh, J. Hartley, Henry Houghton, H. W. Hough- ton, J. Houghton, Thomas Houghton, J. Harding, R. Isherwood, Ralph Johnson, W. D. Jeans, James Grace, A. J. Jolley, G. Jolley, W. Jolley, T. Knight, S. Mason, T. Mason, P. Naylor, T. H. A. Pilkington, J. Partington, J. H. Pickston, J. Hugh, G. G. Payne, Plunkett, T. H. Ashcroft, W. E. Aitken, T. C. Brookes, J. Burgess, R. W. Collin, R. H. Dainteth, V. B. D ivies, R. G. Fairclough, and E. L. Fletcher. Just after 7 30 Lord Ashbourne, accompanied by Mr. and Mrs. J. Charlton Parr, Mr. Robert Pierpoint, M.P., Mr. Co ingsby Disraeli, M.P., Mr. H. Seton- Karr, M.P., Mr. John White, anl Alderman Sutton, appeared on the platform, and were immediately welcomed by loud: cheering, which lasted for several moments.</p>
        <p>Lord ASHBOURNR, who was received with cheers and the singing of "For he's a jolly good fellow," said it gave him a great deal of pleasure to be present that night. He promised in reply to a courteous invitation of that great federation,before toe general election,that he would give himself the honour and pleasure of ad- dressing them, amd he was glad to redeem that pro- mise. It was always a pleasure to any man who had any human sympathy in his composition to address a great and crowded asseinb'y composed of men and women who were animated to a large extent by com- mon views upon great public questions; and he must be conscious,as he (the speaker) was conscious at the pre- sent time, that he was addressing those who by their eneray,their determination,their power of work,their in- sistence upon their own convictions—that he was ad- dressing those who largely and powerfully contributed to the immense victory of the last general election—(cheers) —the great victory that had placed in power Lord Salisbury and Mr. Balfour—(cheers)—the Duke of Devonshire and Mr. Chemberlain—(cheers)—and that had called into existence a Government which not only recognized the obligations entailed upon them by their office, but recognized, as all men who were worthy of power must recognize the responsibilities that came from power. (Loud cheering.) At home, in their domestic affairs, in that region which touched them so closely and which came home to their every-day life, of course their responsibilities were great, they were obvious, they came home to every one of them; they a federation whose very title proclaimed their interest in all that went to make up the power, the real power of Eogland, they largely must feel an interest in the domesti .; legislation that must engage, and that did en- gage much of the thoughts of the present Government. (Applause.) Their workmen who were disabled in their employment,those measures popularly called the Employ- ers' Liability Bills—these were great topics that must en- gage the cloie and anxious,the sympathetic consideration of any Government which came into sower ia England. Amore: other topics that has come within this short ses- sion before the House of Commons was the question of the housing of the 'working classes. If they found a question like that of the housing of the working classes that must engage and enlist sympathy, surely it was worth an effort, surely when proposals were made that had been made in Parliament they were worth an honest trying to see whether sornethina soundly practical could be found in them. (Cheers.) He was not going to dis- cuss that night the questions in domestic legislation that might well occupy the attention of Government. Proceeding, Lord Ashbourne said he sunpesed since the present Government came into power the most startling and striking event that history, when it turned to note the incidents of the last twelve months, would stop to dwell upon was the extraordinary, marvellous upheaval of high, worthy patriotic sentiment. (Hear, hear and applause.) It had been said that the people of this country were a nation of shopkeepers, that they were forgetting the old days of English spirit, of English coutage, of English magnificent nerve, and of the splen- did patriotism that had made England the great Empire that it was—(applause)—and he did believe that many foreign nations and many people at home had begun to think that the England of the past was never again to be the England that people would look on with honour, with respect, and with some awe. (Applause.) The change had come about, and it was a change to make thoughtful people more thoughtful still, a great and interesting fact for all who came to think of what was in a nation, and what were its great powers. How did this come about? The German Emperor's message (hisses) Lord Ash. bourne observed that be was not dealing with the ques- tion of what was the motive that induced the telegram to be sent. He believed In his heart it did not pass in the mind of the person that sent the message the enor- nous feelings that were excited in a moment in the Eng- lish mind. (Applause.) He believed that the Emperor did not,: as rnanytof them 'did not, know the circum- stances of the raid into the:Transvaal. They now knew all about it, or a great dee.' about it. They I knew very little about it then. He ventured to say that the Emperor when he sent the telegram knew a very moderate amount about it indeed. (Interrup- tion, and cries of " Order.') He was now speakiag upon the results if the telegram and they were enormous re- sults. (Great cheering.) They were resists which every- one of them the longest day they might live, together with their children after them, could look upon with generous and manly pride. (Applause.) Rightly or wrongly, they conceived that it was necessary for Fug- land and Englishmen to assert themselves—(hear, hear) —to stand together as a great united nation, shoulder to shoulder, heart beating with heart, and with one common purpose of a lafty and pure English patriotism. (Hear, hear, and loud applause.) Thus it had come about that their navy,. always a fine navy, whose interests were looked after with honourable zeal by Lord Spencer—(hear, hear, and applause)—en- gaged the immediate and earnest attention of the present Government, and that lately in the House of Commons 1 a programme was unfolded with power by Mr. Goschen —(applause)—stating wnat was intended to be done to I develop and to foster the great navy of England. (Ap- plause.) More than that, though that was an immense thing to have done for this country of ours, surrounded by the sea,that must always depend upon the magnitude and upon the power of its navy—(applause)—more than that, they found that their colonies that surrounded the ' world—that the heart of England was able to find an I echo all round he world—(applause)—and that a nation of shopkeepers had sent out to its colonies all round the world true-hearted men and women who were proud to ray they were proud to cast in their lot with England if the moment of trial and difficulty ever occurred. (Hear, hoar, and applause.) Surely these were vast results, Foreign affairs in different parts of the world, said Lord Ashbourne, had since the present Government come into office occupied much of their attention. He alluded to I the Venezuelan difficulty—they lived so quickly now-a- I days that it seemed half a generation since President Cleveland's Message was received, though it was but a few months ago—and observed that he believed it was as old as the hills. It was absurd to toink that this country and America should ever fro to war over a matter of this kind. Between the two peoples there was a great deal of common blood, sympathy, his- tory and common language, and it was thought that there would be some way got out of the Venezue- lan boundary difficulty, by arbitration, good sense and common understanding. (Applause.) He believed that represented the public opinion of the country. Everyone was for arbitration and peace about A.merica and Venezuela, and when they turned to Turkey with its miserable, wretched episode of Armenia, what a di f ferent kind of language was often used. It was a sad wretched story that one could not speak about withou a swelling heart and tears almost coming into the eyes and a pain pressing upon that part which contained the human sympathy. (Hear,hear,and applause.) It was - sad and awful thing to read the record of thode miser able, wretched, revolting, barbarous outrages in Ar- menia. (Applause.) He did not believe that great , crimes of either individuals or nations could for ever go , unpunished, and he believed, however it was accom- plished, there would be a great punishment meted out for those great and appalling wrongs. (Hear, hear.) It used to be a tradition that they in England had a great friendship for Turkey. It was a common feeling with everyone. They could remember back for many a long day, those of them that were not as young as the young ones, that there was always a friendly feeling between Turkey and England. Was that feeling gone? When that feeling went owing to the weakness of Turkey, owing to those outrages not being checked as they bad wished and hoped they would have been, when that friendly feeling went, could it be so easily got back, and was Turkey .a Power that could see with ease and resignation slipping away from her the sympathy, good will and friendly feeling of a great em- pire like England ? (Applause.) There might be occa- sions when war was necessary for the vindication of their safety and their honour—(hear, hear)—but they should not be made ever to go to war except under the impulse of a great necessity, under the obligation of a clearly ascertained duty. (Ap- plause.) Continuing, Lord Ashbourne observed that amidst the train of foreign affairs with which the present Government had to deal there was the question which was debated the:previous night in the House of Commons—a debate in which his friend Mr. Seton-Barr took part. (Applause.) There bad unfortunately been a debate and a division. He was always sorry on those questions upon which England should speak with an un- broken voice that there appeared to be a debate repre- senting difference of opinion, and a division, no matter</p>
      </div>
