      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1896-03-28_WidnesExaminer_art0147" n="art0147">
        <head>MR. MEL B. SPURR A.T THE PARR</head>
        <p>MR. MEL B. SPURR A.T THE PARR</p>
        <p>HALL, On Friday night there was a very good attendance at the Parr Hall when Mr. Mel B. Spurr, the deserv- edly popular entertainer ,was the principal artiste in an admirably arranged programme. The other indi- vidual contributor to the enjoyment of the audience ' was Miss Ethel Thompson,solo pianist (pupil of Mr. A. R. Sutton). Mr. Sutton's amateur band also interspersed items of music, the first of which,com- mencing the programme,was a Mendelssohn march. Mr. Spurr then made his initial appearance and was well received. He sang his own interpretation of "The Tin Gee Gee," and was loudly applauded. Following this he related the side-splitting mishaps of a number of boys bent on "robbing the larder,,' under th 3 title of " my friend Treacle," Elliott)'( and achieved a distinct success. Mr. Spurr then treated the audience to one of his exquisitely played zither solos following with his admirable recital of "The Game of Life," to zither accompani- ment. The orchestra gave an excellent treat- ment of Mr. F. H. Crossley's intermezzo and also a skilful execution of " Czardas " (Michiels). a spirited piece of Russian character, and suggesting merry festivities. Oi the whole in this, the first half of the programme, Mr. Sutton's orchestra did itself and its originator great credit. The last and chief item in the first part of the programme was a very versatile musical sketch entitled "Our Smoking Concert" containing songs, speeches, recitals, &amp;c., which were condu- cive of much merriment and which reflected great credit upon Mr. Spurr, the composer. Theirr originality was another pleasant feature. Afte a short interval the second half of the pro gramme was commenced by the band rendering well Sup* "Poet and Peasant." The remaining items followed successively and were :—musical monologues,"After the Ball," Mr.Spurr; concerto for piano and orchestra (Mendelssohn) : Miss Ethel Thompson as solo pianists in this acquitted her- self in a most praiseworthy manner, her finished execution redounding to the credit of her teacher, Mr. Sutton. Mr. Spurr concluded the proceedings by another musical sketch "Liberty Hall " (of his own composition), a synopsis of which included a host of items of the most varied, comical, and generally pleasing character. The audience ap- plauded vociferously, and Mr. Spurr's reputation was was largely enhanced by last night's "samples of humour and musicianly skill. As an entertain- ment the affair was a great success and few persons —if any—had any fault to find with its composi- tion and submission.</p>
        <p>WEEKLY REPORT ON THE HEALTH OF WARRINGTON. Week ending 14th March, 1896. During the week, which was the 11th, of the current year, the following births and deaths were registered : Births. Deaths. Warrington 33 30 Latchford 4 4</p>
        <p>Total 37</p>
        <p>The'excess orbirths over deaths was 3. The death rate from all causes was equal to 30.4 per I,ooo,against 221. the average of the previous four weeks, and against 21.8 the corresponding week of last year. The death rate from the 'seven chief zymotic diseases was equal to 5-3 per :,000, against 21 the average rate of the four previous weeks, and againtt 0.9 the corresp,,nding week 0f,1895. The ages of mortality were- -1. 1-5 5-15 15-25 25-65 65 up. 11 7 2 1 7 6 34</p>
        <p>. •</p>
        <p>The deaths included-1 from diarrhcea, 4 from measles, 1 from whooning cough, 1 from alcoholismus, 4 from phthisis, 3 from old age, 3 from brain disease 5 from lung disease, 2 from liver disease. The following cases of infectious disease have been reported during the week :—Nil. Total number of patients in hospital on Mar.l4th-14: METEOROLOOY.—The mean temperature was 44 Fah., and therefore the 'same as the preceding week. The maximum temperature 55F.. was recorded on the 9th inst., and the minimum temperature 33F., on the 11th inst.</p>
        <p>The: Rainfall measured .49 inch. The general direc- tion of the wind was S.W. The reading of the Anemo- meter denoting 2,655 miles. JOHN H. GORNALL, '4,edical Officer of Health.</p>
        <p>THE FREEST WOMEN IN THE WORLD.</p>
        <p>As the men are the life nod soul of the religion of Bo.rmah, so the women are the heart and soul of the life of the country. Mongolian in race, and Indian now by conquest, they have achieved for themselves a free- dom which may be envied not only by their Hindoo- Chinese sisters, but by European women as well. The Burmese are absolutely the freest women in all the world. Marriage is an equal contract, all property being held jointly. Property inherited by a woman be- fore or after marriage, or earnings made by her, are ab- solutely her own. Marriage is an affair of the heart, and ameng this easy-going and affectionate people love- matches are not delayed till the husband can provide a hame and an income, but the girl-wife and boy-husband are taken into the house of either parent and maintained till a separate household can be set up. If love is lost, or there is found to be incompatability of temper, or if either wife or hasband become a drunkard, a gambler, or vicious in life,divorce or separ- ation can be easily obtained by application to the magis- trate,with equal division of ttte joint property. Divorces are, however, rare ; family life is said to be very affec- tionate, and children are adored. Wives are consulted in all affairs of life, and a farmer would hardly dare to sell his paddy harvest without the consent of his wife ; in fact, she is found to be a much closer bargainer than he is.</p>
        <p>_ Here we have the equality of the sexes, but in a tiny island called Minicoy, off the southern coast of India, womed have gone one better, and the positio rs of the sexes are reversed. In Minicoy woman is lord—or lady —of all she surveys, and it is the man who knuckles under. The wife is the recognised bead of the house. She owns it and everything in it, while everything that her husband—who works very hard—can earn goes to increase her wealth. Her husband belongs to her, too, and when she marries him she gives him her name in- stead of taking his.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1896-03-28_WidnesExaminer_art0148" n="art0148">
        <head>HEAT WAVd IN AUSTRALIA.</head>
        <p>THE HEAT WAVd IN AUSTRALIA.</p>
        <p>A letter from a man living on the borders of New South Wales, about 300 miles from Broken Hill, gives so-ne particulars of the terrible effects of the heat wave that recently passed over the colony. The record for the week ending January 20 is given as 115 deg., 116 deg., 117 deg., 1191eg', 115deg.,120deg. "We are all done up," he wrote subsequently ; " thermometer at 122deg.in the shadiest spot we can hang it! We have had many sad deaths, among others our poor gardener. I heard he was ill and ran down to his hut, to find him lying insensible. The manager and I did all we could for an hour, but I saw it was hopeless from the first. He died at nightfall. We dared not wait to make a coffin; indeed the exertion of digging his grave was as much as we could do, so we laid him in the earth in his ' bluey,' with a sheet of galvanised iron off the roof unler him and anocher over. We could not even read a prayer,as it was dark and too far to go for book and light and return. The sheep are dying by thousands ; horses all knocked up for want of water ; the mail coach cannot run, and we have to depend on a camel or two that are being sent. So C- and I have determined te start to walk to Broken Hill, as if we wait longer we may not have the strength. Many squatters here are quite ruined, the millions of rabbits own the country, and this drought has finished their ruin. Even the blacks have prickly heat, a fact hitherto unknown."</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1896-03-28_WidnesExaminer_art0149" n="art0149">
        <head>REUARKA FiLE AFFAIR IN STAFF() RDSHI RE.</head>
        <p>REUARKA FiLE AFFAIR IN STAFF() RDSHI RE.</p>
        <p>AN INSANE LOVER AND HIS SWEETHEART.</p>
        <p>A somewhat sensational affair took place at Wallbrook Coseley, Staffordshire, on Sunday afternoon. Whilst walking along the canal a young man named Powell heard a splash, and .directly afterwards discovered a man and a woman in the canal. He promptly jumped in, and succeeded in getting them both out. It was sub- sequently ascertamed that the names of the people were Abraham Mason, aged twenty-thz ee bricklayer, Broad- street, Wallbrook, and Gertie Westwood, Tipton Green, who, it was stated, were lovers. On being taken to Coseley Police S7ation Mason made some incoherent statements. He was subsequently examined by Dr. Clendinnen, who certified him to be insane, and yester- day he was removed to an asylum. He had only re- cently recovered from a serious illnrss, since which he has had to be closely watch-d. No ill-feeling, it was stated, existed between the pair, and no reason could be assigned for the strange occurrence.</p>
        <p>The annual number of births is estimated at 36,792,000 an average of 100,800 a day, 4.2J0 an hour, aud 70 a minute.</p>
        <p>According to the most careful computation, or,ly one person in 100,000 of both sexes attain the age ot 100 years, ard six to seven in 100 the age of 60. The total population of the earth is estimated at about 1,200,000,000 souls. of Whom 35,214,000 die annually,an average of 98.848 a day,4,020 an hour, and 67 a minnte. Between 1881 and 1893 the number of suicides in Ger- many was 105,327. The highest rate, 65 in 100,000 in- habitants, was in the Prussian army. Saxe Altenburg heads the list of political divisions with 46 suicides to 100,000 inhabitants, while the proportion in Elsass Lothringen, 11, is the lowest.</p>
      </div>
