      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1896-03-28_WidnesExaminer_art0170" n="art0170">
        <head>Eetterfs to tin elbitor.</head>
        <p>Eetterfs to tin elbitor.</p>
        <p>A CORRECTION.—" Harry Esmond" asks us to state that the last word in his letter of last week should have been " humility " and not " humanity " as printed.</p>
        <p>WHO IS RESPONSIBLE?</p>
        <p>Sir,-1 should like to call attention through your valuable journal to the miserable condition of the lane leading from "Darlington Lodge" on the Manchester Road to " Padgate Lane." I had cause to make use of this thoroughfare the other day and found that (after a spell of fine dry weather) the lane was almost impass- able being from end to end almost a perfect quagmire, and I am informed that it has been in this disgraceful condition for 12 or 1.8 months past; in fact it is evident that such must have been the case by the beaten track made by pedestrians along the adjoining field, they hav- ing evidently been forced to leave the lane and to make a track for themselves to get from one public highway to the ether. Surely it is high time this state of things wa3 rectified. Hoping these remarks may tend to such a desirable result.—l remain, yours Ice.,</p>
        <p>STROLLER.</p>
        <p>" ZAMINER " EXA.M.INED.</p>
        <p>" Wisdom is the principle thing ; therefore get wisdom : and with all thy getting get understanding." Pros'. iv. 7. Sir,—ln one sense, at least, this correspondence has been to me a disappointment. I could have wished the creed,that once was mine and now is Zaminer's—l should have liked it to have had defenders better worthy of my old belief. Not to mention the pleasure of having a foreman worthy of your steel, it is disheartening to find that so very little can be said,or has been said, in favour of the creed which once was ours.</p>
        <p>We have had plenty of assertions and a plentiful lack of argument. lime dixit has been the shibboleth—in the" Layman " style—a style too puerile for criticism. In looking back over the letters of these Christian apolo- gists one is inclined to ask what is there here conducive to national convincement of the divine origin of the creed ? Vapouring and vituperation are not conducive to this end, but easier to produce.their evidence and reasoning. Again,one would think it a first need in a Christian controversialist to I,)e truthful. Zaminer seems to be fond of tabulations ; I here present him with him with a pair. ZAMINER SAYS,-I. It is not true t!.3t. Christianity is forced down the throats of either the" Yellows" or the" Browns " by bayonets or gunboats. 11. Chinese opposition to Christian missions does not proceed from any question of religion. 111. Fine maxims merely are not sufficient to meet the world's needs, or work the regeneration of any people. IV. The present condition of civilization among the leading nations upon earth is due to the divine character and teaching of Christ. THE FACT It is only by the "forco" of the gun- boats and bayonets that the missionary is able to do V2is.</p>
        <p>11. No one said it did ! But Chinese opposition im- plies English force. No force, no missionaries. 111. No one said they were; but stolen maxims do not prove the divine origin of a creed. IV. Another assertion ! and one badly in want of that evidence which history, and present facts do not supply. After this brilliant specimen of fatuous "logic ':—this self-contradictory tabulation—will Zaminer still deny that " force "is used in Chinese missiopary work? To assist his apprehension I offer him this sweet little par- able. "A certain little cheeky boy would insist, despite the repeated and openly expressed objections of some elder boys, in intruding in their games. They would have boxed his ears and sent him about h!s business.but refrained—why? The little cheeky boy's elder brother was seen in the distance shaking his fist at the rude boys who disliked the little cheeky boy playing with themi And so the little cheeky boy intruded without using force. Q.E.D. Nevertheless the British forces have been and are being used to force two pernicious drugs upon the Chinese—opium and Christianity. And it is noteworthy that it is the cultivated section of Chinese society who object to the introduction of both drugs,and the vendors of both "get along well enough with the vil- lagers." Have we not said it before—that it always is the the ignorant who accept and the learned who reject ;the old tale of Palestine peasants and Greek philosophy? And the reason is plain. A very little knowledge of the science of religion would render it quite impossible for a man to acc3pt that as a revealed religion which bore upon it so many traces of growth—its earthly origin. Its borrowings from the pagans is, alone, a sufficient evidence of this. And the " best bit" of Christianity, which Zaminer challenges me to produce, is among these, the rest all " leather and prunella, as dry as the remainder biscuit after a voyage." The dogma of bodily resurrection presents insuperable difficulties to the instructed of to-day, but, to the uninformed believers of old,none whatever. eind,now,the two chief buttresses of the faith are sentinient and self-interest. These,separately or combined,are the main stays of the creed which even Christians themselves are becoming ashamed of as is evinced by the way they contract themselves out of its peculiar provisions. The commercial instincts of the unctious Christian make him regard the "the needle and camel" doctrine as good to preach, but foolish in practice ; which is by no means the only proof of his being wiser than his creed. But my dealings with Mr. Facing-both-ways has left me no space to examine Zamines iv.th section, or History, seen through the dis- torting and chromatic Christian spectacles.—Yours truly.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1896-03-28_WidnesExaminer_art0171" n="art0171">
        <head>JOHN PEERS.</head>
        <p>JOHN PEERS.</p>
        <p>"HARRY ESSIOND" TO THE RESCUE</p>
        <p>'0 dear brother, aid me quickly ; let .3 both at least restrain the force of this daring man,"—Art OLD EPIC. Sir,—l am afrail that "Harry Esmond's "courtesy is likely to be yet more " embarrassed " by his interference in this discussion. I imagine that your readers will de- cide very promptly to whom is due the charge of "gross ignorance or wilful fraul." My courteous critic might at least have reflected whether it was likely that a litteratemr such as Matthew Arnold, could be in any sense suspected of "ignorance or fraud," whose lines I quoted in which he describes the state and condition of the Roman world at large as a human "hell." Quite unconcerned as to my critic's feelings in the matter, I repeat it all but in more detail and larger proof than I could have imagined was necessary for even the merest tyro in Roman history. He might have spared his re- ference to Gibbon, for the passage has no bearing what- ever upon the subject at issue, namely, the moral and spiritual condition of the world at the advent of Christ. Improvement in arts, splendour of cities, the beautiful face of the country cultivated and adorned,—laws and science—what on earth had all this to de with the other side of the picture ?—the great body of the upper classes wallowing in the filth of beasts, and gloating in the cruelty of fiends; while the mass of the lower people equally sunk in degraded im- moralities and all manner of vice and ungodliness find life in the end to be a burden of sadness and des- pair. If my critic had t2en better acquainted even with Gib- bon than he appears to be, he might have learned from him that "the golden age of Trajan and the Antonines had been preceded by an are of iron. It is almost super- fluous to enumerate the unworthy successors of Augus- tus. Their unparalleled vices and the splendid theatre on which they were acted have saved them from oblivion. During four score years (excepting only the short and doubtful respite of Vespasian's reign) Rome groaned beneath an unremitting tyranny which exterminated the ancient families of the republic and was fatal to almost every virtue and every talent that arose in that unhappy period" (vol. I. 73-4). The period here referred to was the last 80 years of the first centuryor at least to the accession of Nerve A.D 96.</p>
        <p>Referring to the .external splendour of the cities, "their great works," says Dean Howson, " may be safely taken as emblems of the magnitude, strength, grandeur, and solidity of the Empire ; but they are em- blems no less of the tyranny and cruelty which had pre- sided at its formation, and of the general suffering which pervaded it, whilst much misery followed in the train of Rome's advancing greatness. The Empire was only the order of external government with a chaos of both opinions and morals. The writings of Tacitus and Juvenal remain to attest the corruption which festered in all ranks alike,in the Senate and the family; the tem- per of the times was essentially both cruel and profane. and the human race were groaning for the be ter peace of " a kingdom not of this world." (Vol. I. 12-14). The greatest of Roman historians, Livy, who was a contemporary in the reign of Tiberias declares in the preface to his history, " Our nation has now reached a pitch of iniquity at which it can bear neither its vices nor yet the remedies for them;" and, to waive further references to authorities living at the time, the poet Martial confirms every line of St. Paul's awful picture of human depravity as recorded in the first chapter of his epistle to the Romans. I may now deal with "Harry Esmond's " handling of Archdeacon Farrar's authority. He gives us a quota- tion from "Seekers after God " which ,like the one from Gibbon.has no relevancy to the subject of my contention, namely, the state and condition of the world at the ao- vent of Christ. My point has nothing to do with" the high moral and intellectual standards a few great men attained to in that wretched and debased Roman age. Yet this only is the subject of his quotation—taken be it i observed from the preface of the book! May I ask" Harry Esmond" if he got no further in the book than its pre- face P If he did not then be is "grossly ignorant "of the state of Roman Society as described in its third chap- ter; if he did, then he is guilty of a" wilful fraud" for pretending that Archdeacon Farrar supports his views as opposed to mine. Now what does Farrar tell us in this third chapter. "The darkness is deepest just before the , dawn,and never did a grosser darknessor a thicker mist of moral pestilence brood over the surface of Pagan , Society than at the period when :he "Sun of Righteous- ness arose with healing in His wings ;" " never probably was there any age or any place where the worst forms of wickedness were practised with a more unblushing effrontery than in the city of Rome under the government of the Clegars. It is sure!y a lesson of deep moral significance that juit as they became moat polished in their luxury they</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1896-03-28_WidnesExaminer_art0172" n="art0172">
        <gap reason="ocrQuality" quantity="1" unit="words">
          <desc>skipped/gap: fewer than 20 non-control characters after cleanup</desc>
        </gap>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1896-03-28_WidnesExaminer_art0173" n="art0173">
        <head>EXAMINER SATURDAY. MARCH 28. 196.</head>
        <p>THE EXAMINER SATURDAY. MARCH 28. 196.</p>
        <p>became most vile in their manner of life " (p. 37). , Horace and Juvenal are summoned to give this evidence, ' and Seneca is made to bring up the rear of witnesses to the unblushing and unutterable degradation of the period in these words," All things are full of iniquity and vice, mpre crime is committed than can be remedied by restraint, daily the passion for sin is greater' the shame in committing it is less. Wickedness is no longer com- mitted in secret ; it flaunts before our eyes, and has been sent forth so openly into public sight, and has prevailed so completely in the breast of all, that innocence is not rare but non-existent" (p. 48, 9). I have only time left to refer to a statement in the latter half of " Harry Esmond's " letter. "It is only too true," he says, " that instead of the Roman Empire being rescued from utter corruption and ruin by the application of the purifying salt of Christianity its cor- ruption and ruin date from the time that Christianity began to obtain the ascendancy in the Empire. Then it was that ignorance, brutality, and fierce intolerance,and every vice that dominates the human mind raged unre- t trained throughout the land " ! Now there is a jumble of ideas in this precious utterance rather difficult to unravel. That the " corruption " of the Empire was due to " the porifying salt "el the gospel of Christ is as foul a calumny as ever issued from the mouth of the " father of lies." That the " ruin" of the infamous system upon which that old pagan Empire was built up and administered may not only be truly attributed to that source, but is a matter of the deepest gratitude to Almighty God. It may or may not be worth my while to follow "Harry Esmond "through all his rambling allusions to the perversion of Christianity—on the part of wicked men and devils ; it must suffice for the present to refer him and your readers to the last chap`er of " Seekers after God" for a truthful representation of what the salt of Christianity has effected, and what it can do, in contrast to the poor feeble results which have ever fol- lowed the highest efforts of enlightened paganism.— Yours truly, Z</p>
        <p>20th Marcia, 1896. This correspondence is now closed—En-Ex.</p>
        <p>LOCAL PARISH COUNCIL ELECTIONS.</p>
        <p>To-day (Saturday) elections for parish councillors in the undermentioned places will take place. The polling booths will open at twelve noon, and ccntinue open until eight p.m. The votes will then be counted, and the result declared the same evening. The candidates names are given in the order they will appear on the ballot paper. STOCKTON EIBATII :—Polling Station, St. Thomas's schools. The following are the candidates for the seven vacant seats :—Messrs. A. Broady, William Coppock, Charles William Davenport, Thomas Hampson, Ashton Hayes, John Hedgcock, Frederick Pearson, John Pem- berton, James Warburton, and James Whitley. ANTROBUS :—Polling Station, National schools. Coun- cillors required, eight. Candidates nominated :—Joseph Bolshaw, Henry Booth, James Blackshaw Bradbury, John Burgeis, Thomas Burgess, William Burgess, John Arthur Edwards, Peter Frith, Mark Plumb, Isaac Wil- son.</p>
        <p>WESTON :—Polling Station, Church schoolroom. There are eight vacancies for which the following have been nominated :—John Ball, Daniel Basnett, William Dun- babin, James Ford,sen.,Thomas Griffiths, Arthur George Guest, Thomas Toward Sill,David Somerville, John Ellis Sweetman, James Tudor, Alfred Maitland Wood. HELSBY :—Polling Station, National Schools. The fol- lowing are the candidates for the eleven seats on the Council : William Barlow, Thomas Brandroth, Arthur John Briant, John Thomas Collier, William Davies, Thomas Ellams, William Guest. Samuel Moss, Thomas Ncden, Joseph Potts, George Crossland Taylor, James Taylor, Abraham White, Frrthrick Wright ALYANLEY : Polling Station, National Schools. Seven councillors required. Candidates: John Antrobus, Thomas Booth, Robert Dilworth, John Dugdale, William Dugdale, Arthur Greenway. Joseph Littlemore, William Henry Lowe, Samukl Henry Noden, Thomas Wright.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1896-03-28_WidnesExaminer_art0174" n="art0174">
        <head>FOOTBALL.</head>
        <p>FOOTBALL.</p>
        <p>(Continued from page 6.)</p>
        <p>ASSOCIATION.</p>
        <p>WARRINGTON PRESS V. POLICE--Teams composed of members of the Warrington Press and the Police Force met at Dalton Bank on Wed nesday afternoon to engage in a game of Asssociation football. The reporters were assioted by R. Connor, H. Evans, and W. Creighton of the Warrington A.P.C.. and almost all the papers which have connection with Warrington had representatives on the team. The police won the toss and played down hill, and during the first quarter of an hour—the reporters being a man short—they had pretty much their own way, and scored three goals in quick succes- sion. Immediately after the third goal, however, the missing man of the Press team arrived, and play then became more equal. Shortly before half time C.Gill beat Pennington by a neat shot after a fine dribble along the field by Connor, and the interval arrived with the score —Police, three g34ls ; Press, one goal. After changing ends, a drizzling rain commenced to fall, and rendered play very uncomfortable. The Press asserted their superiority during the greater portion of this half, and Connor put on three goals,thus giving the Press the leads The Police again played up, and narrowly escaped scor- ing on thre e different occasions, but about two minute. off time they rushed a goal through, and thus the game ended in a draw of four goals each.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1896-03-28_WidnesExaminer_art0175" n="art0175">
        <head>-NEWTON DISTRICT COUNCIL ELEC</head>
        <p>-NEWTON DISTRICT COUNCIL ELEC</p>
        <p>TIONS.</p>
        <p>VIADUCT WARD</p>
        <p>A meeting in support of the candidature of Mr. P. MacMahon for the Viaduct Ward was held in the Town Earlestown, on Thursday evening. Mr. J. W. Emmett, J.P., presided, and he was supported on the platform by Messrs. T. Ormand,,C. W. Emmett, J. H. Profit, W. Clare, J. Galligan, and others. The Chairman said the meeting was called for two objects; the first was in order to give Mr. MacMahon an opportunity of giving an account of his stewardship for the last 12 months, and the other to persuade all the voters in the Viaduct Ward to give him their votes, so that he might be put at the head of the poll by a very large majority. They would remember when the elec- tion took place about 15 or 16 months ago that the new members of the Council promised to do a very great many things for the township.lf anybody could tell him what the Council had done for the township during the last 12 months, he should be very much obliged to them. He did not know that they had done anything FO far as benefiting the neighbourhood in any way, with the ex- ception of laying some pipes, and putting some gas round the Common. That was a very good thing,so far as the Common was concerned. There was another thing they intended to do, and they made a great parade of it. They were going to pave all the streets, and give every tenant a good road to his house. He had seen no good roads since, but he did not know how soon they would come. Another thing, they were going to give them water for almost nothing. Perhaps Mr. MacMahon would be able to say something abeut the water scheme. He had been on the special committee for about six months, but what they had done ? He (Mr. Emmett) could not tell them, and none of the outside members of the Council could tell them what had been done with regard to the water scheme. Whatever was done he advised them not to be content with having water filtered. Have it direct out of the earth, because if it came through a course of filtration they would not get as good water as they were getting now. He did not say that because he was connected with the London and North-Western Railway Company, for he should be as glad as anybody to get water as good as it was now. In Mr. smith's address for the Crow-lane ward, he says, "although some useful work had been done, much more would have been accomplished, had not the persistent obstruction of a small minority of the members of the Council, in spite of Mr. Borron's admirable conduct in the chair, rendered the work of the Council well nigh impossible," which he thought showed how short-sighted Mr. Smith was. They all knew that. Mr.Borron and Mr. Smith had a majori y on the Cotilicil,and if they wished to carry anything they coeld do so, and to say that the minority had retarded business was simply saying what was not correct. As regarded Mr. Borron's admirable conduct in the chair,he would tell them what a friend of Mr. Borron told him. This friend said " What do you think about Mr. Borron's chairmanship." He (Mr. Emmett) said he would rather not say anything about it. " You are a friend of Mr. Borron and I would like to hear your view of it." He replied " Well if I must give you my candid opinion about Mr. Borron' conduct in the chair, he was just about as much fit to be Chairman of the District Council, as I am fit to go into St. Paul's Cathedral to preach a sermon to the Royal family." (Laughter.) He (Mr. Emmett) had nothing to say about Mr. Borron but he could endorse what his friend had said. (Laughter.) Thsy had a Council meet- ing that day, and what had been done was for a section of the Council to praise the Chairmati,and the Chairman to praise the section. (Hear, hear.) Mr.McMahon said that at the last election it was prom- ised to have the streets better lighted. They had tried very hard to get one part of the town lighted, and that was the Common-road.and when it was lightedithe lamps were tut on the wrong side of the road as far away as p,ssible from the houses. He was a member of the special Water Committee, but although several meet- ings had been held, he was not for some time summoned to attend to any of the meetings. If that was not hole and corner business he did not know what was. What they were doing at present he was not at liberty to ex- plain,but he thought they were going from bad to worse. A lot of correspondence had been carried on but he could net say by whose authority the correspondence had been conducted. He did not think they could get better water than they were getting from the London and North- Western Co., and until they could get that, with abet- ter supply, he could not see any use of throwing away what they were already having. He referred ts the overseer's agitation and street making. Mr. .1. Smith moved that that meeting of electors of Viaduct ward tendered to Mr. MacMahon their thanks for his services on the Council, and begged to move that he was a lit and proper person to represent them on the Council, and that they pledge themselves to use every legitimate means to return him by a triumphant majority next Monday. (Hoar, hear.) Mr. E. Cronin seconded, and Mr. W. Clare and Mr. Proet spoke in support. Mr. T. Ormand also supported the motion, and said he was glad to belong to the minority in the Council, be- cause the majority admitted that the minority could beat them. How waa it that if they had a majority,the minority were the masters of the situation. Were they fit to conduct the affairs of the township any longer ? if so then he did not understand the meaning of minority and majority. He referred to the manner in which the special Water Committee conducted the water question, which he severely criticised. Mr. MacMahon was not being brought out by any political section, and was not supported by any creed or social club,and was essentially a workingman's representative. (Applause.) The resolution was put and carried unanimously. Mr. MacMahon thanked them for their support, and I proposed a vote of thanks to the Chairman. Mr. J. Galligan seconded. The Chairman said he had no ambition to fill the chair of the District Council, and if he was asked to-morrow he should certainly reject it for more than one reason. He did think when the District Council was first elected, that he might have the good fortune to be elected chair- man for one year, seeing that he had been chairman for a number of years prior to that time, but of course the Council was differently formed, and there was a differ- enee of opinion. He was not the right man, and if an angel from heaven had come down and been elected to the Council, that angel would not have been made chair- Inan.Certain gentlemen had made up their minds whom to elect.whether fit or not. (Laughter and applause.)</p>
        <p>Seventy-two races inhq.bit-the wnrH and use 3004, ffereat tosgue 8. There are about 1,000 religions.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1896-03-28_WidnesExaminer_art0176" n="art0176">
        <head>RUNCORN- 'MUSICAL SOCIEY'S CONCERT.</head>
        <p>RUNCORN- 'MUSICAL SOCIEY'S CONCERT.</p>
        <p>The members of the Runcorn Musical Society brought theh season's series of concerts to a fitting close on Thursday evening, when Mendelssohn's " Elijah" was given on a most adequate scale. It was generally ad- mitted that in venturing on the performance of this work the members had set themselves an arduous task. " Elijah "—the last and perhaps the greatest work of Mendelssohn's, now being performed before the public for the fifteenth year—stands in the front rank of modern sacred compositions. Mendelssohn here seems to have found an outlet for the religious feelings with which he was so strongly imbued. Thursday evenine was the first occasion on which the work has been pro- duced before a Runcorn audience, and the local Musical Society received much-appreciated assistance from members of the Warrington and Widr es societies. Numerous rehearsals have been held,at which Mr.Cross- ley has displayed a commendable amount of persever- ance and tact, and the result was seen in the excellent performance. The leading vocalists were Miss Ada Patterson (ioprano), Miss Alice Bertenshaw (contralto), Mr.Thurgate Simpson (tenor),and Mr.Bantock Pierpoint (bass). The principal share of the solo work of the " Elijah" devolves largely on the titular part,and in this Mr. Bantock Pierpoint proved himself equal to the exi- gencies of the occasion. Additional interest was centred in him owing to the fact that he is a native of Runcorn, and it is something to be proud of that Runcorn has ' been able to supply the musical world with such a first- class artiste. His singing was particularly fine, and was much appreciated. He was seen at his best in the dramatic , dialogue between " Elijah " and the people who call upon Baal to show by his power that he is God, and after repeated and unsuccessful petitions, Elijah scorn- fully derides them. This enthralling episode receives impressive force by the touch of Mendelssohn's genius. The powers of both the principal soloist and the chorus are taxed to the uttermost until the climax is reached in that majestic chorus "Thanks be to God." Toe other leading artistes sang exceedingly well, Miss Bertenshaw being very successful in the singing in her rich contralto voice, "0 rest in the Lord." Of the concerted music, special mention must be made of the double quartette " For He shall give His angels," in which the leading artistes were Miss Jones, Miss Simk n (Runcorn) and Messrs. Howard and Eccles (Warrington) took part. In the quartette "Cast thy burden upon the Lord," Mr. Eccles, of War- rington, took the bass part with infinite credit to him- self. Two other interesting items were the quartette of female voices in " Holy, holy, holy is God the Lord," and also the quartette "Oh ! come every one that thirsteth." Greater interest,however, lay in the choruses for they are the very soul of the oratorio. A. powerful and well-balanced chorus was present, and their work was characterised by strength and robustness of attack, also that power of producing subtle gradations of tone. Mention has already been made of one really fine chorus, and another one which should be referred to, was "Behold ! God the Lord passed by !" in which the members shone. The audience was aroused to many demonstrations of approval. The oratorio makes heavy demands upon the orchestra, but it was well equipped for the performance of its important work. Their per- formance of the tuneful overture, the many beautiful movements associated with the solos, and the more onerous choruses with their muted passages and out- bursts of praise, was given with musicianly taste and skill. Mr. Frank H. Crossley conducted throughout the evening in a masterly manner. There was a large attendance at what proved to be a most successful co aced.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1896-03-28_WidnesExaminer_art0177" n="art0177">
        <head>FRODSHAM.</head>
        <p>FRODSHAM.</p>
        <p>CONFIRMATION SERVICE.—This annual service was held in the Parish Church, on Friday, when 204 per- cons were confirmed by the Right Rev. the Bishop of C lester. The Bishop was assisted in the service by the Revs.H. B. Blogg, vicar, and W. T. Diekonson, curate. After the ceremony of " laying on of hands," the Bishop delivered an address. Tea was served in the Boys' School, to which a large number sat down. In the evening the Vicar held a short service. MUSICAL SOCIRTY'S CONCRRT.—The Frodsham Musical Society gavu a concert in the Town Hall. Frodsharn, on Tuesday evening, and it was largely attended. The first part of the programme consisted of Van Bree's " St. Cecilia's Day." The work provides ample scope for the chorus and orchestra to display their powers, and it is satisfactory to state that the performers acquitted them- selves in a most suitable manner. Madame Laura Ha- worth was the principal vocalist, and her soprano solos were greatly appreciated. Mr. R.Birkett Musgrove was the conductor. and Miss Lewis the pianist. The second part of the programme was of a miscellaneous character. It opened with a fine chorus entitled "The Carty). vale," which had been arranged for the occasion by Mr. Musgrove, and did him infinite credit. Mr. Ben Roberts, of Liverpool, treated the audience to an effective rendering of " The Star of Bethlehem." He also sang "The Anchor's Weighed," for which he received an encore. Miss Laura Graham proved herseli a capable violinist, and received an encore. MaPlame Laura Haworth contributed three songs entitled " Sing sweet bird," " I cannot can I," and " A Dream of Para- dise." She also sang in a duet with Mr. Roberts. An instrumental trio was contributed by Mr. Pope (flute), Mr. Simkia ('cello), and Mr. Musgrove (piano).</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1896-03-28_WidnesExaminer_art0178" n="art0178">
        <head>HALTON.</head>
        <p>HALTON.</p>
        <p>SIIG4IESTED DISTRICT NaRSH.—A meeting was held at Halton on Monday for the purpose of considering the advisability of appointing a district nurse. A number of schemes were brought forward and considered. The need of a nurse in the district was much felt,and it was considered that if one could be appointed on similar lines to the district nurse at Runcorn it would be a distinct advantage. It was ultimately agreed that the matter should bc brought before the notice of the various clubs and benefit societies of the village, and that they should be askedto make suggestions. These will be considered at a future meeting.</p>
        <p>INDEPENDENT LABOUR PARTY, EARLESTOWN BRANCH.</p>
        <p>Under the auspices of the above a lecture was given in the Co-operative Hall, Earlestowu, on Monday evening, by the Rev. Conrad Noel, a Church of England minister, on "True Religion and Unde- filed.' The chair was occupied by Mr. H. Swans- brick, and there was a very fair audience. The Lecturer dealt with his subject from a purely socialistic standpointeasserting that the true idea of religion was to give and not receive, and to sacri- fice ourselves. If they had the spirit of grab or greed, that was the spirit of the devil and not true religion. They talked of Salvation which they said meant getting let off punishment in the next world. Of course Christ taught that it meant getting rid of wickedness in this world. He claimed that the socialists were Christians and not the people who went to chapel and taught, every man for himself and God for us all. EARLESTOWN WESLEYAN HOME AN FOREIGN M IrSIONARY SOCIETY.</p>
        <p>On Sunday afternoon in the Wesleyan Chapel, Eariestown, prizes were distributed to the juvenile collectors connected with the above society. The Rev. J. Bartley presided over the proceedings, and Mr. P. Parr, on behalf of the secretary (Mrs. War- burton), gave a report on the working of 1895, re- marking that there were 78 collectors,and together they had raised the sum of £7O 133. 101,which was an advance over 1894 of £lO. The girls had collected £4l us. id., and the boys £29 23. 3d. Prizes con- sisting of books were then distributed to each col- lector, the allowance being at the rate of one shilling for every sovereign collected. During the afternoon sacred solos were sung by Miss O.Roberts, Miss L. Barr, Miss M. Barr, and Miss E. Collinge.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1896-03-28_WidnesExaminer_art0179" n="art0179">
        <p>_y</p>
        <p>NOTES ON GAMES AND c ATHLETICS. 1</p>
        <p>RUGBY FOOTBALL.</p>
        <p>Warrington did not cover themselves with glory on Saturday in their match with Batley. After the show they made against the powerful Manningham team the previous week, it was thought that they would easily account for Batley who have made but a poor show in the Northern League. As far as the players went War. rington had a big advantage, but they made but poor use of the openings which offered themselves, and, the result was that until about ten minutes from the close, they were actually in a minority of one point. The try which gave them the victory was however a splendid piece of play,and to some extent atoned for the previous lack of combination.</p>
        <p>The Warrington team played with plenty of dash dur- ing the game, hut lack of finesse was evident through- out. The Batley forwards were adepts at the art of heeling out,and it was to this more than anything else that they owed their let off from a heavy defeat. They had a very good full back in Naylor,and three fine backs in the two Goodalls and Fitzgerald, and altogether, they did not give a very unpleasing impression to the War- rington spectators, and it is evident that notwithstand- ing their present humble position in the league lately they have very considerable ability.</p>
        <p>Amongst the Warrington forwards there was some very good work put !n at various times by almost the whole of them, but the most prominent were Taylor, Saunders, Swift, and Fairhurst. At half back Bate and Higginbottom played a good game,•although the latter plays a little too much off-side. Barber gave one of his best exhibitions and it is a pity that he cannot go to Brighouse to-day,as his absence will considerably weaken the team, although O'Brien is an excellent substitute.</p>
        <p>Cross was also in fine form, but Lawless and Carey had repeated hard luck in not scoring. .Heesom was very safe in his tackling but rather weak in his kicking.</p>
        <p>To-day, Brighouse Rangers are met at Brighouse, and it is to be hoped that Warrington do not catch them in the same form that they were when they came to War- rington. They were at their best then, but they seem to have fallen a lot since hen and perhaps there is a chanc3 for Warrington this time.</p>
        <p>The following is the team selected to go to Yorkshire to-day : Ileesom, full back ; Carey, Cross, O'Brien, and Lawless, threequarters ; B tte and Higginbotcom, half- backs ; Saunders, Andrews, Savage, Berry, Scholze,Sud- low, Fairhurst and Dakin, forwards.</p>
        <p>The Widnes team surprised themselves and everybody else on Saturday by beating Hunslet by 22 points to 13. Such proFfic scoring as this is rarely witnessed, and it is pretty evident that the defence of each team was very poor, and though Widnes scored the most points, they were well scored against. Had Hunslet their full de- fence on, it is very probable that they would not have scored half their 22 points. However they are to be con- gratulated upon having scored the highest number of Points against Hunslet this season, and they also share with Warrington, the credit of being the only two teams who have beaten Hunslet twice this season.</p>
        <p>No other match was a very great surprise on Saturday unless it was the heavy beating the Hull team received at the hands of Tyldesley. Hull have been doing so well all along that a defeat of six tries to nil is astonishing. Tvldesley,were evidently" off" at goal kicking in the match. _</p>
        <p>The Runcorn team just managed to take two points Wit of BradfOrd on Saturday. It was an interesting and evenly contested game throughout, and up to within twenty minutes from the doge, things looked ominous for Runcorn. Towards the close,the homesters wakened up, and snatched a notable victory. The two tries scored by the visitors were chiefly owing to the blunders of Houghton, the full back, who was closely off colour during the match. On Saturday, the burly Wiganers are to visit the Irwell-lane enclosure when a keenly con- tested game may be anticipated. The match is a very important one as it will affect :he position of the two clubs in the Lancashire competition. Runcorn occupy a very good position, and Wigan are close upon their heelq. The loss of a single match to either club affects their chances of gaining the foremost place. The Northern Union is as follows -</p>
