        <p>EARLESTOWN AND DISTRICT MEDICAL</p>
        <p>AID SOCIETY.</p>
        <p>The annual meeting of the above Society was held in the Co-operative Hall, Earlestown, on Tuesday evening. Mr. C. B. F. Borron presided, and he was supported on the platform by the Revs. J. Carson, T. R. Matthews, E. W. W. Kaye, F. E. Miller, J. T. Camm,Dr. Mouncey, Messrs. Fe T. Evans, J. Roberts, J. E. Worsley (trea- surer), A. Peake (secretary), and Nurse Jones. The Chairman said he regretted very much to see so very few present, because he certainly considered that was a society which deserved the support of the neigh- bourhood. The business of the meeting was to hear the report and financial report of the Medical Aid Society. The meeting ought to have been held last October, but owing to the regrettable illness of their hon. sec., Mr. Peake, it had been postponed until that date. He was very glad, and he was sure they would be, to see him there that evening in good health. It was unneces- sary for him to state the objects of the Society. It had been five years in existence in Earlestown, and that was the third annual report which had been read, and it had been generally felt that it had cone a useful work. It had to a large extent done mach to ameliorate the sufferings which in large communities occurred through accidents, and he thought few individuals could wish to be more of the good Sameritan than by supporting a good Society like that. (Applause.) The Secretary then read letters of apology from the Revs. Canon Penrhyn, H. Monk, A. M. Mitchell, and E. R. Davies' Drs. Haywood, Valentine, Thompson, and Mr. 'l'. Ormond. He afterwards read the report of the Society for 1894-5, which stated that since the work was commenced in 1890 there had been 1,276 recommends given for the loan of appliances, divided as follows :—During 1891, 159 articles were lent, of which 115 were issued on hire and 44 free, the position of the borrower being such as to make this necessary ; the amount earned was £2 7s. 11d. During 1892, 240 articles were lent, 115 being issured on hire, earning £5 95., and 93 free. During 1893, 306 articles were lent, 200 being on hire, earning £5, and 106 free. During 1894, 364 articles were lent, 292 being hire and 72 free ; and from October, 1894, to October, 1895, 371 articles were lent, 299 being on hire and 72 free, £lO ss. 9d. being earned since January, 1894 ; thus showing the steady and increas- ing usefulness of this bi of the Society's work. During the year additionsl • rides, as required, have been obtained,and the stock of appliances kept in good repair and condition. The committee gratefully acknow- ledge the gift of a water-bag from Mrs. Sines, Burton- wood,and two bed-pans from Messrs. Stirling and Co., per Mr. William Kirk. Nurse Jones has visited from October, 1894,t0 October 1895, 111 patients,to whom she has made 2,086 visits. Toe cases are very various in their kind, as the following list will show :—Confine- meats 19, massage 8, accidents 10,rheumatism 7, opera- tions 7, inflam-nation 18, influenza 11, ulcerated legs 3, dropsy 3, mental 2,hemorrhage 2, brain fever I,paralysis 3, senile decay 3, unclassified 14; and has received in donations to the society for her services £7 2s. 21. The above list fully justified the :anticipation of the society that the services of a trained nurse were really required in the district,and emphasized the pity it would be if her services had to be dispensed with for want of funds. Last year a precedent was established by having collec- tions made in the various works of the district in aid of the objects of the Society, and the majority responded more or less liberally. This year a similiar endeavour is being made, and collections will be made in most of the works as soon as convenient days can be arranged, when it is hoped that the total amount of £22 17s. 7d. which was raised in this way last year will be consider- ably exceeded. Various changes on the committee had been brought about, and the members together , with the whole neighbourhoed, deeply deplored the loss of Colonel McCorqucdale, not only on the ground that they had lost one who heartilysyinpathised with them in their work, and who was a personal friend to everyone in dis- tress or real need of help, but also as having lost their most liberal subscriber. The Committee regretted the removal to other spheres of labour of the Revs. T. Wynne Jones and J. Cottam, both of whom took an active part at many of their meetings. They were pleased to wel- come the Revs. J. Bartley and F. E. Millsr amongst them and hoped to receive much valuable help from their de- liberations. In the annual report of 1893 it was men- tioned that as a compliment to the value of the work done by this society, a sirniliar one had been formed for the parishes of Golborne and Lowton. This society had just issued their report for 1895, from which it seemed they have already obtained the services of a ' trained nurse who is doing good work, and this com- mittee would point out the very generous response which has been made to the appeal there for annual subscrintions,as they were able to show a list amounting to ever £5O per annum. The committee therefore hoped that the residents of Earlestown and district would not allow the child to surpass the parent in the firmness of its financial basis. The committee would direct the attention to the very unfavourable balance sheet for this year, and do not see how the usefulness of the society can be maintained with the extra expenditure entailed by retaining the services of a nurse without some regu- lar income, and would, therefore, most earnestly appeal for annual subscriptions for that purpose. Subscrip- tions and donations will be thlnkfully received by the Bankers, Messrs. Parr and Co.. Warrington, or any of the branches; or by the Hon. Treasurer. J. E. Worsley, , Winwick ; Hon. Secretary, A. Peake, Eq., Queen-st., Earlestown ; and the Nurse at the Depot, 21, Queen-st., Earlestown.</p>
        <p>The financial statement showed the income, including a balance of £5B 9s. 4d., to be £164 14s. 81, and the expenditure £147 10s., leaving a balance in tank of £l7 4s. Bd.</p>
        <p>'The report and balance sheet were adopted. The Rev. F. E. Miller said he thought the report just read spoke for itself. They had simply to exercise their imagination a little, and try to get at the back of the figures laid before them to get at the fact that the society was doing a work second to none. If they realised what sickness meant, they should at once realise that the society had a claim upon their sympathy and liberality. He was inclined to think they had reason to congratu- late .-hemselves upon the possession of such a society in their midst, and he believed the work was carried on, on the whole, in the most efficient and satisfactory way possible. (Hear, hear.) He had heard testimonies as to the work done by Nurse Jones, not only from the patients, but from the modical men, who had the cases under the'r charge, and they had reason to feel the ut- most confidence in the nurse. So far as the society was concerned, he thought it might fairly ask for the efforts and support of the people of Eariestown. The society was rich indeed in having such a person as Mr. Peake as secretary, and in having such a nurse as Nurse Jones, and they had every reason to believe the money con- tributed would be expended in the most careful and judicious manner. (Applause.) Mr. J. Roberts said he had every admiration for the Surgical Aid Society, and he thought that as the inhabi- tants reatlised the value of it, the money would come. The Rev. J. T. Carnm also spoke in laudatory terms of the society. The Rev. J. Carson said he did not know when he had heard such a good speech as that given by Nurse Jones. He was sure her exam. le would be the means of stimu- lating borne nursing. The medical men had to depend a good deal upon the nurse, and he made an urgent appeal for subscriptions in aid of the society. Mr. J. E. Worsley (Warrington), treasurer, could not understand how such an excellent society as that did not receive a far larger measure of support than it had hitherto received. The good work the society did was not sufficiently known, or he felt sure the financial account would be in a better position than it was to-day. As to the usefulness af the society he did not think ic possible to exaggerate its wcrk. There had been some- thing said about the christian aspect of the society, and he would point out that the nursing of the sick was essentially a mission of mercy. If there ever was a system outside of religion which could be claimed as dis- tinctly Christian, it would surely be the attendance on the sick and amelioration of human pain. (Hear, hear.)</p>
        <p>Dr.Mouncey thought they had excellent reasons why the Society should not be left in the lurch se far as its pecuniary position was concerned. use year they flourished,and the next year they were nearly bankrupt, and what they wanted was for ladies and gentlemen to give them a fixed annual subscription. They had a smaller list of annual subscribers than they had at Golborne, although this district had a better wage-earning popelation. The Rev.E. W. W. Kaye suggested that the Secretary asked the works to elect two men to act upon the com- mittee according to the rules of the Society. He would suggest that the Loudon and North-Western Company elect two men, and the Vulcan Foundry 'two, and that the bottle works elect one man, and the printing works, Newton, one man.</p>
        <p>Mr. D. Williams suggested that those who bad col- lected in the works funds in aid of the Society should in virtue of that join the comnaittee. Mr. Peake was glad this matter had been brought for- ward, as it did not seem to be fully appreciated by the works, and he would suggest that at the next meeting of the committee he should propose as an amendment to the rule that the c(illectors be asked to become members of the cammittee. He would like to subscribe to the In- firmary and Eye Hospital, so that they could give re- commends to people in need of them. If they could do that they could amplify their work, and of course help so many more. (Applause.) Votes of thanks to the Chairman,Secretary, and Trea- surer terminated the proceedings.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1896-03-28_WidnesExaminer_art0180" n="art0180">
        <head>EARLESTOWN I.ND NEWTON.</head>
        <p>EARLESTOWN I.ND NEWTON.</p>
        <p>DRUNKENNESS.</p>
        <p>At the Earlestown police station on Monday before Mr. J. W. Emmett, John O'Brien of Crow- lane, was charged with being drunk in Market-st. on Saturday night and fined 2s. 6d. and costs. Thomas Kennedy of Grafton-st.,and Robert Spillane were each mulcted in a like penalty for a similar offence. EARLESTOWN ORCHESTRAL SOCIETY.</p>
        <p>On Saturday evening the above society gave an entertainmcmt in the Co-operative Hall. A very good programme was submitted, but there was only a moderate audience. The items which were well executed included half-a-dozen selections by the band, violin solos by Mr. F. Whatmough, clarionet solo by Mr. A. Ashley, pianoforte solo by Mr. E. Harrison. euphonium solo by Mr. Jackson, a couple of songs by Mr. J. H. Harrison, and humorous items by Mr. W. 11. McDermott of Liverpool. PRESENTATION TO A COLLIERY MANAGER.</p>
        <p>On Saturday evening a number ot the officials and workmen engaged at Messrs. Evans and Co.'s No. 1 Newton Wood Pit. Haydock,assembled at the Prince of Wales hotel, Earlestown, for the purpose of pre- senting Mr. S. flutchison, late manager of the No. 1 wood pit, and his wife with some evidence of the esteem in which they were held during their con- nection with the colliery. The proceedings included an excellently served dinner, provided by Mr. R. Lythgoe, to which about 70 were present, several of the workmen being accompanied by their wives. After dinner,the cloth was removed,and Mr. Higson presided. The usual loyal toasts were proposed and duly honoured, and the presentation which con- sisted of a massive marble timepiece and a pair of bronze ornaments, with a copper tea urn for Mrs. Hutchinson was made by Mr. T. Taberner. It should be stated that Mr. Hutchinson was unable to be present, being unavoidably detained on some business at Burnley. His wife, however, was present, and received the articles on his behalf. Mr. Hutchinson had for some time been manager of the Wood pit, but had left to take up a similar position at Hindley. He and his wife had gained the esteem of the officials and workmen, who determined to express their regard in a substantial manner. Mr. Taberner in making the presentation, read a tele- gram which he had received from Mr. Hutchinson expressing his regret at being unable to attend. He then referred to the presentation to Mr. Hutchinson of the magnificent clock and bronzes which they saw before him, and a most useful tea urn to his good wife. (Hear, hear.) He felt sure he echoed the sentiments of all present when he said that they wished Mr. and Mrs. Hutchinson long life and pros- perity, and that they might be spared many years to look upon the evidences of goed will from those with whom they were at one time associated.. (Applause.) Mrs. Hutchinson suitably acknow- ledged the gift. The evening was afterwards spent in a convivial manner,songs being given by Messrs. G. Stokes, Baron, Fitzpatrick, Mrs. Sherlock and Mrs. Grant.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1896-03-28_WidnesExaminer_art0181" n="art0181">
        <head>TOWNSHIP MEETING AT NEWTON.</head>
        <p>TOWNSHIP MEETING AT NEWTON.</p>
        <p>The annual meeting of ratepayers of Newton in Maker&amp;ld was held in the Town Hall, Newton on Thursday evening. Mr. J. Sankey presided, and there were present Messrs. Barrett, Speakman, and Houghton (assistant overo-eer). The minutes of the past meeting, and the account q for the year were read and adopted. Messrs. D. Williams and D. For- rester were re-elected burleymen and common lookers. On the motion of Mr. Sankey, seconded by Mr. Speakman, a voluntary rate of a halfpenny in the was laid, for incidental expenses. Messrs. Barrett and Fairclough were re-elected auditors.</p>
      </div>
