        <p>Eglitial 3otires. RUNCORN URBAN DISTRICT COUNCIL ELECTION. TO THE ELECTORS OF CENTRAL WARD. LADIES AND GENTLEMEN,— Kindly accept my most sincere thanks for the honour you have conferred upon me in renewing confidence in me as your representative, and electing rag without opposition. My efforts in the past have been directed to secure economy, with efficiency. This shall be my purpose in future. Yours sincerely, JOHN BAPTIST GASTALDI, The Presbytery, Irwell-lane.</p>
        <p>p UNCORN URBAN COUNCIL ELECTION. Jt TO THE ELECTORS OF BRIDGEWATER WARD. LADIES AND GIIITLEMEN,— I respectfully ask you to vote for me on Saturday nest, the poll will be open from 12 to 8 p.m., at the National Schools. As a native of Runcorn and life-long resident, my constant aim will be, if elected, to do my utmost for its progress and prosperity. Yours truly, WILLIAM HANDLE!.</p>
        <p>RUNCORn URBAN DISTRICT COUNCIL. BRIDGRWATHR WARD. LADIES GENTLEMEN,— Having been again nominaiird for this ward, I willingly place my services at your disposal. The health of the town has been my especial care during my term of office, and I claim to bare been the means of making the Infectious Diseases Hospital of greater useful- ness than ever before. The real work of the Council is done in committee, each committee being composed of the whole of the members, and in order that the public may be aware of how their business is conducted, I shall advocate that in future the committee meetings be open to the newspaper reporters. Truly yours, MURDOCH McKENZIE.</p>
        <p>TO THE ELECTORS OF STOCKTON HiIATH WARD. L&amp;rnED AND GENTLNMEN I beg to submit myself and seek re-election for this Ward on the Appleton Parish Council. If elected the interests and requirements of the Ward shall have my faithful service and support. Your obedient servant, ASHTON HAYES. Aldersry Villas, March 1898.</p>
        <p>A PPLETON PARISH COUNCIL, STOCKTON HEATH WARD. TO THE PAROCHIAL ELECTORS. LADIES AND GENTLEMEN,— Fifteen months ago, I was elected at your hands to be one of your representatives on the above Council, A fresh Election of Councillors for. the Stockton Heatn Ward will shortly take place. I beg to ask a renewal of your confidence, and to solicit your votes. Yours Obediently, THOMAS lIAMPSON. Lilac Villas, 19th March, 1896.</p>
        <p>fro THE ELECTORS OF THE STOCKTON 1 HEATH WARD OF TRH APPLETON PARISH COUNC IL. LADIES AND GENTLEMEN, - My term of office as one of your representatives on the above Council will shortly expire, and at the request of a large number of ratepayers I again place my services at your disposal. The views expressed by me at the last election are un- changed, and I hope that the attention I have given to the duties attached to the office is sufficient justification for ap- pealing to you for a renewal of your confidence. Your Obedient Servant, JOHN HEDGCOCK. "The Grange," Stockton Heath. 24 March, 1896.</p>
        <p>pARISII COUNCIL OF API?Tvti:TON.—"2O..., ELECTORS OF THE IiTOCK If' 11/3/411 Lanai AND The period of office, for which In December, elected me as one of your seven representatives on the Connell, has now expired. In thanking you for your past confidence " and favours, I beg again to offer yau my services and to solicit your sup- port. The work e the past 15 months, during which the new law of Parochial Government was in operation, has been some- what arduous. )3y now, however, the procedure and practice have been got into working order. I submit it will be wise to return to office some, at least,of those who have before held the reins, and who by previous services and experience, and by acquaintance with the needs Of the Board, have qualified themselves for the position. On this ground (amongst o hers) having for sere ral years been your representative as Guardian (now District Counr,ll- - and as Parish Councillor, and having been Chairman of of the Parish Council, I confidently appeal for your support and votes, promising i• ieturn my beet and eonstant efforts and exertions on your behalf. I beg to remain, Ladies and Gentlemen.; Yours obediently, CHARLES WILLIAM DAVENPORT. Stockton Heath. 20th March, 1896.</p>
